#grid 512

// THE PALETTE IS THE DIAL, because the same simulation is a Caribbean sea and
// an indoor pool at dusk depending only on where these two colours sit. The
// defaults are the sea this was authored as, so every scene already wearing it
// renders unchanged; X333's own water, measured off its material, is #007992
// deep against a #00a3b2 shallow.
#param color DEEP_COLOR #0f87c7
#param color SHALLOW_COLOR #7ba9d4
#param float WAVE_SPEED 1.4 0.2 4
#param float DAMPING 0.955 0.8 0.999
#param float STEP_PUSH 0.3 0 1 — depth of the dent it presses

// WHERE SHE MEETS THE WATER, which is not always a foot. Six joints down each
// limb, so whichever ones are actually crossing the surface are the ones that
// ring: ankles when she walks, knees when she kneels, wrists when she trails a
// hand. The torso comes free through rzSubjectHip and costs no slot.
//
// Six of the scene's sixteen anchor slots. That table is shared by every
// installed effect, so an effect wanting hands as well should expect to be told
// the budget is spent — see EFFECT_ANCHORS.
#anchor 左足首
#anchor 右足首
#anchor 左ひざ
#anchor 右ひざ
#anchor 左手首
#anchor 右手首

// Water — a surface with a depth, that something can stand in.
//
// The motion is the grid mount: a wave equation after Evan Wallace's WebGL
// Water, whose update is three lines and is stated PER FRAME:
//
//   info.g += (average - info.r) * 2.0;   // acceleration toward the neighbours
//   info.g *= 0.995;                      // damping
//   info.r += info.g;                     // integrate
//
// This is the half that could not be a texture. A ripple has to LEAVE the foot
// that made it, spread, and reflect off the far edge seconds later — that is
// memory, and the grid is the only thing in this engine that has any. Every
// procedural surface pattern tried here was a function of position and could
// only ever look like one.
//
// A Blender anime-water graph was surveyed for the LOOK (tools/blender-port,
// 194 nodes flattened to 82) and three things in it are worth recording even
// though its layers are not in this file:
//
//   1. ANISOTROPY. Every mapping node scales one axis and not the other —
//      (0.11, 1, 1), (2.55, 1, 1), (4.22, 1, 1). Stretching the domain nine
//      times along one axis is what turns noise into FLOW.
//   2. VORONOI, not fbm, for foam cells: round and separated, which is what
//      foam is. fbm gives connected veins, which is marble.
//   3. Ramps 0.032 to 0.064 wide on a 0..1 field. That hard cut is the cel edge.
//
// They were ported and REMOVED. Their thresholds were measured on a field
// sampled in a normalised 0..1 domain and then read back in WORLD units through
// invented conversion factors, so they landed nowhere near the coverage they
// were placed at, every layer saturated, and the surface came out a carpet of
// white confetti. Doing it properly means sampling in the domain the thresholds
// were measured in, which is its own piece of work rather than a tuning pass.

// ── The pool ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WATER_Y is the surface height in world units, and it is the dial that decides
// what this scene IS. A model stands about 20 units tall, so:
//   0.5   a wet floor — ankles break the surface as she dances
//   4.0   shallow water, a ring off every step
//   10.0  waist deep, wading
//   16.0  swimming
// Anything below the surface is simply covered by it: the depth test does that
// for free, so her legs go under with no per-model work.
const WATER_Y: f32 = 4.0;
const SIM_AT: vec2f = vec2f(0.0, 0.0);
// How wide the pool is. Independent of how FAST its waves look, which is the
// non-obvious part and worth stating: a ripple's crossing time is
//
//     N / (sqrt(params.WAVE_SPEED/2) * fps)
//
// — the GRID, not the span. Widening the pool makes each texel cover more
// world, so the wave covers more world per frame too, and the two cancel
// exactly. Raising the grid to 1024 for sharpness therefore doubled how long a
// ripple took to cross (19 seconds), and a 19-second ripple does not read as
// water, it reads as the surface slowly warping. 512 puts it at 8.8s and costs
// a quarter as much: the step runs one invocation per texel per frame.
//
// So widening this is nearly free in the ways that matter — the pace is
// unchanged and the surface detail is procedural, so neither cares. What it
// does cost is SIMULATION resolution: 340 units over 512 texels is 660mm each,
// and a footfall ring is about fifteen of them. Past roughly 400 the rings
// start to show their steps, and the answer then is a bigger grid and a slower
// wave, which is a trade rather than a fix.
const SIM_SPAN: f32 = 340.0;

// ── The wave equation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Backed off the stability limit deliberately. It also sets how fast a ring
// GROWS, and a slower ring stays near whoever made it — which matters more here
// than crossing the pool quickly, now that ripples are spent before they could.

// PER FRAME, and the only thing that decides how FAR a ripple carries — width
// does not enter into it. At 39 units a second the half-distances are: 0.991 to
// 50 units, 0.982 to 25, 0.968 to 14. The pool's radius is 170, so anything
// above about 0.98 sends rings most of the way across it and they arrive
// looking as strong as they left, which is what reads as wrong — a real ripple
// is spent within a few paces.

// HEIGHT damping, and its absence was a real bug rather than a small number.
// Wallace damps the VELOCITY only, which is right for an endless pool: a
// displaced height then loses amplitude solely by radiating, so a ring keeps
// almost everything it started with and simply travels. Measured, that ring was
// still visible 101 units out after two seconds and arrived looking as strong
// as it left. Damping the height as well makes a ripple actually SPEND itself:
// with these two it is gone inside about a second, which is what a footfall in
// a pool does.
const HEIGHT_DAMPING: f32 = 0.965;
// The only thing that sets a ripple's WIDTH — damping does not touch it, so
// these two are independent. Measured: 5.0 gives a 10.6-unit ring, 3.5 gives
// 8.0, and 2.5 gives 6.6 but at 10 texels it is nearing the point where the
// ring shows its steps rather than its shape.
const STEP_R: f32 = 3.5;          // world units a limb disturbs
const LIMB_SLOTS: i32 = 6;        // how many of the anchors above to read

const STEP_BAND: f32 = 3.0;       // how near the surface it has to be
const STEP_SPEED: f32 = 0.35;     // extra push per unit of speed

// ── Colour ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Blue, not teal. The green was coming from the body colour itself, not from
// the light — a pool reads as blue because water absorbs red first and leaves
// the short end of the spectrum, so the deep end should get BLUER as it darkens
// rather than greyer.
// CRYSTAL, which is a claim about the DEEP end more than the shallow one. Dirty
// water darkens toward black as it deepens because the light is scattered out
// of it; clear water stays BLUE, because the only thing removing light is
// absorption and absorption spares the short wavelengths. So the deep colour
// keeps its blue and simply loses the rest — that, plus a lower absorption
// coefficient below, is the whole difference between a pool and a pond.
// DEEP is the colour of open water with NOTHING under it, and that is the case
// it has to carry: with no floor the depth term saturates immediately, so this
// single colour is the whole pool. It was mixed as an absence — the shallow
// tone with the light taken out — which is why it went slate. Water with no
// bottom is not darker water, it is BLUER water: the red is gone by then and
// what is left is the short end, still bright.
const FOAM: vec3f = vec3f(0.95, 1.0, 1.0);
const SKY_LOW: vec3f = vec3f(0.72, 0.86, 0.95);
const SKY_HIGH: vec3f = vec3f(0.30, 0.58, 0.95);
const MIRRORING: f32 = 0.30;      // sky surviving a straight-down view
// CLEAR looking down through a hand's depth, murky through a body's. This is
// the pair that makes someone look like they are IN the water rather than
// behind a coloured sheet: her shins show through, her thighs go green, and the
// waterline is where the two meet.
// A FLOOR IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF WATER. At 0.14 a shallow pool was so nearly
// invisible that whatever lay under it — a ground plane, its grid — simply
// showed through unchanged, and the surface stopped reading as a surface at all
// wherever it was shallow. Worse, it made a hard edge exactly where the floor
// ended and the depth term jumped: water on one side, floor on the other.
//
// 0.34 still shows the bottom clearly, which is what clear water does. It just
// no longer pretends to be nothing.
const CLEAR: f32 = 0.34;          // a shallow column tints, without hiding
const OPACITY: f32 = 0.88;        // a deep one hides what is under it
// Shorter, so the shallows reach the deep colour sooner and the boundary where
// a floor ends stops announcing itself.
const COLUMN: f32 = 14.0;         // world units of water to go from one to the other
const WADE_FOAM: f32 = 2.5;       // world units of foam collar at the waterline
// ── Seen from inside ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A plane has no inside, and that is exactly what gives a fake one away: put
// the camera below it and the world is unchanged. Water is a VOLUME, so a ray
// that starts underwater is tinted by however far it travels before it leaves.
//
// Beer-Lambert rather than a linear ramp: absorption is exponential, which is
// why a metre of pool water is nearly clear and ten metres is opaque. A linear
// fade cannot be both, and reads as a coloured sheet at every distance.
const UNDER_TINT: vec3f = vec3f(0.070, 0.470, 0.780);
const UNDER_RANGE: f32 = 30.0;    // world units to lose most of the colour
const UNDER_MAX: f32 = 0.88;      // never quite opaque, so the scene stays legible
const NORMAL_SCALE: f32 = 0.035;  // height units per world unit, for the normal
// DISPLACEMENT, in world units. The reference simulations displace real
// geometry, and that is why their ripples have height: a raised crest hides
// what is behind it and shifts what is under it. A flat plane with a bent
// normal can only ever change the shading, which is why these ripples read as a
// pattern painted on glass however the normal is tuned.
//
// The plane cannot be tessellated from here, so the RAY is solved against the
// displaced surface instead: guess the flat intersection, ask how tall the
// water is there, re-solve. Two iterations is plenty for a surface this shallow
// and it costs two extra grid samples.
const SIM_LIFT: f32 = 4.0;        // world units per unit of simulated height
const CHOP_LIFT: f32 = 0.5;
const SOLVE_STEPS: i32 = 2;
// The pool is a DISC. A square grid put a straight edge across the scene that
// nothing in the world explained; a circle reads as a pond.
//
// The fade is LONG — nearly half the radius — because a short one only moves
// the hard edge rather than removing it. And it damps the DISPLACEMENT as well
// as the opacity: a surface still standing in relief at the rim shows its
// boundary through the warp no matter how transparent it has become, which is
// the giveaway a short fade leaves behind.
const DISC_EDGE: f32 = 0.52;      // where the fade begins, as a fraction of the radius

// ── The resting surface, and the light on it ─────────────────────────────────
// The simulation is flat everywhere nothing has touched it, and flat water is a
// mirror. This is the chop that makes it a surface — and the reason the white
// specks in a reference photograph exist at all.
//
// ANISOTROPIC, which is the one thing the Blender graph was unambiguous about:
// every mapping node in it scales one axis and not the other. Stretching the
// domain is what turns noise into FLOW; isotropic noise reads as stone.
// Features every ~1.8 world units, which is where the surface stops reading as
// low resolution. AMP moves WITH it and inversely: a gradient noise's slope
// scales with its frequency, so making the chop 2.5x finer at a fixed amplitude
// makes it 2.5x steeper and floods the glints. Measured: (0.22, 0.55) and
// (0.55, 0.22) both land at 0.7% sparkle, and only the second has fine detail.
const CHOP_SCALE: f32 = 0.55;     // wave features per WORLD unit
const CHOP_ASPECT: f32 = 0.35;    // how far the domain is stretched crosswise
const CHOP_AMP: f32 = 0.22;
const CHOP_DRIFT: f32 = 0.35;
const CHOP_WARP: f32 = 0.55;      // how far the lattice is bent out of alignment
// The sun, named here because an effect cannot ask the scene for it. Points
// FROM the surface TOWARD the light.
const SUN_DIR: vec3f = vec3f(0.42, 0.66, -0.62);
const SUN_TINT: vec3f = vec3f(1.0, 1.0, 0.98);
// A very tight lobe, and it has to be. Measured on this chop: 400 lights 3% of
// the surface and reads as haze, 1800 lights 0.7% and reads as the scattered
// dashes a photograph has. The dots are LIGHT, not a texture — they sit where
// the wave normal happens to bisect sun and eye, which is why they line up
// along crests and why the ripples off her feet get them too.
const GLINT_POW: f32 = 1800.0;
const GLINT: f32 = 3.0;
// A lobe this tight is far NARROWER THAN A PIXEL, and that is fine only while
// the surface is calm. Once a dance fills the pool with ripples the normal
// swings within a single pixel, neighbouring pixels fall in and out of the
// lobe, and the highlight scintillates — the flashing dots. This is ordinary
// specular aliasing and the ordinary answer is Toksvig's: where the normal
// varies fast across a pixel, WIDEN the lobe to cover what it is varying
// between, and dim it to keep roughly the same energy. A calm surface is
// untouched; a churned one goes from glitter to sheen, which is what churned
// water does anyway.
//
// Measured WITHOUT fwidth, which cannot be called here: this function returns
// early for every ray that misses the disc or hits geometry first, so by the
// time a normal exists the control flow is not uniform and the derivative is
// illegal. The estimate below is the same quantity reasoned about instead —
// how much surface a pixel covers, times how steeply that surface is turning.
// Both of the things that actually cause the shimmer are in it: fine chop, and
// distance.
const GLINT_POW_MIN: f32 = 90.0;  // the widest the lobe is allowed to open
const GLINT_FILTER: f32 = 26.0;   // how strongly per-pixel normal wobble opens it
// Vertical arc of the frame, as a ratio — 2*tan(fov/2) for a 45 degree camera.
// Only sets the scale of the estimate above, alongside GLINT_FILTER.
const PIXEL_ARC: f32 = 0.83;

fn wHash2(p: vec2f) -> vec2f {
  let k = vec2f(0.3183099, 0.3678794);
  let x = p * k + k.yx;
  return -1.0 + 2.0 * fract(16.0 * k * fract(x.x * x.y * (x.x + x.y)));
}

/** Gradient noise with its ANALYTIC derivative: (value, d/dx, d/dy).
 *
 *  Analytic because the derivative IS the surface normal here, and a central
 *  difference is a blurred normal whose blur width is a constant nobody can
 *  defend. Gradient rather than value noise because value noise puts its
 *  extrema on the lattice and reads as blobs on a grid. */
fn wNoised(p: vec2f) -> vec3f {
  let i = floor(p);
  let f = fract(p);
  let u = f * f * f * (f * (f * 6.0 - 15.0) + 10.0);
  let du = 30.0 * f * f * (f * (f - 2.0) + 1.0);
  let ga = wHash2(i + vec2f(0.0, 0.0));
  let gb = wHash2(i + vec2f(1.0, 0.0));
  let gc = wHash2(i + vec2f(0.0, 1.0));
  let gd = wHash2(i + vec2f(1.0, 1.0));
  let va = dot(ga, f - vec2f(0.0, 0.0));
  let vb = dot(gb, f - vec2f(1.0, 0.0));
  let vc = dot(gc, f - vec2f(0.0, 1.0));
  let vd = dot(gd, f - vec2f(1.0, 1.0));
  let k1 = vb - va;
  let k2 = vc - va;
  let k3 = va - vb - vc + vd;
  let deriv = ga + u.x * (gb - ga) + u.y * (gc - ga) + u.x * u.y * (ga - gb - gc + gd)
            + du * vec2f(k1 + k3 * u.y, k2 + k3 * u.x);
  return vec3f(va + k1 * u.x + k2 * u.y + k3 * u.x * u.y, deriv);
}

/** Chop slope in WORLD units — three octaves, each rotated ~29 degrees off the
 *  last so the lattices never line up, each drifting at its own rate so the
 *  surface does not slide as one sheet. */
fn wChop(xz: vec2f, t: f32) -> vec3f {
  let r = mat2x2f(0.8776, 0.4794, -0.4794, 0.8776);
  // DOMAIN WARP, and it is not decoration. Gradient noise lives on a lattice,
  // and a specular lobe this tight picks out the lattice-regular extrema rather
  // than the waves — rendered out, the glints line up into a visible diagonal
  // grid of dots, which is the array that was flashing. Bending the coordinate
  // with a slower noise first destroys that alignment; it is the same trick the
  // Blender graph opens with, and it is there for the same reason.
  let w0 = wNoised(xz * CHOP_SCALE * 0.09 + vec2f(t * 0.05, 0.0)).x;
  let w1 = wNoised(xz * CHOP_SCALE * 0.09 + vec2f(11.3, 5.7)).x;
  let warped = xz + vec2f(w0, w1) * CHOP_WARP / max(CHOP_SCALE, 1e-4);
  var p = vec2f(warped.x * CHOP_SCALE * CHOP_ASPECT, warped.y * CHOP_SCALE);
  var g = vec2f(0.0);
  var hgt = 0.0;
  var amp = 1.0;
  for (var k = 0; k < 3; k = k + 1) {
    let nd = wNoised(p + vec2f(t * CHOP_DRIFT, t * CHOP_DRIFT * 0.6));
    hgt = hgt + nd.x * amp;
    g = g + nd.yz * amp;
    p = r * p * 2.03;
    amp = amp * 0.45;
  }
  return vec3f(g * CHOP_AMP, hgt);
}

fn wWorld(uv: vec2f) -> vec2f { return SIM_AT + (uv - vec2f(0.5)) * SIM_SPAN; }
fn wUV(xz: vec2f) -> vec2f { return (xz - SIM_AT) / SIM_SPAN + vec2f(0.5); }

fn gridStep(uv: vec2f, prev: vec4f, dt: f32) -> vec4f {
  // Flat and at rest. Unlike fog there IS a correct initial state for water and
  // it is nothing — every wave you see should be one that something made.
  if (rzGridFrame() == 0) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // ONE FRAME PER STEP at most. This scheme's stability limit is a coefficient
  // near 2 per frame, so a 200ms hitch asking for twelve frames of acceleration
  // at once does not make slow water, it makes NaN that never leaves the grid
  // because the grid is its own input.
  let n = clamp(dt * 60.0, 0.0, 1.0);

  let te = rzGridTexel();
  let hL = rzGridPrev(uv - vec2f(te, 0.0)).x;
  let hR = rzGridPrev(uv + vec2f(te, 0.0)).x;
  let hD = rzGridPrev(uv - vec2f(0.0, te)).x;
  let hU = rzGridPrev(uv + vec2f(0.0, te)).x;

  var h = prev.x;
  var v = prev.y;

  let avg = (hL + hR + hD + hU) * 0.25;
  v += (avg - h) * params.WAVE_SPEED * n;
  v *= pow(params.DAMPING, n);
  h += v * n;
  h *= pow(HEIGHT_DAMPING, n);

  // ── Where she meets the water ──
  //
  // Every joint is measured against the WATERLINE, not against the floor, and
  // only the ones actually crossing it disturb anything. That is what makes
  // this the contact point rather than the foot: raise WATER_Y to her waist and
  // the ankles fall silent while the knees and hips take over, which is what
  // wading looks like. An arm swung down into the surface rings on its own.
  let world = wWorld(uv);
  for (var c = 0; c < rzSubjectCount(); c++) {
    for (var slot = 0; slot < LIMB_SLOTS; slot++) {
      let a = rzAnchor(c, slot);
      if (!a.valid) { continue; }
      let d = distance(world, a.pos.xz);
      if (d > STEP_R) { continue; }
      let imm = clamp(1.0 - abs(a.pos.y - WATER_Y) / STEP_BAND, 0.0, 1.0);
      if (imm <= 0.0) { continue; }
      // INTO THE HEIGHT, not the velocity, and briefly. Pressing velocity every
      // frame is a sustained impulse, and on an explicit scheme that excites the
      // GRID frequency: the ring came out a texel-scale bullseye carrying six
      // times the high-frequency energy this does. Wallace adds to height.
      let k = 0.5 - cos((1.0 - d / STEP_R) * 3.14159265) * 0.5;
      let speed = length(a.vel.xz) + abs(a.vel.y);
      h -= k * imm * (params.STEP_PUSH + speed * STEP_SPEED * 0.01) * n * dt * 6.0;
    }
    // The torso, for water deep enough to wade in. Wider and gentler than a
    // foot, because a body displaces more and slower.
    let hip = rzSubjectHip(c);
    let dh = distance(world, hip.xz);
    if (dh < STEP_R * 1.6) {
      let immH = clamp(1.0 - abs(hip.y - WATER_Y) / (STEP_BAND * 2.5), 0.0, 1.0);
      if (immH > 0.0) {
        let kh = 0.5 - cos((1.0 - dh / (STEP_R * 1.6)) * 3.14159265) * 0.5;
        h -= kh * immH * params.STEP_PUSH * 0.5 * n * dt * 6.0;
      }
    }
  }

  // Bounded, because the grid feeds itself: one bad frame is otherwise
  // permanent. Clamping the STATE is the insurance the ping-pong needs.
  h = clamp(h, -2.0, 2.0);
  v = clamp(v, -0.5, 0.5);
  return vec4f(h, v, 0.0, 0.0);
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let cam = rzCameraPos();
  let scenePos = bgWorldPos(ray, depth);
  let toScene = distance(scenePos, cam);

  // ── The camera is UNDER the surface ──
  //
  // Everything it sees is seen THROUGH water, so the whole frame is tinted by
  // the distance the ray covers before it leaves — either at the surface, if it
  // is looking up, or at whatever it hits.
  if (cam.y < WATER_Y) {
    let camR = length(wUV(cam.xz) - vec2f(0.5)) * 2.0;
    if (camR <= 1.0) {
      // Where the ray breaks the surface. Only ever ahead of us looking UP;
      // level or downward it never leaves, so the whole path is submerged.
      var exitT = 1e9;
      if (ray.y > 1e-4) { exitT = (WATER_Y - cam.y) / ray.y; }
      let path = max(min(toScene, exitT), 0.0);
      let absorbed = 1.0 - exp(-path / UNDER_RANGE);

      return vec4f(UNDER_TINT, clamp(absorbed, 0.0, 1.0) * UNDER_MAX);
    }
  }

  if (abs(ray.y) < 1e-4) { return vec4f(0.0); }
  let t = (WATER_Y - cam.y) / ray.y;
  if (t <= 0.0) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // Solve the ray against the DISPLACED surface, not the flat plane. Each pass
  // asks how tall the water is where the last guess landed and re-intersects.
  var tt = t;
  var hit = cam + ray * tt;
  for (var i = 0; i < SOLVE_STEPS; i = i + 1) {
    let gg = wUV(hit.xz);
    // Flat by the rim, so the pool has no relief to show an edge with.
    let taper = 1.0 - smoothstep(DISC_EDGE, 1.0, length(gg - vec2f(0.5)) * 2.0);
    let lift = (rzGrid(gg).x * SIM_LIFT + wChop(hit.xz, time).z * CHOP_LIFT) * taper;
    tt = (WATER_Y + lift - cam.y) / ray.y;
    if (tt <= 0.0) { return vec4f(0.0); }
    hit = cam + ray * tt;
  }
  let g = wUV(hit.xz);
  // ROUND, not square. Radius from the middle, so the pool has no corners.
  let rad = length(g - vec2f(0.5)) * 2.0;
  if (rad > 1.0) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // DEPTH, so this is a surface in the scene and not a decal over it. Whatever
  // the scene drew nearer than the plane stays in front; whatever it drew BEYOND
  // is underwater, which is how her legs go under with no per-model work.
  if (toScene < tt - 0.05) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // ── The simulated surface, differenced into a slope ──
  let te = rzGridTexel();
  let sL = rzGrid(g - vec2f(te, 0.0)).x;
  let sR = rzGrid(g + vec2f(te, 0.0)).x;
  let sD = rzGrid(g - vec2f(0.0, te)).x;
  let sU = rzGrid(g + vec2f(0.0, te)).x;
  // TWO SOURCES, summed. The grid carries what something DID to the water and
  // is flat everywhere nothing has; the chop carries the surface being a
  // surface. Neither alone is water — the simulation alone is a mirror with
  // rings in it, and the chop alone cannot be disturbed.
  let simSlope = vec2f(sL - sR, sD - sU) / NORMAL_SCALE;
  let slope = simSlope + wChop(hit.xz, time).xy;
  let n = normalize(vec3f(slope.x, 1.0, slope.y));

  let view = normalize(cam - hit);
  let NV = clamp(dot(n, view), 0.0, 1.0);
  // Fresnel, floored: physical water reflects 2% from above, which on a stage
  // camera is no reflection at all.
  let fres = clamp(MIRRORING + (1.0 - MIRRORING) * pow(1.0 - NV, 5.0), 0.0, 1.0);

  // ── Depth: how much water the ray crossed before it hit anything ──
  // This is what a pool's colour actually IS, and it comes free — the scene's
  // own depth is already an argument to this function.
  let under = max(toScene - tt, 0.0);
  let column = clamp(under / COLUMN, 0.0, 1.0);
  let body = mix(params.SHALLOW_COLOR, params.DEEP_COLOR, column);

  // NO FOAM ON A RIPPLE. This was the whole error, and no threshold could fix
  // it because the layer should not exist: a ripple is a NORMAL, and it is seen
  // through the specular and the reflection that normal bends. Wallace's demo —
  // the reference this simulation comes from — renders its water with
  // reflection, refraction and caustics and has no foam anywhere in it. Painting
  // white on every crest gave a cloudy annulus around her feet that read as
  // spilt milk, and thinning it only made a thinner ring of the wrong thing.
  //
  // Foam is for where water MEETS something — a shoreline, a hull, her calves.
  // That is the collar, and it is the only white on this surface.
  let foam = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, WADE_FOAM, under);

  let bounce = reflect(-view, n);
  let sky = mix(SKY_LOW, SKY_HIGH, clamp(bounce.y, 0.0, 1.0));
  // The rim, faded and circular: the plane is unbounded and the simulation is
  // not, so without this the surface stops dead somewhere in the middle
  // distance, and a straight edge there reads as a bug where a curved one reads
  // as a bank. Squared, so it leaves gently rather than in a band — a linear
  // smoothstep over this distance still shows a ring where its slope peaks.
  //
  // Declared HERE rather than beside the alpha it also feeds: WGSL resolves
  // locals in source order inside a function, so a value used by the colour
  // below has to exist above it.
  let fade = 1.0 - smoothstep(DISC_EDGE, 1.0, rad);
  let rim = fade * fade;

  // THE GLINTS. Sun caught on a wave face — the white specks in the reference,
  // computed rather than painted, so they follow the chop AND the ripples she
  // makes without either being drawn twice.
  let half = normalize(normalize(SUN_DIR) + view);
  // How far the normal moves across ONE pixel, estimated. A pixel at distance
  // tt covers roughly tt * PIXEL_ARC / verticalResolution world units; multiply
  // by the slope to get how much the surface turns over that span. Far water
  // and choppy water both raise it, which is exactly when the lobe stops being
  // resolvable.
  let pixel = tt * PIXEL_ARC / max(rzResolution().y, 1.0);
  let wobble = clamp(length(slope) * pixel * GLINT_FILTER, 0.0, 1.0);
  let power = mix(GLINT_POW, GLINT_POW_MIN, wobble);
  let glint = pow(max(dot(n, half), 0.0), power) * GLINT * (1.0 - wobble * 0.55);

  // The detail fades WITH the surface. Left at full strength, the glints and
  // the collar go on sparkling after the water beneath them has gone, and a
  // ring of loose highlights is a worse edge than the one being hidden.
  let color = mix(body, max(sky, body), fres) * (1.0 - foam * rim)
            + FOAM * (foam * rim) + SUN_TINT * (glint * rim);

  // COVERAGE BY WATER COLUMN, not a constant. Absorption is what makes a body
  // in water read as submerged: near the surface almost nothing is taken out,
  // and a metre down the colour is gone. A single opacity cannot say both, and
  // at 0.9 it said neither — it drew a flat sheet over her legs and she looked
  // like she was standing BEHIND the water. Fresnel still takes it to opaque
  // edge-on, which is what hides the far bank.
  let depthAlpha = mix(CLEAR, OPACITY, column);
  let alpha = clamp(mix(depthAlpha, 1.0, pow(1.0 - NV, 5.0)) + foam * 0.5, 0.0, 1.0) * rim;
  return vec4f(color, alpha);
}

#param float SCAN_DEPTH 0.26 0 1 — how black the gaps between them go, 0..1
#param float GRAIN 0.07 0 0.4 — strength at its peak
#param float GRILLE_DEPTH 0.14 0 0.5 — darkening in the gaps between stripes

// CRT Static — an old set, flickering.
//
// A scanned beam, a phosphor grille, static, a rolling bar, tape sway and the
// unsteady brightness of a set that has been on too long.
//
// BOTH HALVES: an overlay laid over everything, and a GLITCH that displaces the
// picture itself where there is picture to displace.
//
// The second half used to shift the ground's colour away from the background
// beside it, and that was not a tuning problem. rzScene is linear and
// pre-tone-map, so a resample has to put what it fetched back into display
// space, and doing that by hand — a Reinhard shoulder, a 2.2 gamma — is near
// enough at a glance and wrong everywhere it matters. rzSceneDisplay runs the
// composite's OWN chain instead: exposure, view transform, grade, gamma. Fetch a
// pixel at its own uv and you get the pixel that is already there, so a
// displacement of zero is invisible and the treated region stops reading a shade
// off from the untreated one.
//
// A FILTER OVER THE WHOLE FRAME. The picture is rebuilt from two sources — the
// scene where it was drawn, rzBackground's colour where it was not — and the
// tube is applied to all of it. Treating only the drawn part is what made the
// ground read differently from the sky beside it: a real set has no opinion
// about which of its pixels are geometry.
//
// WHAT IT ERASES: background EFFECTS. Stars, a waveform, anything on the
// background mount composes in the pass after this one, so it cannot be read and
// cannot be re-drawn. Over a TRANSPARENT background — a backdrop video, an alpha
// export — there is nothing to rebuild, so those pixels take the overlay alone
// and keep what they had.
//
// Alpha-over rather than additive: half of what makes a CRT read as a CRT is the
// black between its lines, and additive can only brighten.
//
// THE GRILLE IS AN HONEST COMPROMISE. A real shadow mask multiplies the image
// PER CHANNEL — red survives the red stripe, green does not. Compositing gives
// one scalar coverage, so per-channel attenuation is not expressible. What it
// does instead is darken at the gaps by the mask's own profile and lay the
// phosphor colour back over the stripes, which lands close on ordinary content
// and drifts on heavily saturated colour.
//
// TWO CLOCKS, both quantised. A preview at 60fps and an export at 30 would
// otherwise draw different noise from the same second of the same scene.
//
// MEASURED IN THE PICTURE, never in device pixels — a 320-line tube is 320 lines
// whether shown in a 700px preview or written to 4K. Each pattern also fades
// where the output cannot resolve it: a grille finer than the sampling that
// draws it is not a grille, it is moire that crawls.

// ── The beam ──
// COARSER THAN A REAL TUBE. 480 lines is what a set had, and at that pitch the
// scan is nearly invisible on a modern display — the pattern has to be seen to
// do any work, so this is a tube you are sitting closer to than you were.
const SCAN_LINES: f32 = 320.0;    // lines down the whole picture

// GAUSSIAN, not a sine. A sine spends as much of the line dark as lit and reads
// as a grey wash; a real line is a beam with a bright core and a fast falloff.
const SCAN_WIDTH: f32 = 0.24;     // its width as a fraction of the line pitch

// ── The phosphor grille ──
const TRIADS: f32 = 160.0;        // RGB triads across the whole picture

const GRILLE_TINT: f32 = 0.05;    // phosphor colour laid back over the stripes

// ── Static ──

const GRAIN_FLOOR: f32 = 0.30;    // fraction of params.GRAIN always present, 0..1
const GRAIN_COLS: f32 = 420.0;    // grain cells across the picture
const GRAIN_ROWS: f32 = 236.0;    // and down it
const GRAIN_HZ: f32 = 20.0;       // times a second the static is redrawn
const BURST_HZ: f32 = 0.7;        // how fast the signal wanders in and out

// ── The roll ──
const ROLL_SPEED: f32 = 0.26;     // pictures a second the bar travels
const ROLL_WIDTH: f32 = 0.10;     // its height, as a fraction of the picture
const ROLL_LIFT: f32 = 0.05;      // how much its body brightens
const ROLL_EDGE: f32 = 0.10;      // how much its leading edge darkens

// ── The tape ──
// The mask sways and tears while the picture under it holds still. A viewer
// reads an unstable signal from an unstable mask, which is the part of tape
// damage an overlay can carry honestly.
const SWAY: f32 = 0.0045;         // slow horizontal drift, fraction of the width
const SWAY_HZ: f32 = 1.6;         // how fast it wanders
const JITTER: f32 = 0.35;         // share of the sway that is per-line hash, 0..1
const TEAR_HZ: f32 = 1.1;         // times a second a tear is rolled for
const TEAR_ODDS: f32 = 0.42;      // how often that roll lands, 0..1
const TEAR_WIDTH: f32 = 0.030;    // height of a torn band, fraction of the picture
const TEAR_DRAG: f32 = 0.055;     // how far it drags the mask sideways
const TEAR_LIFT: f32 = 0.14;      // how much it flares

// ── The glitch ──
// AS EVENTS, NOT ON A PERIOD. One envelope on a fixed loop is the most
// predictable thing a shader can do: watch it twice and you know when the next
// one lands, how hard it hits and how long it stays. It stops reading as a fault
// and starts reading as an animation.
//
// So time is cut into slots and each slot ROLLS for a burst — most come up
// empty, which is where the quiet stretches come from. A slot that fires picks
// its own start inside itself, its own length, its own strength, its own BAND of
// the picture, and its own mix of tearing against dropout. Nothing is shared
// between two bursts except the machinery, so they arrive off the beat, at
// different sizes, in different places, and no two are the same kind of wrong.
//
// Deterministic in the second, not in the frame: every roll is a hash of the
// slot index, so an export replays exactly what the preview showed.
const SLOT: f32 = 0.9;            // seconds per roll
const ODDS: f32 = 0.26;           // chance a slot fires at all, 0..1
const LIFE_MIN: f32 = 0.06;       // shortest burst, seconds
const LIFE_MAX: f32 = 0.55;       // longest. Past ~0.6s a burst stops reading as an event
const BAND_MIN: f32 = 0.16;       // tightest band a burst takes, fraction of height
const BAND_MAX: f32 = 1.5;        // widest — past 1 it takes the whole frame
// HOW HARD, and it is three separate rolls rather than one. A burst has a peak,
// a SHAPE that peak is reached through, and a flutter riding on top of it. Vary
// only the peak and every burst is still the same gesture at a different volume,
// which is what a single multiplier gets you.
const PEAK_MIN: f32 = 0.22;       // a burst you could miss
const PEAK_MAX: f32 = 1.0;        // one you could not
const SHAPE_MIN: f32 = 0.22;      // low holds near its peak — a sustained dropout
const SHAPE_MAX: f32 = 2.2;       // high spikes and is gone — a single torn frame
const STUTTER: f32 = 0.55;        // how far the strength drops between flutters
const STUTTER_HZ: f32 = 22.0;     // flutters a second, in real time
// DROPOUT STREAKS. A displacement can only be seen where there is detail to
// displace — tear a flat sky sideways and nothing appears to happen — so tearing
// and block-fetching alone put every visible glitch on the one part of the frame
// that has texture. These replace a band of the picture outright, so they read
// wherever they land, and that is what spreads the fault over the whole height.
const STREAK_LINES: f32 = 110.0;  // bands the picture can drop out in
const STREAK_ODDS: f32 = 0.34;    // share of them gone at the peak of a burst
const STREAK_MIX: f32 = 0.7;      // how completely a dropped band is replaced
const STREAK_HZ: f32 = 26.0;      // times a second they are re-rolled
const WRAP: f32 = 240.0;          // seconds before the fault pattern repeats
const IDLE: f32 = 0.01;           // how much survives between bursts
// WHAT A QUIET STRETCH LOOKS LIKE. Not zero — a set is never perfectly received
// — but low enough that the picture reads as simply being on a tube, with the
// faults gone until the next one arrives.
const REST: f32 = 0.06;           // share of the tube's unrest present at rest
const WARP: f32 = 0.030;          // barrel bulge. The centre stays undistorted
const SLIP: f32 = 0.045;          // how far a torn band goes, fraction of the width
const TEAR_BANDS: f32 = 16.0;     // bands the height is cut into. Fewer is chunkier
const TEAR_SHARE: f32 = 0.30;     // share of them torn at a burst's peak, 0..1
const TEAR_RATE: f32 = 14.0;      // times a second the selection is redrawn
const TEAR_RAGGED: f32 = 0.30;    // per-line roughness on a torn band
// NOTHING TEARS UNTIL A BURST IS REALLY UNDER WAY. A linear response meant the
// resting trickle was always displacing something somewhere, which is what made
// the whole scene look like it was flowing.
const GATE_LO: f32 = 0.18;        // glitch level where tearing starts at all
const GATE_HI: f32 = 0.55;        // and where it is fully in
const SLIP_HZ: f32 = 30.0;        // times a second the tearing is redrawn
const ABERR: f32 = 1.7;           // colour separation, pixels of a 1440-line frame
const BLOCK_BIAS: f32 = 0.03;     // share of the frame dropping out at rest
const BLOCK_GAIN: f32 = 0.22;     // and how much more at the peak
const BLOCK_SHIFT: f32 = 0.055;    // how far a dropped block is fetched from
const BLOCK_HZ: f32 = 30.0;       // times a second the blocks are redrawn

// ── The tube ──
const FLICKER: f32 = 0.030;       // swing of the overall brightness
const FLICKER_HZ: f32 = 11.0;     // times a second it is re-judged
const FLASH: f32 = 0.10;          // strength of the occasional flash
const FLASH_HZ: f32 = 0.55;       // times a second a flash is re-judged
const INTERLACE: f32 = 0.5;       // 0 off, 1 full half-line field offset
const FIELD_HZ: f32 = 50.0;       // fields a second, PAL. 60 for NTSC
const VIGNETTE: f32 = 0.32;       // darkening toward the corners
const DIM: f32 = 0.03;           // a flat veil over everything, 0 for none
const PHOSPHOR: vec3f = vec3f(0.62, 0.80, 1.00);  // colour of the bright parts

/**
 * A stable hash, 0..1. NO sin, and that is the whole point.
 *
 * The usual shader hash multiplies a dot product through sine. Its inputs here
 * are tick counters — floor(clock * 30), and one of them scaled by another 300
 * — which after a few minutes of playback reach the millions, and sine of an
 * argument that large has no precision left in f32: argument reduction throws
 * the meaningful bits away and the numbers stop changing. Which looks like a set
 * that glitched normally for a while and then locked into one displacement, at
 * one strength, in one place, forever.
 *
 * BACKGROUNDING THE TAB IS THE FAST PATH TO IT. Animation stops while hidden and
 * the clock does not, so coming back moves the counters by minutes in one step
 * — straight past the cliff rather than drifting toward it.
 *
 * The multiply-and-fract construction below has no such cliff. It degrades
 * slowly and evenly instead of falling off a shelf, and it costs less.
 */
fn crtHash(p3: vec3f) -> f32 {
  var p = fract(p3 * vec3f(0.1031, 0.1030, 0.0973));
  p = p + vec3f(dot(p, p.yzx + vec3f(33.33)));
  return fract((p.x + p.y) * p.z);
}

/**
 * One roll of a burst's dice, 0..1. Decorrelated in BOTH arguments.
 *
 * The sin-based hash beside this is fine for a pixel — thrown at a whole frame
 * of coordinates its flaws average out — but a burst asks it a handful of
 * questions a second, all with the same slot index and only the seed changing.
 * That is exactly where fract(sin(dot(...))) is worst: the answers correlate,
 * and a value meant to scatter over the frame instead lands in a narrow part of
 * its range. It put every glitch across her legs.
 *
 * This is Dave Hoskins' construction: fold both arguments into three channels on
 * irrational-ish strides, mix them against each other, and take the fraction.
 * Cheap, and it decorrelates the seeds rather than merely disguising them.
 */
fn crtRand(n: f32, seed: f32) -> f32 {
  var p = fract(vec3f(n * 0.1031 + seed * 0.0711, n * 0.1030 + seed * 0.1367, n * 0.0973 + seed * 0.0331));
  p = p + vec3f(dot(p, p.yzx + vec3f(33.33)));
  return fract((p.x + p.y) * p.z);
}

/** Value noise, 0..1. Smooth in every axis, so a displacement built from it
 *  slides rather than steps. */
fn crtNoise(p: vec3f) -> f32 {
  let i = floor(p);
  let f = fract(p);
  let u = f * f * (3.0 - 2.0 * f);
  let a = mix(crtHash(i + vec3f(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)), crtHash(i + vec3f(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)), u.x);
  let b = mix(crtHash(i + vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 0.0)), crtHash(i + vec3f(1.0, 1.0, 0.0)), u.x);
  let c = mix(crtHash(i + vec3f(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)), crtHash(i + vec3f(1.0, 0.0, 1.0)), u.x);
  let e = mix(crtHash(i + vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 1.0)), crtHash(i + vec3f(1.0, 1.0, 1.0)), u.x);
  return mix(mix(a, b, u.y), mix(c, e, u.y), u.z);
}

/** A value that wanders rather than steps: two ticks of a hash, crossfaded. A
 *  hard cut every tick is a fault; this is a set that is merely old. */
fn crtDrift(t: f32, seed: f32) -> f32 {
  let i = floor(t);
  return mix(crtHash(vec3f(i, seed, 13.0)), crtHash(vec3f(i + 1.0, seed, 13.0)), smoothstep(0.0, 1.0, fract(t)));
}

/** src OVER dst, both premultiplied, so repeated OVER composes associatively.
 *  The caller divides the colour back out once at the end. */
fn crtOver(dst: vec4f, col: vec3f, alpha: f32) -> vec4f {
  let a = clamp(alpha, 0.0, 1.0);
  return vec4f(col * a + dst.rgb * (1.0 - a), a + dst.a * (1.0 - a));
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let aspect = res.x / max(res.y, 1.0);
  // THE CLOCK, WRAPPED. Every tick counter below derives from this, and one that
  // counts up forever eventually swamps any hash's precision however good it is.
  // Wrapping means the fault pattern repeats every WRAP seconds — imperceptible
  // in noise, longer than any export, and it survives the tab being hidden for
  // an hour and handing the effect a clock that jumped.
  let t = time - floor(time / WRAP) * WRAP;

  // ── The glitch, as whichever burst is loudest right now ──
  // Three slots are examined, not one: a burst starts anywhere inside its own
  // slot and can outlive it, so the previous and next both have to be asked or
  // events would be clipped at the seams — which would put a rhythm back in.
  var amt = 0.0;
  var bandC = 0.5;
  var bandH = 9.0;
  var tearMix = 1.0;
  var blockMix = 1.0;
  let slotNow = floor(t / max(SLOT, 1e-3));
  for (var k = -1; k <= 1; k = k + 1) {
    let s = slotNow + f32(k);
    // Most slots roll nothing. This is the whole source of the quiet.
    if (crtRand(s, 1.0) > ODDS) { continue; }
    let start = s * SLOT + crtRand(s, 2.0) * SLOT;
    let life = mix(LIFE_MIN, LIFE_MAX, crtRand(s, 3.0));
    let u = (t - start) / max(life, 1e-3);
    if (u < 0.0 || u > 1.0) { continue; }
    // THE SHAPE IS ROLLED TOO. A raised sine with a per-burst exponent: below one
    // it holds near its peak and reads as a sustained dropout, above one it
    // spikes and is gone before you locate it. The exponent is what stops every
    // burst being the same gesture at a different volume.
    let shape = mix(SHAPE_MIN, SHAPE_MAX, crtRand(s, 9.0));
    let peak = mix(PEAK_MIN, PEAK_MAX, crtRand(s, 4.0));
    // AND IT FLUTTERS ON THE WAY. A smooth arc is still a curve someone drew;
    // real signal loss judders while it happens. Rolled against real seconds
    // rather than against the burst's own progress, so a long burst and a short
    // one shake at the same rate instead of the short one appearing calmer.
    let stut = mix(1.0 - STUTTER, 1.0, crtRand(s * 137.0 + floor(t * STUTTER_HZ), 12.0));
    let env = pow(sin(u * 3.1415927), shape) * peak * stut;
    // The loudest burst wins rather than the sum: two overlapping events should
    // not stack into something neither of them was.
    if (env <= amt) { continue; }
    amt = env;
    // Over the WHOLE height, and a little past both ends so a burst can sit half
    // off the top or the bottom rather than always being fully on screen.
    bandC = crtRand(s, 5.0) * 1.3 - 0.15;
    bandH = mix(BAND_MIN, BAND_MAX, crtRand(s, 6.0));
    // What KIND of wrong this one is. A burst that is all tear and no dropout
    // looks nothing like one that is all dropout, and having both vary is most
    // of why no two read the same.
    tearMix = mix(0.1, 1.0, crtRand(s, 7.0));
    blockMix = mix(0.1, 1.0, crtRand(s, 8.0));
  }
  // WHERE it lands. Outside the band a burst still registers faintly, so a tear
  // across the middle does not leave the rest of the frame conspicuously clean.
  let inBand = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, max(bandH, 1e-3), abs(uv.y - bandC));
  // OUTSIDE THE BAND still counts for half. At a quarter a burst was effectively
  // confined to its band, so one stripe of the frame moved while the rest sat
  // perfectly still — which reads as a mask, not as a signal fault.
  let glitch = clamp(IDLE + amt * mix(0.3, 1.0, inBand), 0.0, 1.0);

  var acc = vec4f(0.0);

  // ── The glass ──
  let d = (uv - vec2f(0.5)) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0);
  let vig = clamp(dot(d, d) * VIGNETTE, 0.0, 1.0);
  acc = crtOver(acc, vec3f(0.0), clamp(DIM + vig, 0.0, 1.0));

  // ── Tape instability ──
  // Two timescales, because one alone does not read as tape. The sway wanders
  // over seconds and moves the whole mask together; the jitter is a fresh hash
  // per LINE per field, which is what makes an edge look torn rather than merely
  // displaced. A tear is a band where both jump at once.
  let field = crtHash(vec3f(floor(t * FIELD_HZ), 3.0, 5.0));
  let offset = select(0.0, 0.5 * INTERLACE, field > 0.5);
  let sway = (crtDrift(t * SWAY_HZ, 41.0) - 0.5) * 2.0;
  let lineId = floor(uv.y * SCAN_LINES);
  let jitter = (crtHash(vec3f(lineId, floor(t * FIELD_HZ), 17.0)) - 0.5) * 2.0;
  let tearTick = floor(t * TEAR_HZ);
  // Rolled, not scheduled: most ticks pass without one, so tears arrive in an
  // uneven rhythm instead of on a beat.
  let rolled = step(1.0 - TEAR_ODDS, crtHash(vec3f(tearTick, 51.0, 2.0)));
  let tearY = crtHash(vec3f(tearTick, 52.0, 3.0));
  let tear = rolled * (1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, TEAR_WIDTH, abs(uv.y - tearY)));
  // SCALED BY THE BURST. Sway and tears running constantly is a set that is
  // always broken, and a viewer stops reading them as faults within seconds.
  // Between bursts this collapses to almost nothing and the picture sits still.
  let unrest = mix(REST, 1.0, glitch);
  let shift = (mix(sway, jitter, JITTER) * SWAY + tear * TEAR_DRAG * sign(sway)) * unrest;
  // Everything patterned across the picture reads this rather than uv.x, so the
  // grille and the grain move together — a grain that stayed put while the
  // grille swayed would read as two overlays.
  let mx = uv.x + shift;

  // ── The scanned beam ──
  let linePx = res.y / SCAN_LINES;
  let scanOk = smoothstep(1.4, 2.6, linePx);
  let along = fract(uv.y * SCAN_LINES + offset) - 0.5;
  let beam = exp(-(along * along) / max(2.0 * SCAN_WIDTH * SCAN_WIDTH, 1e-5));
  acc = crtOver(acc, vec3f(0.0), params.SCAN_DEPTH * (1.0 - beam) * scanOk);

  // ── The phosphor grille ──
  // Three stripes to a triad, each a soft lobe rather than a hard band: a hard
  // one aliases into stripes of the wrong colour the moment the picture is not
  // an exact multiple of the mask.
  let subPx = res.x / (TRIADS * 3.0);
  let grilleOk = smoothstep(1.2, 2.4, subPx);
  let sub = fract(mx * TRIADS) * 3.0;
  let ph = vec3f(
    exp(-pow((sub - 0.5) * 1.7, 2.0)),
    exp(-pow((sub - 1.5) * 1.7, 2.0)),
    exp(-pow((sub - 2.5) * 1.7, 2.0)),
  );
  let lit = max(ph.r, max(ph.g, ph.b));
  acc = crtOver(acc, vec3f(0.0), params.GRILLE_DEPTH * (1.0 - lit) * grilleOk);
  acc = crtOver(acc, normalize(ph + vec3f(0.001)), GRILLE_TINT * lit * grilleOk);

  // ── The roll ──
  // Its body lifts and its LEADING edge drops dark, which is what reads as a
  // roll rather than a moving stripe.
  let roll = fract(uv.y + t * ROLL_SPEED);
  let body = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, ROLL_WIDTH, roll);
  let lead = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, ROLL_WIDTH * 0.22, fract(roll + ROLL_WIDTH * 0.22));
  acc = crtOver(acc, vec3f(0.0), ROLL_EDGE * lead * unrest);
  acc = crtOver(acc, PHOSPHOR, ROLL_LIFT * body * unrest);

  // ── Static ──
  // IN WAVES, not at a constant level. Held flat it reads as film grain, which
  // is a property of the stock and always there; a tube's noise is a property of
  // the SIGNAL, so it breathes.
  let burst = mix(GRAIN_FLOOR, 1.0, crtDrift(t * BURST_HZ, 29.0)) * mix(REST, 1.0, glitch);
  let cell = floor(vec2f(mx, uv.y) * vec2f(GRAIN_COLS, GRAIN_ROWS));
  let n = crtHash(vec3f(cell, floor(t * GRAIN_HZ)));
  // Signed, so static darkens as often as it lightens. A one-sided grain reads
  // as dust on the lens; a tube's noise sits either side of the picture.
  let swing = (n - 0.5) * 2.0;
  acc = crtOver(acc, select(vec3f(0.0), PHOSPHOR, swing > 0.0), params.GRAIN * burst * abs(swing));

  // The torn band flares as it drags — the tape losing contact, not just
  // slipping. Drawn after the grain so it sits on top of it.
  acc = crtOver(acc, PHOSPHOR, tear * TEAR_LIFT * unrest);

  // ── The tube's own brightness ──
  acc = crtOver(acc, vec3f(0.0), FLICKER * crtDrift(t * FLICKER_HZ, 7.0));
  // AND ITS OCCASIONAL FLASH. Raised to a high power so most of the curve sits
  // near nothing and only its peaks arrive: a flash that happens continuously is
  // a brightness setting, and what makes a recording read as old is that it is
  // mostly steady and then briefly is not.
  acc = crtOver(acc, PHOSPHOR, FLASH * pow(crtDrift(t * FLASH_HZ, 61.0), 6.0) * glitch);

  let over = vec4f(acc.rgb, clamp(acc.a, 0.0, 1.0));

  // Polar barrel: the radius pushed out by its own square, so the centre is
  // untouched and only the edges bow. On the SAMPLE coordinate, never the pixel.
  let q = uv * 2.0 - vec2f(1.0);
  let rad = length(q);
  let ang = atan2(q.y, q.x);
  let bent = rad / (1.0 - WARP * rad * rad);
  let lens = vec2f(bent * cos(ang), bent * sin(ang)) * 0.5 + vec2f(0.5);

  // Two noises MULTIPLIED: one alone displaces every line a little, the product
  // is mostly zero with occasional strong lines, which is what tearing looks
  // like. Quantised in t so an export matches its preview.
  let sq = floor(t * SLIP_HZ);
  let ln = lens.y * SCAN_LINES;
  // WHICH BANDS ARE TORN, and mostly none of them.
  //
  // A continuous noise over the height does not tear, it FLOWS: every band is
  // displaced by a little, all the time, and the picture reads as a liquid
  // rather than a broken signal. Tearing is discrete — a few bands jump, the
  // rest hold perfectly still, and the contrast between them is the whole
  // effect.
  //
  // So the height is cut into bands and each one is PICKED or not. Most are not,
  // even at a burst's peak. A picked band takes a whole displacement rather than
  // a fraction of one, and holds it for a tick instead of being redrawn every
  // frame — a band that changes every frame is a shimmer, not a tear.
  let gate = smoothstep(GATE_LO, GATE_HI, glitch);
  let bandId = floor(lens.y * TEAR_BANDS);
  let tick = floor(t * TEAR_RATE);
  let picked = step(1.0 - TEAR_SHARE * gate, crtRand(bandId * 13.0 + tick, 41.0));
  let howFar = (crtRand(bandId * 17.0 + tick, 43.0) - 0.5) * 2.0;
  // A ragged per-line edge ON THE TORN BANDS ONLY, so a tear does not look like
  // a rectangle someone slid sideways.
  let ragged = (crtNoise(vec3f(0.0, ln * 0.9, tick)) * 2.0 - 1.0) * TEAR_RAGGED;
  let drag = picked * (howFar + ragged) * SLIP * tearMix * gate + shift;

  // Blocky dropout: bands in x and y crossed, each block fetched from elsewhere.
  // NOT multiplied up. The reference scales this tick by 300 to decorrelate it,
  // which is exactly the move that pushes a hash past its precision; the hash
  // separates its own axes now, so the tick goes in as it is.
  let bt = floor(t * BLOCK_HZ);
  let level = BLOCK_BIAS + BLOCK_GAIN * blockMix * glitch;
  let bx = step(crtNoise(vec3f(0.0, lens.x * 3.0, bt)), level);
  let by = step(crtNoise(vec3f(0.0, lens.y * 4.0, bt)), level);
  let block = clamp(bx * by, 0.0, 1.0);
  let jump = (crtHash(vec3f(floor(ln), bt, 7.0)) - 0.5) * 2.0 * BLOCK_SHIFT;

  let st = vec2f(lens.x + drag + jump * block, lens.y);

  // ── The picture ──
  // COVERAGE, not a depth test. The scene's alpha is what carries the ground's
  // radial fade and its opacity; a depth test is binary and the plane writes
  // depth across its whole square whatever its alpha does, which is what turned
  // a sheet fading into the distance into a hard-edged slab.
  let cover = clamp(rzSceneAlpha(st), 0.0, 1.0);
  let bg = rzBackground();
  let solid = bg.w > 0.5;
  // One fetch per gun, through the composite's own transform, so an undisplaced
  // pixel comes back exactly as it already was.
  let sep = vec2f(ABERR / 1440.0 * (1.0 + glitch), 0.0);
  let guns = vec3f(
    rzSceneDisplay(st + sep).r,
    rzSceneDisplay(st).g,
    rzSceneDisplay(st - sep).b,
  );
  // Premultiplied throughout. Over a solid background the frame is opaque and
  // the background stands in behind the scene; over a TRANSPARENT one there is
  // nothing to stand in — a backdrop video lives behind the canvas — so the
  // coverage passes through and whatever is back there shows exactly as far as
  // the scene lets it.
  let picRgb = select(guns * cover, guns * cover + bg.rgb * (1.0 - cover), solid);
  let picA = select(cover, 1.0, solid);

  // The overlay, over the displaced picture, both premultiplied.
  let outRgb = over.rgb + picRgb * (1.0 - over.a);
  let outA = over.a + picA * (1.0 - over.a);
  var col = clamp(outRgb / max(outA, 1e-6), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));

  // ── Dropout streaks ──
  // The half of the fault that does not need detail underneath it. Rolled per
  // band per tick, so they scatter over the full height rather than following
  // whatever happens to be textured.
  let sId = floor(uv.y * STREAK_LINES);
  let gone = step(1.0 - STREAK_ODDS * glitch, crtRand(sId * 3.0 + floor(t * STREAK_HZ), 31.0));
  let lum = dot(col, vec3f(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));
  col = mix(col, PHOSPHOR * clamp(lum * 1.5 + 0.10, 0.0, 1.0), gone * STREAK_MIX * glitch);
  return vec4f(col, clamp(outA, 0.0, 1.0));
}

#param float PIXELS 256 48 480 — pixels DOWN the frame. 144 Game Boy, 240 NES, 270 modern
#param float LEVELS 16 2 32 — shades a colour may take. 4 is a Game Boy, 16 keeps a face
#param float PUNCH 0.85 0 2 — how far a pixel commits to the colour that owns it
#param float OUTLINE 0.9 0 1.5 — how strongly a form is inked. 0 for none
#param float STAGE_INK 0 0 1 — and how strongly the SET is. 0 leaves the ink to the cast
#param float SAT 1.25 0 2 — colour lift. A sprite palette is louder than a render
#param float DITHER 0 0 1 — ordered dither where the picture varies. 0 is sprite art

// 8-Bit — the whole frame re-rendered as pixel art.
//
// A FILTER, not a mask: the picture is rebuilt from the scene where it was drawn
// and rzBackground's colour where it was not, and all of it goes through one
// grid. A monitor has no opinion about which of its pixels are geometry.
//
// WHAT A SPRITE SHEET ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE, and it is three things:
//
// 1. FLAT BLOCKS OF COLOUR FROM A SMALL PALETTE. Not a fine gradient stepped
//    into bands — a jacket is ONE yellow with ONE shadow yellow. Quantising each
//    of R, G and B on its own gives 512 shades that drift in hue as they step,
//    which is a banded render and reads as one. The snap here is on HUE,
//    SATURATION and VALUE separately, so a lit surface walks down its own colour
//    ramp and lands where the neighbouring cell lands.
//
// 2. THE CAST IS INKED, AND THE SET IS NOT. The silhouette, and the seam between
//    one material and the next: hair against face, jacket against skirt, boot
//    against leg. This is what the eye reads as "drawn" rather than
//    "downsampled", and an earlier version had none of it on a scene with a
//    stage — its only outline came from COVERAGE falling away, and coverage
//    never falls away in the middle of a frame that is geometry all the way to
//    its edges. Depth does.
//
//    But a stage inked by the same rule is worse than one not inked at all: a
//    floor grid, a riser and the horizon are all real steps in depth, so they
//    all take a line, and a character drawn in ink stands in a room drawn in
//    ink and stops being the subject. Sprite art inks the sprite. So the ink is
//    masked to the cast by OBJECT ID — see ebIsCast — and STAGE_INK is there for
//    when the set should be drawn too.
//
// 3. NO DITHER. Ordered dither is a trick for showing more colours than a
//    palette holds; sprite art does the opposite and commits to the palette. It
//    is still here, off, for the frames that want a Game Boy's chequer.
//
// THE RESOLUTION IS A REAL ONE. 240 rows is the NES and the SNES; the Game Boy
// is 144, the Mega Drive 224, a modern indie canvas 270. A sprite sheet's
// character stands about 70 pixels tall, which a subject filling the frame
// reaches at PIXELS near 150; the default sits above that because a face wants
// cells to spend on it, and 48 is there for anyone who wants the Game Boy.

// —— The screen ——
// COUNTED DOWN THE HEIGHT, not across the width, and that is what keeps a pixel
// the same size when the export aspect changes.
//
// The camera's field of view is anchored VERTICALLY: narrow the frame to 9:16
// and the horizontal view narrows with it, so the subject grows relative to the
// width. Counting cells across the width then hands her more of them and the
// pixels come out finer — the same scene at a different pixel size, which is
// exactly what changing the aspect should not do. Counted down the height, the
// one axis the lens holds fixed, she keeps her pixel size and a wider frame
// simply gets more pixels beside her.
//
// TAPS PER PIXEL, CHOSEN FROM THE CELL'S ACTUAL SIZE — not a constant, because a
// constant is what made an export look different from its preview. A cell is
// about 2.5 screen pixels in the editor and about 8 at 4K; a fixed 3x3 covers the
// first completely and under a sixth of the second, so the same rule produced a
// different average and the export was a different picture.
const SAMPLE_CAP: i32 = 6;        // most taps per axis, so at most 36 per pixel
const SAMPLE_MIN: i32 = 2;        // fewest, so a tiny cell is still averaged

// —— The palette ——
// HUE AND SATURATION SNAP TOO, and that is the whole difference between this and
// a posterised render. A stage light sweeping a wall walks its hue continuously;
// snapped, the wall becomes a few flat fields with hard boundaries — which is
// what an artist paints, because an artist has sixteen colours and not sixteen
// million.
// These two do most of the flattening, and LEVELS does less of it than its name
// suggests: at 16 steps a lit surface still has its modelling, and what makes
// the result read as art is that every step of it lands on one of sixteen hues
// at one of four saturations. Drop LEVELS toward 4 for the banded Game Boy read.
const HUES: f32 = 16.0;           // hues the palette holds
const SATS: f32 = 4.0;            // saturation steps
// NEAR-GREYS KEEP THEIR OWN HUE. The hue of a near-white is whatever noise the
// last decimal held, so snapping it invents a colour cast: pale skin under a
// pink key went fully pink and the subject dissolved into the stage behind her.
// Below this the snap fades out and the colour stays where the render put it.
const HUE_HOLD_LO: f32 = 0.05;
const HUE_HOLD_HI: f32 = 0.18;
const DITHER_LO: f32 = 0.02;      // variation below which nothing is dithered
const DITHER_HI: f32 = 0.10;      // and above which it is dithered fully

// —— Legibility ——
// SHARPEN, against the four neighbouring CELLS — the resolution the art is drawn
// at, not the pixels inside one cell, or it sharpens detail the grid is about to
// throw away. Gentler than it was: an unsharp halo that used to hide inside a
// gradient is a visible ring once the gradient is four flat steps.
const SHARPEN: f32 = 0.35;
// HOW READILY A DARK ACCENT CLAIMS ITS PIXEL. Eyes, nostrils, the line of a
// mouth: a few cells of near-black inside a lot of skin, so by area the skin
// owns them and they wash out. An artist draws those pixels dark deliberately,
// and pixel art without them reads as a blurred face.
//
// Tilting the whole owner test toward dark would fix the eyes and dim every
// bright thin line in the same stroke. But the two are told apart easily: an
// eye's dark end sits FAR below its cell's mean, while a grid line's dark end is
// just the ordinary floor of the picture. So the tilt is scaled by how deep the
// dark end actually is, and a bright line barely feels it.
const DARK_BIAS: f32 = 0.26;      // 0 is pure area, higher lets dark accents survive

// —— Styling ——
const CONTRAST: f32 = 1.06;       // S-curve before snapping. 1 leaves values alone
const HUE_SHIFT: f32 = 0.35;      // shadows cool and lights warm, as painted art does
const COOL: vec3f = vec3f(0.86, 0.92, 1.12);
const WARM: vec3f = vec3f(1.10, 1.03, 0.90);

// —— The outline ——
// THREE SOURCES, because a form ends in three different ways and only one of
// them is visible to colour alone. All three are then masked to the cast, which
// is what keeps a floor grid and a horizon out of the ink — see ebIsCast.
//
// COVERAGE falls away where the scene stops being drawn at all. It is the only
// test the earlier version had, and on a scene with a floor and a backdrop it
// fires nowhere: every cell is geometry, so the subject stood un-inked against
// the stage and the picture read as a mosaic.
//
// DEPTH is what says "this form is in front of that one", and it is measured as
// a CURVATURE OF INVERSE DEPTH. 1/z is exactly affine across a flat surface in
// screen space, whatever its slope — so a floor stretching to the horizon has
// zero curvature and never inks, while a step to something behind it spikes.
// Signed, and only the positive side fires, so the ink lands on the NEARER cell:
// one line, on the inside of the form, the way it is drawn.
//
// COLOUR is the seam between two materials that touch — a jacket over a skirt.
// Compared as CHROMATICITY, colour with its brightness divided out, so a shading
// band on one material is not a seam and a bright line on a floor is not one
// either. Two further tests keep it off things that are light rather than form:
// the boundary must still be there TWO cells out (a spark, a specular hit and a
// grid line have all closed up again by then; a garment has not), and the
// brighter side must not be blown out.
const EDGE_COVER: f32 = 0.26;     // coverage drop outward that counts as a silhouette
const EDGE_DEPTH: f32 = 0.03;     // inverse-depth curvature that counts as a step
const EDGE_COLOR: f32 = 0.13;     // chromaticity difference that counts as a seam
const EDGE_HI_LO: f32 = 0.78;     // brightness above which a neighbour is light, not form
const EDGE_HI_HI: f32 = 0.95;
// THE INK, and it is a colour rather than a percentage. Darkening a cell by a
// fraction does almost nothing to black shorts and turns pale skin grey, so the
// same rim reads as a line in one place and as smudge in another. Pixel art inks
// a form in a DEFINITE dark, tinted toward the form so it still belongs to it —
// which is what INK_TINT balances: 0 is a flat near-black, 1 is purely a darker
// version of whatever the cell already was.
const INK: vec3f = vec3f(0.05, 0.04, 0.08);
const INK_TINT: f32 = 0.45;

/** The classic 4x4 ordered (Bayer) matrix, centred on zero. A var rather than a
 *  const because WGSL only allows a dynamic index into a function-scope array. */
fn ebBayer(p: vec2f) -> f32 {
  var m = array<f32, 16>(
    0.0, 8.0, 2.0, 10.0,
    12.0, 4.0, 14.0, 6.0,
    3.0, 11.0, 1.0, 9.0,
    15.0, 7.0, 13.0, 5.0,
  );
  let x = i32(p.x) & 3;
  let y = i32(p.y) & 3;
  return m[y * 4 + x] / 16.0 - 0.46875;
}

fn ebLuma(c: vec3f) -> f32 { return dot(c, vec3f(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722)); }

/** The largest of three, for reading a colour difference as one number. */
fn ebMaxC(c: vec3f) -> f32 { return max(c.x, max(c.y, c.z)); }

/** Colour with its brightness divided out. Two cells of the same material under
 *  different light agree here; two materials do not. The floor keeps a black
 *  cell from dividing into noise. */
fn ebChroma(c: vec3f) -> vec3f { return c / max(ebLuma(c), 0.08); }

/** One cell, point-sampled: display colour and coverage. For the neighbour reads
 *  the outline needs — the cell being drawn gets the filter below instead. */
fn ebPeek(at: vec2f) -> vec4f {
  return vec4f(rzSceneDisplay(at), clamp(rzSceneAlpha(at), 0.0, 1.0));
}

/** Inverse depth. Where nothing was drawn this goes to nearly zero rather than
 *  to infinity, which is what makes an empty neighbour read as "very far" in
 *  every comparison below instead of as a division by zero. */
fn ebInvDepth(at: vec2f) -> f32 { return 1.0 / max(rzSceneDepth(at), 1e-3); }

/**
 * Is a MODEL drawn here, as opposed to the set it stands on?
 *
 * Asked of the id attachment rather than guessed at, because every cheap proxy
 * for it is wrong somewhere: depth says a riser is a form, colour says a floor
 * grid is a seam, and coverage says the whole frame is one object. The ids are
 * the scene's own answer, and comparing against the CAST is what excludes the
 * ground, the backdrop and any prop while including every model on stage.
 *
 * NOT FREE, and worth saying plainly: naming rzObjectAt anywhere in this file is
 * what makes the scene pass STORE its id attachment — a few tens of megabytes a
 * frame at 1080p that a scene without an id reader never pays. This effect is
 * already the expensive kind, and an outline that lands only where it is meant
 * to is what the whole look rests on.
 */
fn ebIsCast(at: vec2f) -> bool {
  let o = rzObjectAt(at);
  if (o == 0u) { return false; }
  let n = rzSubjectCount();
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
    if (o == rzSubjectId(i)) { return true; }
  }
  return false;
}

fn ebRgb2Hsv(c: vec3f) -> vec3f {
  let K = vec4f(0.0, -1.0 / 3.0, 2.0 / 3.0, -1.0);
  let p = select(vec4f(c.bg, K.wz), vec4f(c.gb, K.xy), c.g >= c.b);
  let q = select(vec4f(p.xyw, c.r), vec4f(c.r, p.yzx), c.r >= p.x);
  let d = q.x - min(q.w, q.y);
  return vec3f(abs(q.z + (q.w - q.y) / (6.0 * d + 1e-10)), d / (q.x + 1e-10), q.x);
}

fn ebHsv2Rgb(c: vec3f) -> vec3f {
  let p = abs(fract(vec3f(c.x) + vec3f(1.0, 2.0 / 3.0, 1.0 / 3.0)) * 6.0 - vec3f(3.0));
  return c.z * mix(vec3f(1.0), clamp(p - vec3f(1.0), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0)), c.y);
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let aspect = res.x / max(res.y, 1.0);
  // SQUARE PIXELS: the row count is fixed and the columns follow from the frame's
  // shape, so a pixel is square at any aspect and the same size at 4K as in the
  // preview.
  let rows = max(floor(params.PIXELS), 8.0);
  let grid = vec2f(max(rows * aspect, 1.0), rows);
  let ij = floor(uv * grid);
  let c0 = (ij + vec2f(0.5)) / grid;

  // —— The pixel: its average, and its most distinctive sample ——
  var sum = vec3f(0.0);
  var cov = 0.0;
  var hiC = vec3f(0.0);
  var loC = vec3f(1.0);
  var hiL = -1.0;
  var loL = 2.0;
  // The coverage's own range across the cell, which is what tells an EDGE from a
  // see-through surface further down.
  var aLo = 1.0;
  var aHi = 0.0;
  // And how much of the cell is CAST rather than set — counted, not asked once.
  var castCov = 0.0;
  // One tap per screen pixel the cell spans, so the filter reads the same
  // fraction of the cell whatever it is being drawn into.
  let cellPx = res.y / max(grid.y, 1.0);
  let n = clamp(i32(ceil(cellPx)), SAMPLE_MIN, SAMPLE_CAP);
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
    for (var j = 0; j < n; j = j + 1) {
      let o = (vec2f(f32(i), f32(j)) + vec2f(0.5)) / f32(n) - vec2f(0.5);
      let at = (ij + vec2f(0.5) + o) / grid;
      castCov = castCov + select(0.0, 1.0, ebIsCast(at));
      let s = ebPeek(at);
      sum = sum + s.rgb * s.a;
      cov = cov + s.a;
      aLo = min(aLo, s.a);
      aHi = max(aHi, s.a);
      let l = ebLuma(s.rgb) * s.a;
      if (l > hiL) { hiL = l; hiC = s.rgb; }
      if (l < loL) { loL = l; loC = s.rgb; }
    }
  }
  let inv = 1.0 / f32(n * n);
  let cover = cov * inv;
  let castCover = castCov * inv;
  let mean = sum * inv / max(cover, 1e-6);
  let meanL = ebLuma(mean);
  // WHICH COLOUR OWNS THIS PIXEL, not which is most extreme.
  //
  // Taking the extreme was wrong and thin bright lines showed it: any cell merely
  // GRAZED by one went fully bright, so a one-pixel line bloomed to fill a cell,
  // and because such a line runs diagonally in perspective the cells it grazes
  // alternate — a dashed, blobby mess.
  //
  // Where the mean sits between the two extremes says how much of the cell each
  // one covers: a mean near the dark end means the bright sample is a sliver.
  // So the pixel takes whichever actually owns it, crossed over smoothly so a
  // cell hovering at the boundary does not flicker between them frame to frame.
  let frac = clamp((meanL - loL) / max(hiL - loL, 1e-5), 0.0, 1.0);
  // How far below the cell's own mean its dark end reaches, as a share of that
  // mean. Near 1 for an eye on skin; small for a white line on a mid ground,
  // whose dark end is simply the ground.
  let deep = clamp((meanL - loL) / max(meanL, 1e-3), 0.0, 1.0);
  let bias = DARK_BIAS * deep;
  // A WIDE crossover, and that width is the difference between a line that sits
  // still and one that flashes. A cell holding a thin line lands near the middle
  // of this range, and the camera moving a hair walks it back and forth across
  // the boundary — with a narrow crossover that is a hard flip every frame. Wide,
  // it drifts through the mean instead, which is stable because the mean is what
  // an average is.
  let owns = mix(loC, hiC, smoothstep(0.22 + bias, 0.78 + bias, frac));
  var col = mix(mean, owns, params.PUNCH);
  // How much this cell is CHANGING. The dither is applied only where it is not flat.
  let spread = hiL - loL;

  // —— The neighbourhood, on the cell grid ——
  // Sampled at cell CENTRES, so every test below is asked at the resolution the
  // art is drawn at and a line lands on a pixel boundary rather than across one.
  let e = vec2f(1.0, 0.0) / grid;
  let f = vec2f(0.0, 1.0) / grid;
  let pR = ebPeek(c0 + e);
  let pL = ebPeek(c0 - e);
  let pU = ebPeek(c0 + f);
  let pD = ebPeek(c0 - f);
  // And one cell FURTHER, colour only — the "is the other side still there" test.
  let fR = rzSceneDisplay(c0 + e * 2.0);
  let fL = rzSceneDisplay(c0 - e * 2.0);
  let fU = rzSceneDisplay(c0 + f * 2.0);
  let fD = rzSceneDisplay(c0 - f * 2.0);

  col = clamp(col + (col - (pR.rgb + pL.rgb + pU.rgb + pD.rgb) * 0.25) * SHARPEN,
              vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));

  // —— Styling, before the snap so a lift lands ON a palette step rather than
  // between two of them ——
  let grey = ebLuma(col);
  col = clamp(vec3f(grey) + (col - vec3f(grey)) * params.SAT, vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));
  col = clamp((col - vec3f(0.5)) * CONTRAST + vec3f(0.5), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));
  let tint = mix(COOL, WARM, smoothstep(0.12, 0.88, ebLuma(col)));
  col = clamp(col * mix(vec3f(1.0), tint, HUE_SHIFT), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));

  // —— The palette ——
  var hsv = ebRgb2Hsv(col);
  // DITHERED ONLY WHERE IT VARIES, and only in VALUE. On a flat fill the matrix
  // would chequer forever; banding is what it is for, and a flat fill has none.
  let steps = max(params.LEVELS, 2.0);
  let amount = params.DITHER * smoothstep(DITHER_LO, DITHER_HI, spread);
  hsv.z = hsv.z + ebBayer(ij) * amount / steps;
  // The hue snap fades out on near-greys — see HUE_HOLD_LO. Taken as a signed
  // step around the circle rather than as a mix of two numbers, because 0.98 and
  // 0.02 are neighbours on a wheel and their average is the colour opposite both.
  let hold = smoothstep(HUE_HOLD_LO, HUE_HOLD_HI, hsv.y);
  let dh = fract(floor(hsv.x * HUES + 0.5) / HUES - hsv.x + 0.5) - 0.5;
  col = ebHsv2Rgb(vec3f(
    hsv.x + dh * hold,
    floor(hsv.y * SATS + 0.5) / SATS,
    clamp(floor(hsv.z * steps + 0.5) / steps, 0.0, 1.0)));

  // —— The outline ——
  // HOW FAR COVERAGE FALLS GOING OUTWARD, not how much it differs. The absolute
  // difference fires on both sides of an edge and on any antialiased wobble
  // along it, which is what made an early rim patchy; the drop to the emptiest
  // neighbour fires once, on the inside, where the ink belongs.
  let minA = min(min(pR.a, pL.a), min(pU.a, pD.a));
  let rim = smoothstep(EDGE_COVER, EDGE_COVER * 2.0, clamp(cover - minA, 0.0, 1.0))
          * smoothstep(0.35, 0.7, cover);
  // Curvature of inverse depth, per axis, signed so only the nearer side inks.
  let w0 = ebInvDepth(c0);
  let cx = w0 - (ebInvDepth(c0 + e) + ebInvDepth(c0 - e)) * 0.5;
  let cy = w0 - (ebInvDepth(c0 + f) + ebInvDepth(c0 - f)) * 0.5;
  let ledge = smoothstep(EDGE_DEPTH, EDGE_DEPTH * 2.5, max(cx, cy) / max(w0, 1e-6));
  // A seam: a chromaticity difference that is still there one cell further out.
  let ch = ebChroma(mean);
  let sR = min(ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(pR.rgb))), ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(fR))));
  let sL = min(ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(pL.rgb))), ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(fL))));
  let sU = min(ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(pU.rgb))), ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(fU))));
  let sD = min(ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(pD.rgb))), ebMaxC(abs(ch - ebChroma(fD))));
  let seam = max(max(sR, sL), max(sU, sD));
  let lightest = max(max(ebLuma(pR.rgb), ebLuma(pL.rgb)),
                     max(ebLuma(pU.rgb), ebLuma(pD.rgb)));
  let inner = smoothstep(EDGE_COLOR, EDGE_COLOR * 1.7, seam)
            // the darker side of the seam takes the ink, so the line is drawn once
            * smoothstep(0.0, 0.06, lightest - meanL)
            // and light is not form: a blown-out neighbour is a highlight
            * (1.0 - smoothstep(EDGE_HI_LO, EDGE_HI_HI, lightest));
  // AND ALL THREE ARE MASKED TO THE CAST — BY COVERAGE, NOT BY ONE QUESTION.
  //
  // Asking ebIsCast once, at the cell centre, is what made the outlines FLASH
  // while she moved, and the reason is in the id attachment's own contract: it is
  // deliberately left unresolved, because an averaged id belongs to nothing. So
  // sample 0 draws a hard, aliased silhouette — a DIFFERENT edge from the one she
  // is rendered with — and a cell sitting on it answers yes or no depending on
  // where one subsample landed this frame. Depth and colour meanwhile move
  // smoothly. The mask disagreed with the ink's own source at exactly the cells
  // the ink lives on, and full ink flipped to none between frames.
  //
  // Counting the taps recovers what the single sample cannot. Every one of the
  // cell's colour taps asks the question too, so the mask arrives as a FRACTION
  // at the same resolution as the coverage beside it, and sub-pixel movement
  // shifts it by a tap rather than flipping it. This is the selection pass's own
  // fix — it counts MSAA samples to recover coverage — done across the cell,
  // which is the only axis an effect can reach.
  //
  // The ramp saturates early on purpose: a cell half full of her is a SILHOUETTE
  // cell, which is where the ink matters most and must not arrive at half
  // strength.
  let allow = mix(params.STAGE_INK, 1.0, smoothstep(0.06, 0.45, castCover));
  let darken = clamp(max(max(rim, ledge), inner) * params.OUTLINE * allow, 0.0, 1.0);
  let ink = mix(INK, col * 0.28, INK_TINT);
  col = mix(col, ink, darken);

  // —— Over the background ——
  // COVERAGE HARDENED AT AN EDGE, AND ONLY THERE.
  //
  // A pixel belongs to one thing: half a cell of subject blended with half a cell
  // of backdrop is a colour the palette does not hold, and a row of them is the
  // soft fringe that says "resized photograph" rather than "sprite".
  //
  // BUT PARTIAL COVERAGE HAS A SECOND CAUSE, and hardening that one is a bug this
  // effect shipped with for a version. A surface can be genuinely see-through,
  // and the ground is exactly that: it fades to nothing at its rim, so its whole
  // outer band sits at a low alpha honestly rather than because a silhouette
  // crosses it. Squaring that band up erased every cell of it under the
  // threshold, and the floor came back visibly smaller than the scene had drawn.
  //
  // The two are told apart INSIDE the cell. A silhouette puts samples at 1 and at
  // 0 in the same cell; a translucent surface puts samples that agree with each
  // other at whatever level it is drawn at. So the hardening is scaled by how far
  // the coverage disagrees with itself, and a smooth fade keeps the alpha the
  // scene gave it.
  let edgy = smoothstep(0.15, 0.45, aHi - aLo);
  let a = mix(cover, smoothstep(0.35, 0.65, cover), edgy);
  let bg = rzBackground();
  let solid = bg.w > 0.5;
  col = select(col, col * a + bg.rgb * (1.0 - a), solid);
  return vec4f(clamp(col, vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0)), select(a, 1.0, solid));
}

#anchor 首
#anchor 上半身2
#anchor 下半身
#anchor 左足
#anchor 右足

#param float CELL 16 6 120 — a block's size, in pixels of a 1440-line frame
#param float CHEST 1 0 2 — the chest patch, against her torso. 0 takes it off
#param float HIP 1 0 2 — the hip patch, against her torso. 0 takes it off
#param float SHADE 0.12 0 0.4 — how far one block may differ from the next, so a patch over white or black still shows its blocks

// Mosaic — the censor blocks a phone's photo editor paints, over her chest and
// her hips.
//
// WHAT MAKES IT READ AS THAT TOOL, and it is three things:
//
// 1. FLAT SQUARE BLOCKS, each the plain average of the picture under it. No
//    grout between them, no blur, no rounding — a block is one colour.
//
// 2. THE GRID STAYS ON THE SCREEN. Only the patch moves with her; the blocks it
//    is made of are fixed to the frame, so as she dances, blocks switch on and
//    off at the patch's edge rather than the whole grid sliding and turning
//    with her body. That is what every editor and every broadcast censor does,
//    and a grid riding the body shimmers.
//
// 3. EVERY BLOCK IS A LITTLE ITS OWN SHADE. An average over flat white skin is
//    white, and so is the next block's: the patch went blank exactly where it
//    was covering the most. So each block takes a fixed nudge of its own, away
//    from the end of the range it is near — a bright block darkens, a dark one
//    lifts — and white blocks read as blocks. Fixed per block of the grid, so a
//    block keeps its shade while she moves under it rather than flickering.
//
// 4. THE EDGE IS WHOLE BLOCKS. A block is in the patch when its centre is, so
//    the outline is a staircase on the grid — never a soft oval, never a block
//    cut in half.
//
// A FILTER: it reads rzSceneFrame, so it runs after every other field effect
// and pixelates the finished picture — the background behind her, a glow, a
// caption — exactly as an editor pixelates the photo it is handed.
//
// THE BLOCK IS COUNTED IN FRAME HEIGHT, not in device pixels, so the export is
// the preview enlarged: 16 at 1440 lines is 24 pixels at 4K.
//
// THE PATCH IS SIZED BY HER TORSO, neck to waist, at her distance — so it
// covers the same part of her close up and across the stage. Its block size
// does not follow her: a censor's blocks are the frame's, and a far dancer
// gets a patch only a few blocks wide, which is how it looks on a phone too.

// —— Where the patches sit, in torso lengths (neck to waist) ——
// The chest: this far from 上半身2 toward the neck, and pushed out in front of
// the spine, where the chest actually is rather than where the bone runs.
const CHEST_T: f32 = 0.25;
const CHEST_FWD: f32 = 0.12;
// Rigs without 上半身2 take the chest off the neck-to-waist chord instead.
const CHEST_T_CHORD: f32 = 0.60;
// The hips: between the two leg joints, and a little below them — the crotch
// sits under the hip sockets, not between them.
const HIP_DROP: f32 = 0.10;
const HIP_FWD: f32 = 0.02;

// —— How big, in torso lengths, as half-extents (across, along the body) ——
const CHEST_HALF: vec2f = vec2f(0.224, 0.112);
const HIP_HALF: vec2f = vec2f(0.158, 0.112);

// Most taps per axis inside a block — 64 a block at most, and only blocks in a
// patch pay anything.
const TAP_CAP: i32 = 8;

/** Is the block centred at px (screen pixels) inside a patch centred on world
 *  point c, at half-extents `half` torso lengths along her body's own up and
 *  across it? `up` is her body's up, so a dancer lying down gets a patch
 *  lying down too. */
fn mzInPatch(px: vec2f, res: vec2f, c: vec3f, up: vec3f, torso: f32, half: vec2f) -> bool {
  let p = rzProject(c);
  if (p.z <= 0.05) { return false; }
  // Pixels per world unit at her distance, measured up the screen.
  let q = rzProject(c + rzCameraUp() * torso);
  let scale = abs(q.y - p.y) * res.y / torso;
  // Her up, as the screen sees it — only its direction, so a camera looking
  // down on her does not flatten the patch.
  let u2 = rzProject(c + up * torso * 0.25).xy - p.xy;
  var a = abs(normalize(vec2f(u2.x * res.x, u2.y * res.y) + vec2f(0.0, 1e-6)));
  // A patch on the screen's own axes, its sides swapped as she turns from
  // upright to lying: the blocks never rotate, so neither does what they fill.
  let h = vec2f(half.x * a.y + half.y * a.x, half.y * a.y + half.x * a.x) * torso * scale;
  let d = abs(px - p.xy * res);
  return d.x <= h.x && d.y <= h.y;
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let cell = max(params.CELL * res.y / 1440.0, 2.0);
  let ij = floor(uv * res / cell);
  let centre = (ij + vec2f(0.5)) * cell;

  var hit = false;
  let n = rzSubjectCount();
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
    if (hit) { break; }
    let s = rzSubject(i);
    // Not on this effect, or dissolved away entirely.
    if (!s.valid || s.dissolve <= 0.01) { continue; }
    let neck = rzAnchor(i, 0);
    let chest = rzAnchor(i, 1);
    let waist = rzAnchor(i, 2);
    let legL = rzAnchor(i, 3);
    let legR = rzAnchor(i, 4);
    if (!neck.valid || !waist.valid) { continue; }
    let spine = neck.pos - waist.pos;
    let torso = length(spine);
    if (torso < 1e-4) { continue; }
    let up = spine / torso;
    // Her front, from the line across her hips and her up. MMD faces -Z with
    // her left at +X, and up x left = +Y x +X = -Z: in front of her.
    var fwd = vec3f(0.0);
    if (legL.valid && legR.valid) {
      let across = legL.pos - legR.pos;
      let f = cross(up, across);
      if (length(f) > 1e-5) { fwd = normalize(f); }
    }

    if (params.CHEST > 0.0) {
      var c = mix(waist.pos, neck.pos, CHEST_T_CHORD);
      if (chest.valid) { c = mix(chest.pos, neck.pos, CHEST_T); }
      c = c + fwd * CHEST_FWD * torso;
      if (mzInPatch(centre, res, c, up, torso, CHEST_HALF * params.CHEST)) { hit = true; }
    }
    if (!hit && params.HIP > 0.0) {
      var c = waist.pos - up * (0.25 + HIP_DROP) * torso;
      if (legL.valid && legR.valid) { c = (legL.pos + legR.pos) * 0.5 - up * HIP_DROP * torso; }
      c = c + fwd * HIP_FWD * torso;
      if (mzInPatch(centre, res, c, up, torso, HIP_HALF * params.HIP)) { hit = true; }
    }
  }
  if (!hit) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // The block's average: one tap per screen pixel it spans, up to the cap, so
  // the same block averages the same picture in the preview and at 4K.
  let taps = clamp(i32(ceil(cell)), 2, TAP_CAP);
  var sum = vec3f(0.0);
  for (var y = 0; y < taps; y = y + 1) {
    for (var x = 0; x < taps; x = x + 1) {
      let o = (vec2f(f32(x), f32(y)) + vec2f(0.5)) / f32(taps);
      sum = sum + rzSceneFrame((ij + o) * cell / res);
    }
  }
  let avg = clamp(sum / f32(taps * taps), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));
  // The block's own shade: toward black if it is bright, toward white if it
  // is dark, by an amount drawn from where the block sits on the grid.
  let luma = dot(avg, vec3f(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));
  let away = select(vec3f(1.0), vec3f(0.0), luma > 0.5);
  let nudge = fract(sin(dot(ij, vec2f(12.9898, 78.233))) * 43758.5453);
  return vec4f(mix(avg, away, nudge * clamp(params.SHADE, 0.0, 1.0)), 1.0);
}

#param float SIZE 0.72 0.3 1.2 — how much of the frame's height the card stands
#param float TILT 1.0 0 3 — how far it swings, 1 = the studio's own ±22°, 3 = near edge-on
#param float SPIN 1.0 0 6 — how fast it turns
#param float LENS 1.0 0 2 — how close the eye stands: 0 flat, 1 pokemon-cards-css's perspective
#param float ZOOM 1.00 0.4 2 — how much of the shot the card holds
#param float FOIL 0.65 0 1.2 — 镭射强度, the studio's own dial
#param float STARS 1.0 0 2 — the Voronoi star flecks
#param float GLOW 1.0 0 2.5 — the light off her outline, where the sweep passes
#param float HALO 1.0 0 3 — the light the card throws into the dark

// Holo Card — the shot, as it is, on a holographic trading card turning in the
// dark.
//
// THE PICTURE IS NOT TOUCHED. Earlier versions split the frame into a subject
// and a painted backdrop at different parallax depths, after holo-card-studio's
// four image layers. Here there is one layer — the finished frame the scene
// rendered, cropped to the card, at depth zero. Two things went wrong with the
// split and both are structural: two lookups of one picture at two offsets put
// a ghost of the scene beside itself the moment the floor was let through, and
// a painted backdrop was replacing pixels the scene had drawn. A card laid over
// the shot cannot do either.
//
// What IS the studio's, formula for formula (assets/web-template/app.js):
//
//   A PINHOLE EYE, where the studio's camera is orthographic. Under
//   orthographic projection a turned card only narrows — its far edge stays as
//   tall as its near one — and a squeezed picture is what it reads as.
//   pokemon-cards-css puts its eye at perspective: 600px, about 1.17 card
//   heights away, and that keystone is what makes a tilt read as the card
//   facing somewhere. LENS 0 sends the eye back to the studio's flat view.
//   THE FOIL PHASE FOLLOWS THE VIEW. Bands at uv + view.xy · 2.4, overlaid at
//   the studio's strength, so turning the card sweeps them.
//   THE SWEEP. A highlight band crossing the card as it turns — and the only
//   thing that lights her outline (line × sweep × .22), so the glow moves with
//   the card instead of sitting on her as a rim.
//   THE FRAME IS TWO RINGS. A holographic ring at the cut (.060 on a 6.3-wide
//   card), a gap, then a gold hairline (.018). The thickness edge is the
//   studio's edgeFragment.
//   STARS. Voronoi distance-to-edge at 105, sparse, twinkling with the view.
//
// The bloom the studio adds in post is a no-op on a field effect, so the light
// the card throws is drawn: a bounded falloff off the cut into the black, in
// the foil's own colour, and no more.

const CARD_W = 6.3;        // the studio's stock, in its units
const CARD_H = 9.45;
const CORNER = 0.20;
const THICK = 0.045;
const RING_HOLO = 0.060;   // outer ring, at the cut
const RING_GAP = 0.025;
const RING_GOLD = 0.018;   // the hairline: ring('内圈 · 古金细边', 6.13, 9.28, .018)
const GOLD = vec3f(0.749, 0.635, 0.420);       // 0xbfa26b, MeshBasicMaterial
const EDGE_GOLD = vec3f(0.55, 0.34, 0.10);     // edgeFragment's base
const LINE_TINT = vec3f(1.0, 0.94, 0.78);
const STAR_TINT = vec3f(0.66, 0.86, 1.0);
const ROOM = vec3f(0.006, 0.007, 0.011);       // the page is #000; a hair above it
const HALO_R = 0.11;                            // how far the spill reaches, in card widths

fn holoHash(p: vec2f) -> f32 {
  return fract(sin(dot(p, vec2f(127.1, 311.7))) * 43758.5453);
}

/** app.js noise(): value noise with a smooth fade. */
fn holoNoise(p: vec2f) -> f32 {
  let i = floor(p);
  var f = fract(p);
  f = f * f * (3.0 - 2.0 * f);
  return mix(mix(holoHash(i), holoHash(i + vec2f(1.0, 0.0)), f.x),
             mix(holoHash(i + vec2f(0.0, 1.0)), holoHash(i + vec2f(1.0, 1.0)), f.x), f.y);
}

/** app.js spectrum(): pink → yellow → blue → white. */
fn holoSpectrum(x: f32) -> vec3f {
  let t = fract(x);
  let pink = vec3f(1.0, 0.32, 0.62);
  let yellow = vec3f(1.0, 0.85, 0.32);
  let blue = vec3f(0.22, 0.62, 1.0);
  if (t < 0.35) { return mix(pink, yellow, t / 0.35); }
  if (t < 0.7) { return mix(yellow, blue, (t - 0.35) / 0.35); }
  return mix(blue, vec3f(1.0), (t - 0.7) / 0.3);
}

/** app.js overlay(). */
fn holoOverlay(b: vec3f, f: vec3f) -> vec3f {
  return mix(2.0 * b * f, 1.0 - 2.0 * (1.0 - b) * (1.0 - f), step(vec3f(0.5), b));
}

/** app.js wave(): bands at 32°, scale .55, distortion 7, phase riding the view. */
fn holoWave(p: vec2f, view: vec3f) -> f32 {
  let a = p + view.xy * 2.4;
  return 0.5 + 0.5 * sin((a.x * 0.848 - a.y * 0.530) * 6.283 * 0.55 + 7.0 * holoNoise(a * 1.5));
}

/** app.js star(): Voronoi distance-to-edge at 105, sparse, twinkling with the view. */
fn holoStar(p: vec2f, t: f32, view: vec3f) -> f32 {
  let q = p * 105.0;
  let id = floor(q);
  let f = fract(q);
  var first = 9.0;
  var second = 9.0;
  for (var y = -1; y <= 1; y = y + 1) {
    for (var x = -1; x <= 1; x = x + 1) {
      let g = vec2f(f32(x), f32(y));
      let o = vec2f(holoHash(id + g), holoHash(id + g + vec2f(43.3)));
      let d = length(g + o - f);
      if (d < first) { second = first; first = d; } else { second = min(second, d); }
    }
  }
  let edge = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.01, 0.035, second - first);
  let sparse = step(0.90, holoHash(id + vec2f(8.8)));
  let twinkle = pow(0.5 + 0.5 * sin(t * 1.8 + holoHash(id) * 30.0 + view.x * 27.0 + view.y * 21.0), 6.0);
  return edge * sparse * twinkle;
}

/** Signed distance to the rounded stock, negative inside. */
fn holoStock(cu: f32, cv: f32, w: f32, h: f32, r: f32) -> f32 {
  let q = abs(vec2f(cu, cv)) - (vec2f(w, h) * 0.5 - vec2f(r));
  return length(max(q, vec2f(0.0))) + min(max(q.x, q.y), 0.0) - r;
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let aspect = res.x / max(res.y, 1.0);
  // The eye sits on the card's axis, dist in front of it, and the frame's
  // half-height meets the card's plane at k — so the card stands SIZE of the
  // frame at rest whatever dist is, and dist alone decides how much the far
  // side shrinks. LENS 1 is pokemon-cards-css's perspective: 600px on a card
  // 511px tall; LENS 0 sends the eye to infinity, the studio's camera.
  let s = (uv - vec2f(0.5)) * 2.0 * vec2f(aspect, 1.0);
  let k = CARD_H * 0.5 / max(params.SIZE, 1.0e-3);
  let dist = CARD_H * 1.17 / max(params.LENS, 1.0e-3);
  let eye = vec3f(0.0, 0.0, -dist);
  let rd = normalize(vec3f(s * k, dist));

  // The idle motion is pokemon-cards-css's showcase cone — yaw and pitch on
  // ONE rate, a quarter turn apart — at the studio's amplitudes and pace. The
  // studio runs them on two rates (.65 and .85), which pairs each yaw extreme
  // with a different pitch, so its two sides never quite match; on a cone the
  // card reaches full left and full right level, and they mirror.
  let t = time * params.SPIN * 0.65;
  let yaw = sin(t) * 0.38 * params.TILT;
  let pit = cos(t) * 0.12 * params.TILT;
  let cy = cos(yaw); let sy = sin(yaw);
  let cp = cos(pit); let sp = sin(pit);
  // Normal toward the eye (−z at rest); right and up complete the frame.
  let n = vec3f(-sy * cp, sp, -cy * cp);
  let U = vec3f(cy, 0.0, -sy);
  let V = vec3f(sy * sp, cp, cy * sp);
  // The card's centre is the origin.
  let dn = dot(rd, n);
  var facing = dn < -1.0e-4;
  let tHit = select(0.0, dot(-eye, n) / dn, facing);
  facing = facing && tHit > 0.0;
  let hit = eye + rd * tHit;
  let cu = dot(hit, U);
  let cv = dot(hit, V);
  // The eye from this point of the face, in the card's frame — the studio's
  // uView, which its orthographic camera made one vector for the whole card.
  // From a pinhole it differs across the face, the way the pointer does in
  // pokemon-cards-css, and the foil follows it pixel by pixel.
  let toEye = normalize(eye - hit);
  let view = vec3f(dot(toEye, U), dot(toEye, V), dot(toEye, n));
  let sd = holoStock(cu, cv, CARD_W, CARD_H, CORNER);
  let inside = facing && sd < 0.0;
  let inset = max(-sd, 0.0);

  // The back of the stock, one thickness behind: where the ray misses the
  // front and meets it, this pixel is looking at the card's edge.
  let backC = -n * THICK;
  let tB = select(0.0, dot(backC - eye, n) / dn, facing);
  let hitB = eye + rd * tB - backC;
  let sdB = holoStock(dot(hitB, U), dot(hitB, V), CARD_W, CARD_H, CORNER);
  let onEdge = facing && sd >= 0.0 && sdB < 0.0;

  // Card uv, 0..1 across the stock, v up. Then the shot, fitted to the card
  // without squeezing: a card is portrait and the frame is landscape, so
  // matching aspects crops the sides. That crop and nothing else — the picture
  // sits on the card at depth zero.
  let cardUv = vec2f(cu / CARD_W, cv / CARD_H) + vec2f(0.5);
  let fit = vec2f((CARD_W / CARD_H) / max(aspect, 1.0e-3), 1.0) / max(params.ZOOM, 1.0e-3);
  let sceneUv = clamp(vec2f(0.5) + (cardUv - vec2f(0.5)) * fit, vec2f(0.001), vec2f(0.999));

  // The finished frame — cast, stage, background, exactly as rendered.
  var col = rzSceneFrame(sceneUv);

  // Where she is. rzCastDistance answers in screen pixels and goes negative
  // inside the cast, so it is both her mask and her outline.
  let ld = rzCastDistance(sceneUv);
  let onHer = clamp(-ld / 1.5, 0.0, 1.0);

  // ── The foil, the studio's chain ────────────────────────────────────────
  // Overlaid at .28 on her and .36 on the rest, its two layer strengths.
  let w = holoWave(cardUv, view);
  let foil = holoSpectrum(w * 0.8 + holoNoise(cardUv * 5.0) * 0.12);
  col = mix(col, holoOverlay(col, foil), params.FOIL * mix(0.36, 0.28, onHer));

  // The sweep: a highlight band that crosses the card as it turns.
  let sweep = pow(max(0.0, sin((cardUv.x * 0.83 + cardUv.y * 0.35 + view.x * 1.8 + view.y * 0.9) * 6.283)), 12.0);
  col = col + foil * sweep * params.FOIL * 0.28;

  // Her outline, lit only where the sweep passes: a narrow band about zero
  // is the line — the studio's thresholded lineart, found rather than drawn.
  let line = 1.0 - smoothstep(1.0, 6.0, abs(ld));
  col = col + LINE_TINT * line * sweep * params.FOIL * 0.22 * params.GLOW * 2.2;

  // Star flecks over the picture, kept off the cast so she is not speckled.
  col = col + STAR_TINT * holoStar(cardUv, time, view) * params.FOIL * 0.65 * params.STARS * (1.0 - onHer * 0.7);

  // ── The frame: holographic ring, gap, gold hairline ─────────────────────
  let ringHolo = 1.0 - smoothstep(RING_HOLO, RING_HOLO + 0.012, inset);
  let goldIn = RING_HOLO + RING_GAP;
  let ringGold = smoothstep(goldIn - 0.008, goldIn, inset) * (1.0 - smoothstep(goldIn + RING_GOLD, goldIn + RING_GOLD + 0.008, inset));
  let edgeSpectrum = holoSpectrum(holoWave(cardUv, view));
  let ringCol = mix(EDGE_GOLD, edgeSpectrum, 0.65 + params.FOIL * 0.2) * 0.8 + vec3f(0.14);
  col = mix(col, ringCol, ringHolo);
  col = mix(col, GOLD, ringGold);

  // ── The thickness edge: the studio's edgeFragment ────────────────────────
  let edgeUv = vec2f(clamp(sd / max(sd - sdB, 1.0e-4), 0.0, 1.0), fract((cu + cv) * 0.35));
  let edgeCol = mix(EDGE_GOLD, holoSpectrum(holoWave(edgeUv, view)), 0.65 + params.FOIL * 0.2) * 0.8 + vec3f(0.14);

  // ── The room: the page is black, and the card throws light into it ───────
  // Two falloffs off the cut — a tight bright one and a wide soft one — in the
  // foil's own colour where the card is holographic, so the spill changes hue
  // as the card turns. Both bounded by HALO_R; nothing here reaches the frame.
  let d = max(sdB, 0.0) / (HALO_R * CARD_W);
  let spill = (exp(-d * 6.0) * 0.55 + exp(-d * 1.6) * 0.20) * select(0.0, 1.0, facing);
  let room = ROOM + mix(GOLD, edgeSpectrum, 0.6) * spill * params.HALO * 0.5;

  var out = select(room, edgeCol, onEdge);
  out = select(out, col, inside);
  // Opaque: this replaces the picture rather than laying over it.
  return vec4f(max(out, vec3f(0.0)), 1.0);
}

#anchor 左目
#anchor 右目
#anchor 頭
#lights 6
#layer additive
#param color CORE #fff0ee — white-hot, a shade of pink, the heart of the beam
#param color BEAM #ff1a12 — the red around it
#param float LENGTH 60 5 200 — how far the beams reach, in world units
#param float WIDTH 0.08 0.02 0.5 — the tube's radius, in world units — thin is laser
#param float GLOW 1.0 0 3 — the red halo around the tube, and the star in the eye
#param float LIGHT 4.0 0 10 — the light the beams cast onto the scene
#param float FLARE 1.0 0 3 — the burn where a beam lands
#param float UNDER 0.06 0 0.3 — a beam coming at the camera is tilted down this much, so it passes under the lens: into the lens, a beam is a point
#param float SPREAD 0.12 0 0.5 — the star and the beam start this much outward of the eye bone, toward the iris

// Laser Eyes — Homelander's stare: a tube of light out of each eye, down her
// line of sight, scorching whatever it meets.
//
// THE TUBE IS GEOMETRY. Each pixel's ray is measured against a cylinder along
// the sight line — the distance at closest approach says whether the ray is
// inside the tube, and how near its axis — and that point is depth-tested
// against the scene, so the beam goes INTO the floor and BEHIND an arm rather
// than being painted over them. A field effect cannot bloom, so its glow is
// drawn: a bounded falloff off the tube, and a star in the eye. Real lights
// along the beam put its colour on her and on the scene. The burn is where
// the sight line, marched against the scene's depth, first meets a surface.
//
// THE BEAM GOES WHERE THE FACE POINTS: the head bone's forward. The eyes do
// not steer it — with them on the camera it would come at the lens, and a
// tube seen end-on is a point. The beam leaves at the pupil: the eye bone
// sits at the eyeball's centre, and the pupil is on the eye's axis — its
// local +Z, and an MMD model rests facing -Z, so a bone looks down -fwd,
// the head the same — a little in front.
//
// A BEAM COMING AT THE CAMERA is tilted a little down (UNDER), so it passes
// under the lens and is seen coming — into the lens it is a point, a tube
// seen end-on — and it stops six units before the camera, whatever aimed
// it: past the lens it is nothing, and just before it its tube fills the
// frame.

const EYE_R = 0.45;   // from the bone to the front of the eye, world units

fn laserAxis(a: RzAnchor) -> vec3f {
  return normalize(-a.fwd);
}

/** Where the face points: the head's forward, or the eye's own axis for a
 *  model with no head bone by that name. */
fn laserLook(subject: i32, a: RzAnchor) -> vec3f {
  let head = rzAnchor(subject, 2);
  return select(laserAxis(a), normalize(-head.fwd), head.valid);
}

/** Is this beam coming at the camera: still ahead of it, and passing within
 *  eight units of it at the nearest. */
fn laserAtCamera(origin: vec3f, d: vec3f, cam: vec3f) -> bool {
  let toPlane = dot(cam - origin, d);
  return toPlane > 0.0 && length((cam - origin) - d * toPlane) < 8.0;
}

fn laserDir(subject: i32, a: RzAnchor, cam: vec3f) -> vec3f {
  let look = laserLook(subject, a);
  let origin = a.pos + laserAxis(a) * EYE_R;
  let tilted = normalize(look + vec3f(0.0, -params.UNDER, 0.0));
  return select(look, tilted, laserAtCamera(origin, look, cam));
}

/** Where the beam leaves the eye: on the eye's axis, a little in front of the
 *  bone, and a little outward — away from the other eye — toward the iris. */
fn laserOrigin(subject: i32, eye: i32, a: RzAnchor) -> vec3f {
  let other = rzAnchor(subject, 1 - eye);
  let out = select(vec3f(0.0), normalize(a.pos - other.pos), other.valid);
  return a.pos + laserAxis(a) * EYE_R + out * params.SPREAD;
}

/** A world radius at a point, as a fraction of the frame's height: the
 *  point and a point one radius beside it, projected. No reach into the
 *  camera's own numbers, so the library's preview — which stands in for the
 *  scene with an API of its own — draws this exactly as the scene does. */
fn laserScreenRadius(p: vec3f, world: f32) -> f32 {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let aspect = res.x / max(res.y, 1.0);
  let f = rzCameraForward();
  var side = cross(f, vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 0.0));
  if (length(side) < 1.0e-3) { side = vec3f(1.0, 0.0, 0.0); }
  let a = rzProject(p);
  let b = rzProject(p + normalize(side) * world);
  return length((b.xy - a.xy) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0));
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let aspect = res.x / max(res.y, 1.0);
  let cam = rzCameraPos();
  let rdz = dot(ray, rzCameraForward());   // depth per unit along this ray
  var col = vec3f(0.0);
  var amount = 0.0;
  // LIGHT ADDS. The layer lands OVER the frame, so a colour laid at weight a
  // takes a × (frame − colour) out of every channel it lacks: red rays over a
  // pink backdrop came out as dark streaks. So the frame under this pixel is
  // read (rzSceneFrame — which makes this a filter, drawn after the other
  // field effects and carrying their layers) and what goes back is the frame
  // PLUS the light: the composite can then only brighten. Over footage the
  // tap has no picture to add to, and the light lands over it plain.
  let red = params.BEAM;
  let n = min(rzSubjectCount(), 4);
  let step = params.LENGTH / 40.0;
  // A HIT IS A SURFACE, NOT A THING IN FRONT. A point of the beam whose depth
  // is behind what the frame shows there is either inside a surface or hidden
  // behind something nearer — from behind her, the beam's far half sits
  // behind her hand, and that is not a hit. Only a point a little behind the
  // surface, within a couple of steps of the march, counts.
  let thick = step * 2.0 + 0.5;
  for (var s = 0; s < n; s = s + 1) {
    for (var eye = 0; eye < 2; eye = eye + 1) {
      let a = rzAnchor(s, eye);
      if (!a.valid) { continue; }
      let d = laserDir(s, a, cam);
      let origin = laserOrigin(s, eye, a);
      let pe = rzProject(origin);

      // The eye: a STAR where the beam leaves it — a white-hot point, a red
      // halo, and rays off it, four straight and four on the diagonal, the
      // way a lens draws a point of light; the rays turn slowly. Gone where
      // something nearer hides the eye.
      if (pe.z > 0.05) {
        let toEye = (uv - pe.xy) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0);
        let r = length(toEye);
        let rEye = laserScreenRadius(origin, 0.13 * params.GLOW);
        let rHalo = laserScreenRadius(origin, 0.5 * params.GLOW);
        let rRay = laserScreenRadius(origin, 1.1 * params.GLOW);
        let point = exp(-(r * r) / max(rEye * rEye, 1.0e-8));
        let halo = exp(-(r * r) / max(rHalo * rHalo, 1.0e-8));
        let ang = atan2(toEye.y, toEye.x) + time * 0.35;
        let rays = (pow(max(cos(2.0 * ang), 0.0), 40.0) + 0.6 * pow(max(cos(2.0 * ang + 0.7853982), 0.0), 40.0))
          * exp(-r / max(rRay, 1.0e-8));
        let eyeVisible = select(0.0, 1.0, depth > pe.z - 0.6);
        let star = (point * 1.3 + rays * 1.0) * eyeVisible;
        col = col + mix(red, params.CORE, clamp(point + rays * 0.6, 0.0, 1.0)) * star
          + red * halo * 0.55 * eyeVisible;
        amount = max(amount, max(star, halo * 0.55 * eyeVisible));
      }

      // Where the beam lands: the sight line marched against the scene, for
      // the burn alone — the tube is depth-tested per pixel, which is the
      // occlusion that is actually right, and a march that ended it would
      // end it wrongly wherever a sample fell on hair or a raised hand. A
      // landing is a CROSSING: a sample in front of the surface, then one
      // just behind it. Only pixels near the beam's path on screen pay.
      // Short of the camera, when the beam is coming at it. A beam that
      // merely crosses the camera's depth far off to the side keeps its length.
      let toPlane = dot(cam - origin, d);
      var endS = select(params.LENGTH, min(params.LENGTH, max(toPlane - 6.0, 0.0)), laserAtCamera(origin, d, cam));
      var hitZ = -1.0;
      var hitUv = vec2f(0.0);
      var hitP = vec3f(0.0);
      var nearPath = true;
      if (pe.z > 0.05) {
        let toEye = (uv - pe.xy) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0);
        let axis = (rzProject(origin + d).xy - pe.xy) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0);
        let axisLen = length(axis);
        if (axisLen > 1.0e-5) {
          let along = dot(toEye, axis) / axisLen;
          let off = abs(toEye.x * axis.y - toEye.y * axis.x) / axisLen;
          nearPath = along > -0.3 && off < 0.3;
        }
      }
      if (nearPath) {
        var wasFront = false;
        for (var i = 1; i <= 40; i = i + 1) {
          if (f32(i) * step > endS) { break; }
          let pr = rzProject(origin + d * (f32(i) * step));
          if (pr.z <= 0.05) { continue; }
          if (pr.x < 0.0 || pr.x > 1.0 || pr.y < 0.0 || pr.y > 1.0) { continue; }
          let behind = pr.z - rzSceneDepth(pr.xy);
          if (wasFront && behind > 0.1 && behind < thick) {
            hitZ = pr.z;
            hitUv = pr.xy;
            hitP = origin + d * (f32(i) * step);
            break;
          }
          wasFront = behind < 0.0;
        }
      }

      // The tube. Closest approach between this pixel's ray and the beam's
      // axis: h is the ray's distance from the axis there, sc where along the
      // beam, tc how far along the ray — whose depth is what the scene tests.
      let m = cam - origin;
      let md = dot(m, d);
      let rdd = dot(ray, d);
      let mr = dot(m, ray);
      let a2 = 1.0 - rdd * rdd;
      if (a2 > 1.0e-5) {
        let tc = (md * rdd - mr) / a2;
        let sc = md + tc * rdd;
        let zc = tc * rdz;
        if (tc > 0.0 && sc >= 0.0 && sc <= endS && zc < depth) {
          let h = length(m + ray * tc - d * sc);
          let R = params.WIDTH;
          let reach = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.85, 1.0, sc / params.LENGTH);
          // The rim, a pixel wide at this depth, so the tube's edge is smooth.
          let px = 1.0 / max(laserScreenRadius(cam + ray * tc, 1.0) * res.y, 1.0e-4);
          let rim = 1.0 - smoothstep(R - px, R + px, h);
          // Brightest at the axis, the way a hot cylinder reads.
          let body = sqrt(clamp(1.0 - (h * h) / (R * R), 0.0, 1.0));
          let tube = rim * (0.4 + 0.6 * body) * reach;
          col = col + mix(red, params.CORE, sqrt(body)) * tube;
          // The red halo off the tube: wide, bounded, gone by eight radii —
          // what makes a thin white line read as a laser.
          let glow = exp(-pow(max(h - R, 0.0) / (R * 2.6), 1.6)) * (1.0 - rim) * 0.7 * params.GLOW * reach;
          col = col + red * glow;
          amount = max(amount, max(tube, glow));
        }
      }

      // The burn: a white-hot point with a wide warm bloom, on the surface —
      // gone where something nearer than that surface covers it.
      if (hitZ > 0.0) {
        let toHit = (uv - hitUv) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0);
        let rHot = laserScreenRadius(hitP, 0.35 * params.FLARE);
        let rWarm = laserScreenRadius(hitP, 1.4 * params.FLARE);
        let d2 = dot(toHit, toHit);
        let hot = exp(-d2 / max(rHot * rHot, 1.0e-8));
        let warm = exp(-d2 / max(rWarm * rWarm, 1.0e-8));
        let covered = select(1.0, 0.0, depth < hitZ - 1.0);
        let burn = (hot + warm * 0.35) * covered;
        col = col + mix(red, params.CORE, hot) * burn;
        amount = max(amount, burn);
      }
    }
  }
  let a = clamp(amount, 1.0e-4, 1.0);
  return vec4f(min(rzSceneFrame(uv) + col / a, vec3f(1.0)), a);
}

// Three lights per eye, for the first subject: near, mid, far along the beam.
// Lights see no depth, so they sit at fixed stations rather than where the
// beam lands.
fn lightEmitTuned(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l: RzLight;
  l.color = params.BEAM;
  l.intensity = 0.0;
  l.radius = 1.0;
  l.pos = vec3f(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
  let eye = i32(i % 2u);
  let k = i32(i / 2u);
  let a = rzAnchor(0, eye);
  if (!a.valid) { return l; }
  let d = laserLook(0, a);
  let station = min(select(select(14.0, 5.0, k == 1), 1.2, k == 0), params.LENGTH);
  l.pos = laserOrigin(0, eye, a) + d * station;
  l.radius = select(select(22.0, 13.0, k == 1), 6.0, k == 0);
  l.intensity = params.LIGHT * select(select(0.5, 0.75, k == 1), 1.0, k == 0);
  return l;
}

// Its lights as tuned on the engine's old curve — brightness at the light, zero
// at the radius — carried onto the inverse square every light now falls off by:
// the same brightness half-way out, brighter nearer, dimmer beyond, in
// Unity's units (no π); the bulb (near 2.5) is where that old curve flattened.
fn lightEmit(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l = lightEmitTuned(i, time);
  l.intensity = l.intensity * 0.64 * max(0.25 * l.radius * l.radius, 6.25);
  l.near = 2.5;
  return l;
}

#anchor 左目
#anchor 右目
#lights 2
#layer additive
#param color CORE #fff0ee — white-hot, a shade of pink
#param color BEAM #ff1a12 — the red around it
#param float GLOW 1.0 0 3 — how large the star is
#param float LIGHT 2.5 0 8 — the light the eyes put on her face
#param float SPREAD 0.12 0 0.5 — the star sits this much outward of the eye bone, toward the iris

// Laser Stare — Laser Eyes before it fires: the star in each eye, and the red
// light it puts on her face. The eyeball part of Laser Eyes, exactly, with no
// beam. The star sits at the cornea, a little in front of the eye bone, which
// is at the eyeball's centre, on the eye's own axis.

const EYE_R = 0.45;

/** The eye's own axis: the bone's local +Z, and an MMD model rests facing -Z. */
fn laserAxis(a: RzAnchor) -> vec3f {
  return normalize(-a.fwd);
}

/** The star's seat: on the eye's axis, a little in front of the bone, and a
 *  little outward — away from the other eye — toward the iris. */
fn laserOrigin(subject: i32, eye: i32, a: RzAnchor) -> vec3f {
  let other = rzAnchor(subject, 1 - eye);
  let out = select(vec3f(0.0), normalize(a.pos - other.pos), other.valid);
  return a.pos + laserAxis(a) * EYE_R + out * params.SPREAD;
}

/** A world radius at a point, as a fraction of the frame's height: the
 *  point and a point one radius beside it, projected. No reach into the
 *  camera's own numbers, so the library's preview — which stands in for the
 *  scene with an API of its own — draws this exactly as the scene does. */
fn laserScreenRadius(p: vec3f, world: f32) -> f32 {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let aspect = res.x / max(res.y, 1.0);
  let f = rzCameraForward();
  var side = cross(f, vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 0.0));
  if (length(side) < 1.0e-3) { side = vec3f(1.0, 0.0, 0.0); }
  let a = rzProject(p);
  let b = rzProject(p + normalize(side) * world);
  return length((b.xy - a.xy) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0));
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = rzResolution();
  let aspect = res.x / max(res.y, 1.0);
  var col = vec3f(0.0);
  var amount = 0.0;
  // Light ADDS — see Laser Eyes: the frame under the pixel plus the star.
  let red = params.BEAM;
  let n = min(rzSubjectCount(), 4);
  for (var s = 0; s < n; s = s + 1) {
    for (var eye = 0; eye < 2; eye = eye + 1) {
      let a = rzAnchor(s, eye);
      if (!a.valid) { continue; }
      let origin = laserOrigin(s, eye, a);
      let pe = rzProject(origin);
      // The eye: a STAR where the beam leaves it — a white-hot point, a red
      // halo, and rays off it, four straight and four on the diagonal, the
      // way a lens draws a point of light; the rays turn slowly. Gone where
      // something nearer hides the eye.
      if (pe.z > 0.05) {
        let toEye = (uv - pe.xy) * vec2f(aspect, 1.0);
        let r = length(toEye);
        let rEye = laserScreenRadius(origin, 0.13 * params.GLOW);
        let rHalo = laserScreenRadius(origin, 0.5 * params.GLOW);
        let rRay = laserScreenRadius(origin, 1.1 * params.GLOW);
        let point = exp(-(r * r) / max(rEye * rEye, 1.0e-8));
        let halo = exp(-(r * r) / max(rHalo * rHalo, 1.0e-8));
        let ang = atan2(toEye.y, toEye.x) + time * 0.35;
        let rays = (pow(max(cos(2.0 * ang), 0.0), 40.0) + 0.6 * pow(max(cos(2.0 * ang + 0.7853982), 0.0), 40.0))
          * exp(-r / max(rRay, 1.0e-8));
        let eyeVisible = select(0.0, 1.0, depth > pe.z - 0.6);
        let star = (point * 1.3 + rays * 1.0) * eyeVisible;
        col = col + mix(red, params.CORE, clamp(point + rays * 0.6, 0.0, 1.0)) * star
          + red * halo * 0.55 * eyeVisible;
        amount = max(amount, max(star, halo * 0.55 * eyeVisible));
      }

    }
  }
  let a = clamp(amount, 1.0e-4, 1.0);
  return vec4f(min(rzSceneFrame(uv) + col / a, vec3f(1.0)), a);
}

// One light in each eye, for the first subject, just in front of the face.
fn lightEmitTuned(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l: RzLight;
  l.color = params.BEAM;
  l.intensity = 0.0;
  l.radius = 1.0;
  l.pos = vec3f(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
  let a = rzAnchor(0, i32(i));
  if (!a.valid) { return l; }
  l.pos = laserOrigin(0, i32(i), a) + laserAxis(a) * 0.8;
  l.radius = 8.0;
  l.intensity = params.LIGHT;
  return l;
}

// Its lights as tuned on the engine's old curve — brightness at the light, zero
// at the radius — carried onto the inverse square every light now falls off by:
// the same brightness half-way out, brighter nearer, dimmer beyond, in
// Unity's units (no π); the bulb (near 2.5) is where that old curve flattened.
fn lightEmit(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l = lightEmitTuned(i, time);
  l.intensity = l.intensity * 0.64 * max(0.25 * l.radius * l.radius, 6.25);
  l.near = 2.5;
  return l;
}

#mirror

#param float POS_X -11 -60 60
#param float POS_Y 0 0 40 — the FOOT, not the centre
#param float POS_Z 2 -60 60
#param float ROT_X 0 -90 90 — lean the glass back on its foot
#param float ROT_Y 135 -180 180 — turn it; 0 faces +Z
#param float ROT_Z 0 -180 180
#param float WIDTH 14 1 60
#param float HEIGHT 24 1 60
#param color TINT #ffffff — how a mirror falls short of perfect
#param float BLUR 0 0 1 — 0 polished, 1 frosted
#param float FRAME 0.48 0 4 — moulding width in world units, 0 for a bare pane
#param color FRAME_COLOR #a1a1aa — the highlight takes this colour too, so it reads as metal

// Mirror — a real planar reflection, and the only effect with no shader.
//
// What it shows is the SCENE, not the camera. The engine draws every model a
// second time from the camera folded through this plane, so the reflection is
// lit by the same sun, stands in the same shadows, and carries the particle and
// ribbon effects running beside it — put a hand ribbon on her and the mirror
// has one too. A screen-space trick can do none of that: it can only reflect
// what the camera already happens to see.
//
// #mirror is a mount, the same way #lights is. A reflection re-renders the
// scene, which is a PASS, and no shader mount is a pass — so the effect
// declares the plane and the engine reflects.
//
// ── PLACING IT ──
//
// UNITS ARE MMD'S. A character stands about 20 tall, the floor grid steps every
// 4.2, and a second cast member spawns 9 aside. The mirror above is 13 x 24 —
// taller than she is, which is what a full-length mirror is.
//
// IT MUST STAND BEHIND HER. A mirror shows what is on its own front side and
// nothing whatever of what is behind it, so a plane dropped between the camera
// and the cast is a window onto an empty room. The camera orbits to negative Z,
// so the glass belongs at positive Z, turned back toward both — which is what
// ROT_Y 135 does from POS_X -11.
//
// AND OFF TO ONE SIDE. Directly behind her, her reflection lands inside her own
// silhouette and her body covers it. Every mirror shot ever composed is at an
// angle for this reason.
//
// ONE at a time. There is one reflection target, so a second Mirror in the same
// scene is ignored rather than fought over.
//
// WIDTH and HEIGHT are the OUTER size — the glass is inset by FRAME. HEIGHT
// grows UPWARD from POS_Y, because a mirror stands on the floor: shortening it
// takes the height off the top and leaves the foot where it is.
//
// SIZE IT FOR HER IMAGE, not for her. A mirror only shows the part of the scene
// whose reflected ray passes through the glass, so a pane she would fit inside
// standing next to it still cuts her off from a low angle. Too small reads as a
// bug; it is the same physics as stepping back from a bathroom mirror.
//
// The dials are read BY NAME — leave one out and it takes its default.

#param float WIDTH 0.18 0 1 — how thick and how dark the ink reads. The MME controller's width
#param float STRENGTH 3 0 20 — the lift on the edge response. The source's own is 5, which inks the weave of a texture along with the form
#param float THRESHOLD 0 0 1 — the level the ink lifts toward. The controller's a
#param color LINE_COLOR #000000 — raise a channel and the ink takes that hue. The controller's r, g, b
#param float TINT 1 0 1 — RanSe's colouring, laid on the ink
#param float BANDS 1.5 0.25 8 — how many times its rainbow crosses the frame
#param float HUE 0.55 0 1 — the colour the first band starts on

// Line Art — the whole frame redrawn as inked line work.
//
// After two MME post effects that are used together: Dirnot's outline, which
// turns the picture into line art, and 小林呓's RanSe, which colours the result.
// The author's own instruction is that order — draw first, colour second — so
// they are ONE effect here. Two would mean an ordering the layer list does not
// enforce, and RanSe over anything but line art is just a gradient.
//
// A FILTER OVER THE WHOLE FRAME, the same as CRT Glitch and 8-Bit: the picture
// is rebuilt from the scene where it was drawn and rzBackground's colour where
// it was not, and the pen goes over all of it. A draughtsman has no opinion
// about which pixels are geometry.
//
// WHAT IT ERASES: background effects, which compose after this pass and so
// cannot be read. Over a transparent background the paper is only where the
// scene drew, because white paper across an alpha export is not a drawing, it
// is a blank page.
//
// NINE TAPS, and that is the whole budget. The source spends five full-screen
// passes: a separable gaussian pair that softens the picture, a Laplacian over
// the result, a box blur that fattens the lines, then the tint. One pass has no
// intermediate targets, so the softening is bought with SPACING instead — a
// Laplacian read at 1 px, which is what the source used, finds nothing but
// aliasing on a 4K frame, while one read wider ignores detail below its own
// step exactly as a pre-blur would.

// ── The pen ──
// Pixels between taps at each end of WIDTH. The source's own step was a fixed
// 1 px because its pre-blur had already decided what an edge was.
const MIN_STEP: f32 = 1.0;
const MAX_STEP: f32 = 3.5;

// How much the 45°-turned kernel counts. The source's cross kernel is blind to
// diagonals, which its box-blur pass then smeared into place; adding the turned
// one is this port's substitute, and it costs four taps it already has.
const DIAGONAL: f32 = 0.5;

// ── The colouring ──
// How far the bands sit from grey, and where they sit. Read off pix.png, whose
// colours are mid-tones — ink the drawing can still be read through.
const BAND_SATURATION: f32 = 0.62;
const BAND_FLOOR: f32 = 0.45;

fn laLuma(c: vec3f) -> f32 { return dot(c, vec3f(0.299, 0.587, 0.114)); }

/**
 * The finished picture at a point, as one number.
 *
 * ON LUMA FROM THE START, which is not a shortcut: the source runs its kernel on
 * colour, lifts it, inverts it and converts to luma last. Luma is linear and
 * luma(white) is 1, so 1 - 5·luma(L) and luma(1 - 5·L) are the same number.
 * Doing it first is three fewer channels through every tap.
 */
fn laFrame(at: vec2f) -> f32 {
  let bg = rzBackground();
  let sc = rzSceneDisplay(at);
  let a = clamp(rzSceneAlpha(at), 0.0, 1.0);
  return laLuma(select(sc, sc * a + bg.rgb * (1.0 - a), bg.w > 0.5));
}

/**
 * One band of RanSe's gradient, at a point along it.
 *
 * The colouring is a picture — pix.png, which the author says to swap for any
 * other — laid over the drawing at screen uv. The picture is a rainbow in wide
 * diagonal bands, so a hue ramp IS that picture, with how many bands and where
 * they start as dials instead of as a file.
 */
fn laBand(h: f32) -> vec3f {
  let k = fract(h + vec3f(0.0, 2.0 / 3.0, 1.0 / 3.0)) * 6.0 - 3.0;
  let hue = clamp(abs(k) - 1.0, vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));
  return mix(vec3f(BAND_FLOOR), hue, BAND_SATURATION);
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = max(rzResolution(), vec2f(1.0));
  let w = clamp(params.WIDTH, 0.0, 1.0);
  let step = mix(MIN_STEP, MAX_STEP, w) / res;

  let c = laFrame(uv);
  let l = laFrame(uv - vec2f(step.x, 0.0));
  let r = laFrame(uv + vec2f(step.x, 0.0));
  let u = laFrame(uv - vec2f(0.0, step.y));
  let d = laFrame(uv + vec2f(0.0, step.y));
  let ul = laFrame(uv - step);
  let ur = laFrame(uv + vec2f(step.x, -step.y));
  let dl = laFrame(uv + vec2f(-step.x, step.y));
  let dr = laFrame(uv + step);

  // The source's kernel, verbatim: 0,1,0 / 1,-4,1 / 0,1,0. And the same kernel
  // turned 45°, weighted by DIAGONAL.
  let cross = l + r + u + d - 4.0 * c;
  let diag = ul + ur + dl + dr - 4.0 * c;

  // SIGNED, never absolute, and this is the line that makes it read as a pen.
  // 1 - 5·L clips the bright side of every edge back to paper and inks only the
  // dark side, so a form is drawn with ONE line. Taking the magnitude instead
  // draws both sides of every edge and the result reads as an emboss.
  let paper = 1.0 - params.STRENGTH * (cross + DIAGONAL * diag);

  // The source's width amplification, verbatim: invert, scale by 1 + width·10,
  // invert back. It deepens a line rather than spreading it — the spreading is
  // in the tap spacing above.
  var ink = clamp(1.0 - paper, 0.0, 1.0);
  ink = clamp(ink * (1.0 + w * 10.0), 0.0, 1.0);

  // ── The pen's colour ──
  // The author's injection, verbatim, including the 150/255:
  //
  //   c += saturate((t - c) · (1 + 4·k)),  t = (a + 0.1)·150/255
  //
  // Written on the INK rather than on the page. On paper the source's bracket
  // is negative and saturate drops it, so the page is white either way; on ink
  // each channel lifts toward t at its own rate, which is how r, g and b tint
  // the lines. Held here as the colour a full line is drawn in, so a half-inked
  // pixel is half of that line rather than a separate colour of its own.
  let t = (clamp(params.THRESHOLD, 0.0, 1.0) + 0.1) * 150.0 / 255.0;
  let lifted = clamp(vec3f(t) * (1.0 + 4.0 * params.LINE_COLOR), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));

  // ── RanSe ──
  // Sampled at SCREEN uv along the picture's own axis — its bands run
  // bottom-left to top-right — so the colour belongs to the frame and the ink
  // changes hue as the cast moves across it.
  //
  // Laid ON THE PEN, which is the file's blend mode 1: screen(white, g) is
  // white and screen(black, g) is g, so over a white page with a black pen the
  // gradient reaches the ink and nothing else. Running the blend over the
  // finished drawing instead spends the line's weight — a half-inked pixel
  // screened toward a band comes back closer to the paper than it started, and
  // a page of them reads as blank. Mode 2, overlay, is the file's other
  // offer and it leaves a black line black: it is for colouring a rendered
  // scene, and there is no scene left here to colour.
  let g = laBand((uv.x * 0.75 + uv.y * 0.25) * params.BANDS + params.HUE);
  let pen = mix(lifted, g, clamp(params.TINT, 0.0, 1.0));
  let col = mix(vec3f(1.0), pen, ink);

  let bg = rzBackground();
  let cover = clamp(rzSceneAlpha(uv), 0.0, 1.0);
  return vec4f(clamp(col, vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0)), select(cover, 1.0, bg.w > 0.5));
}

#param float WIDTH 0 0 1 — the pen's reach, and so how thick the line. It is also the reach a fill GROWS by, so raise them together if an eye stops filling
#param float STRENGTH 3 0 20 — the lift on the edge response
#param float INK 0.9 0 1 — how black the pen is
#param float DARK 0.34 0 1 — anything darker than this fills solid, whatever the light is doing to it. Hair, trousers, a leather strap
#param float PAPER 0.78 0 1 — anything brighter than this is bare paper
#param float SHADOW 0.55 0 1 — how much darker than its own surroundings a LIT surface must be before it fills too
#param float EDGE 0.14 0.01 1 — how hard the edge of a fill is. Small is a brush cut
#param float GROW 0.6 0 1 — how far a fill reaches into the lines beside it. This is what turns the lashes, the iris rim and the pupil into ONE black eye instead of three thin lines
#param float TONE 0.8 0 1 — how much of the mid range is laid as screentone
#param float PITCH 3 1.5 24 — the tone's dot pitch, in pixels of a 1440-line frame
#param float BACKDROP 0.45 0 1 — how much of the black and the tone the STAGE gets. The cast always gets all of it
#param float DISTANCE 0.35 0 1 — how far into the depth of the scene the page goes blank

// Manga — the frame as a printed page.
//
// Line Art with the other two thirds of the craft: a pen, SOLID BLACKS with a
// hard edge, and SCREENTONE for everything between them. Monochrome, because
// the page is. Fujimoto's ink is the reference — a face lit from one side is
// three values (paper, tone, black) and a line, never a gradient.
//
// A SEPARATE EFFECT FROM LINE ART, not a mode on it. Line Art's pen is tuned
// pale and thin and its other four dials are RanSe's colouring, which means
// nothing on a page that has no colour; a mode switch would leave half the
// panel inert and every description qualified by which mode it belongs to. The
// pen is thirty lines and both want their own defaults.
//
// It rebuilds the picture the way Line Art does — the scene where it drew,
// rzBackground's colour where it did not — so the two sit in the layer list
// the same way. WHAT IT ERASES: background effects, which compose after this
// pass and cannot be read. Over a transparent background the page exists only
// where the scene drew.
//
// ── The three values ──
//
// THE BLACKS ARE TWO READINGS, and the darker wins. An inker fills two
// different things with the same brush. What is DARK — black hair, black
// trousers, a leather strap — goes solid whatever the light is doing to it,
// and that is an absolute level, DARK to PAPER. What is IN SHADOW goes solid
// too, and that one cannot be absolute: a dim room would flood the panel. So
// the second reading compares each point against a wide local average and
// measures a RATIO — an evenly lit wall reads 1.0 and stays white however dark
// its paint is, the turned side of a white shirt reads 0.5 and fills at any
// exposure.
//
// Either alone is wrong in a way you see immediately: absolute alone floods a
// dim scene, relative alone leaves black hair WHITE, because hair lit evenly is
// no darker than the hair around it.
//
// THE CAST OUTRANKS THE STAGE. rzCastDistance is zero on the cast and on what
// she holds, positive everywhere else, so one sample says which side of the
// drawing a pixel is on. She gets the full weight of the black and the tone
// because she is what the page is about; the stage gets BACKDROP of it, which
// is what stops a detailed set from out-inking the figure standing in it.
//
// THE TONE IS GLUED TO THE PAPER. Screentone is a physical sheet: it does not
// move when the camera does, and anchoring the lattice to world position or to
// a model's UV makes it crawl over the surface, which reads as noise rather
// than as print. The grid is in SCREEN pixels, and its pitch is measured in
// PICTURE — dots per 1440 lines — so a 4K export is the same page enlarged and
// not a finer tone nobody asked for.
//
// NO DITHER AND NO NOISE. A hash per pixel is what makes a filter read as a
// screenshot rather than as ink, and the cost ceiling forbids stacking them
// anyway. The lattice is arithmetic, and the whole effect spends thirteen taps
// and no hashes.

// ── The pen ──
// Pixels between taps at each end of WIDTH, against a 1440-line frame.
const MIN_STEP: f32 = 1.0;
const MAX_STEP: f32 = 3.5;
// The 45°-turned kernel's weight, as Line Art's.
const DIAGONAL: f32 = 0.5;

// ── The page reference ──
// How much wider than the pen the local average is read. It has to be wide
// enough that a shadow SHAPE does not average itself away — at nine times the
// pen it is reading the lighting, not the form.
const WIDE: f32 = 9.0;
// A floor under the reference, so a ratio against an almost-black page stays a
// number rather than an explosion.
const FLOOR: f32 = 0.04;

// ── The tone ──
// The printer's angle. A lattice square to the frame beats against every
// horizontal edge in the picture and against the pixel grid itself; turned 45°
// it beats against neither.
const TONE_COS: f32 = 0.70710678;
const TONE_SIN: f32 = 0.70710678;
// A dot of radius r in a unit cell covers PI*r*r, so the radius for a coverage
// is sqrt(k/PI) — the law that makes 50% tone look like 50% and not like 70%.
const INV_PI: f32 = 0.31830989;
// Dots touch at 0.5 and the cell is full at 0.707. Past that the gaps close.
const MAX_R: f32 = 0.72;

fn mgLuma(c: vec3f) -> f32 { return dot(c, vec3f(0.299, 0.587, 0.114)); }

/** The finished picture at a point, as one number — Line Art's tap. */
fn mgFrame(at: vec2f) -> f32 {
  let bg = rzBackground();
  let sc = rzSceneDisplay(at);
  let a = clamp(rzSceneAlpha(at), 0.0, 1.0);
  return mgLuma(select(sc, sc * a + bg.rgb * (1.0 - a), bg.w > 0.5));
}

/**
 * The screentone dot at a point, as coverage 0..1.
 *
 * `cell` is the pitch in DEVICE pixels and the caller is the one that scales it
 * against the picture. The dot's edge is softened by half a device pixel —
 * expressed in cell units, so it is the same half pixel at any resolution and
 * the tone neither shimmers in a 4K export nor goes chalky in the preview.
 */
fn mgTone(at: vec2f, cell: f32, coverage: f32) -> f32 {
  let k = clamp(coverage, 0.0, 1.0);
  if (k <= 0.001) { return 0.0; }
  let turned = vec2f(at.x * TONE_COS - at.y * TONE_SIN, at.x * TONE_SIN + at.y * TONE_COS);
  let cellPos = fract(turned / cell) - vec2f(0.5);
  let r = length(cellPos);
  let want = min(sqrt(k * INV_PI), MAX_R);
  let aa = 0.7 / cell;
  return 1.0 - smoothstep(want - aa, want + aa, r);
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let res = max(rzResolution(), vec2f(1.0));
  // One pixel OF THE PICTURE, in device pixels. Everything measured in page
  // terms — the pen's reach, the tone's pitch — goes through this, which is
  // what makes the export the preview enlarged.
  let px = max(res.y / 1440.0, 0.5);
  let w = clamp(params.WIDTH, 0.0, 1.0);
  let step = mix(MIN_STEP, MAX_STEP, w) * px / res;

  let c = mgFrame(uv);
  let l = mgFrame(uv - vec2f(step.x, 0.0));
  let r = mgFrame(uv + vec2f(step.x, 0.0));
  let u = mgFrame(uv - vec2f(0.0, step.y));
  let d = mgFrame(uv + vec2f(0.0, step.y));
  let ul = mgFrame(uv - step);
  let ur = mgFrame(uv + vec2f(step.x, -step.y));
  let dl = mgFrame(uv + vec2f(-step.x, step.y));
  let dr = mgFrame(uv + step);

  // ── The pen, which is Line Art's ──
  let cross = l + r + u + d - 4.0 * c;
  let diag = ul + ur + dl + dr - 4.0 * c;
  let paper = 1.0 - params.STRENGTH * (cross + DIAGONAL * diag);
  var line = clamp(1.0 - paper, 0.0, 1.0);
  line = clamp(line * (1.0 + w * 10.0), 0.0, 1.0) * clamp(params.INK, 0.0, 1.0);

  // ── The page reference ──
  // A cross of four at nine times the pen's reach. Four, because this sets a
  // LEVEL rather than finding an edge: it wants to be smooth, and the eight the
  // pen already spent are the ones that had to be sharp.
  let far = step * WIDE;
  let wide = 0.25 * (mgFrame(uv + vec2f(far.x, 0.0)) + mgFrame(uv - vec2f(far.x, 0.0))
                   + mgFrame(uv + vec2f(0.0, far.y)) + mgFrame(uv - vec2f(0.0, far.y)));
  // How dark this point is AGAINST ITS OWN PAGE. 1 is "as bright as everything
  // around it"; below BLACK it is in shadow whatever the exposure was.
  let level = clamp(c / max(wide, FLOOR), 0.0, 2.0);

  // Her side of the drawing, or the set's. Zero on the cast and on her props,
  // and the value runs to -0.5 inside her, so the test is a sign.
  let onCast = select(0.0, 1.0, rzCastDistance(uv) <= 0.0);
  // The page falls away with distance, which is how a panel keeps a background
  // from competing. The far plane tracks the camera rather than the metre, so
  // this is a fraction of the visible depth and not a distance in metres.
  let away = clamp(rzSceneDepth(uv) / max(rzSceneFar() * 0.25, 1e-3), 0.0, 1.0);
  let weight = mix(clamp(params.BACKDROP, 0.0, 1.0), 1.0, onCast) * (1.0 - clamp(params.DISTANCE, 0.0, 1.0) * away);

  // ── The three values ──
  // Low-to-high, always: smoothstep is undefined when its edges cross, and it
  // is the kind of undefined that works on the machine it was written on.
  let fillAt = clamp(params.SHADOW, 0.0, 0.99);
  let edge = max(params.EDGE, 0.01);
  let dark = min(params.DARK, params.PAPER - 0.01);

  // WHAT THE FILL READS, and it is not this pixel. An eye is a cluster of thin
  // dark parts — the lash line, the iris rim, the pupil — with lit skin and a
  // highlight in the gaps between them, so no single point is dark for long
  // enough to fill, and the panel comes back with three scratchy lines where
  // the ink should be. An inker fills the cluster as ONE shape. Taking the
  // darkest of the nine taps the pen already spent grows a fill by the pen's
  // own reach, which closes those gaps; GROW says how far toward that darkest
  // reading the fill goes, and at 0 it is this pixel alone again.
  //
  // What it costs: a highlight thinner than the pen's reach is swallowed. A
  // catchlight drawn at three pixels survives, one drawn at one does not.
  let darkest = min(min(min(c, l), min(r, u)), min(min(d, ul), min(min(ur, dl), dr)));
  let fillLuma = mix(c, darkest, clamp(params.GROW, 0.0, 1.0));

  // 0 is solid ink, 1 is bare paper.
  let own = smoothstep(dark, params.PAPER, fillLuma);
  let lit = smoothstep(fillAt - edge, fillAt + 0.3, level * fillLuma / max(c, 1e-3));
  let value = min(own, lit);

  let black = (1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, edge, value)) * weight;

  // ── The tone ──
  // Everything the fill did not take, as dots, with the fill subtracted so the
  // two meet at its edge instead of double-inking across it.
  let coverage = clamp((1.0 - value) * clamp(params.TONE, 0.0, 1.0) * weight - black, 0.0, 1.0);
  let tone = mgTone(uv * res, max(params.PITCH * px, 2.0), coverage);

  // ── The page ──
  // Three layers, darkest wins: tone under the fill, the pen over both. A line
  // that falls inside a solid black is invisible, which is what ink does.
  let ink = max(max(black, tone), line);
  let col = vec3f(1.0 - ink);

  let bg = rzBackground();
  let cover = clamp(rzSceneAlpha(uv), 0.0, 1.0);
  return vec4f(clamp(col, vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0)), select(cover, 1.0, bg.w > 0.5));
}

#anchor 左手首
#anchor 右手首
#lights 3
#layer additive
#duration 12 — one firing, at PERIOD's default

#param float PERIOD 12 3 40 — how long one firing takes, hands to silence
#param color BLUE #0a6bff — the one that draws in
#param color RED #ff1408 — the one that pushes out
#param color PURPLE #a827ff — what they make between them
#param float POWER 4 0 12 — how hard the orbs light her
#param float REACH 16 2 60 — how far each orb carries, in world units
#param float RADIUS 0.26 0.03 3 — the orb itself, in world units
#param float TURNS 5 1 14 — circuits around her before they meet
#param float FLASH 1.0 0 4 — how hard the room flashes when it goes

// Gojo — two small lights circling her, meeting, and going off.
//
// THE LAYER ADDS, it does not cover. These are light sources, and light does
// not occlude what is behind it — an orb blended over the frame puts a disc of
// paint on her arm as it passes, and a flash blended over the room greys every
// black in it. Added, the same pixels can only get brighter, which is the one
// thing a blast is guaranteed to do.
//
// THE LIGHT IS THE EFFECT. Two coloured dots over a frame are two dots; what
// reads is her face going blue on one side and red on the other as they pass,
// and the whole room going violet when they touch. That is why this is built on
// lightEmit rather than on a field layer alone: a field paints over the finished
// picture and can never reach the shading, so the drawing below is only the
// filament — the lamps are what land on her.
//
// ONE TRAJECTORY, READ TWICE. orbAt() is the only place an orb's position
// exists, and both halves call it: the lamp asks where to stand, the field asks
// where to draw. Two closed forms for one path is two things to keep in step,
// and they drift the moment either is tuned — the light ends up trailing the dot
// it is supposed to be inside. This is the same reason the engine runs lightEmit
// as a compute stage on the scene clock rather than taking positions from the
// CPU.
//
// It leaves her WRISTS, not her sides. #anchor binds both, so the orbs start
// wherever her hands are and the throw follows the motion; a rig that spells the
// bones differently reports invalid and the orbs start at her shoulders instead
// of at the world origin.

// The cycle, as fractions of one PERIOD. Four moments and a rest, and the rest
// is as much a part of it as the rest: a detonation that is immediately followed
// by two more orbs launching reads as a loop, and a beat of empty air reads as a
// thing that happened.
//
// SCHEDULED BY THE STRIP, not by a clamp. #duration says this is a HIT rather
// than a condition the scene is in, so dropping it on the timeline gives a strip
// already the right length — and a scheduled instance is handed its STRIP'S
// local time, starting at zero where you placed it, so it plays its opening
// there rather than joining whatever the scene clock had reached.
//
// The phase therefore WRAPS. Clamping it instead was wrong in the one case that
// matters most: an effect that has not been placed on the timeline yet has an
// epoch from when it was installed, so a clamped arc fired once on load and then
// sat dark for ever — which reads as broken, and makes the declared duration
// look like a lie. Wrapped, it shows what it does the moment you add it, plays
// from the top wherever you schedule it, and repeats only if you give it a strip
// longer than one firing.
const T_SPIRAL = 0.60;    // circling, tightening, speeding up — most of it
const T_COLLAPSE = 0.68;  // the violet gathering itself at the top
const T_BOOM = 0.91;      // and opening — a quarter of the cycle
const TAU = 6.2831853;

const LAUNCH = 0.07;    // how much of the spiral is spent leaving her hand
const TILT = 0.30;      // how far each circle leans out of level
const WHIP = 2.6;       // how sharply the turning accelerates as they close
const FALL = 1.25;      // how fast the orbit decays; >1 holds the radius, then dives
const GATHER = 0.45;    // how small the violet pulls itself before it goes
const BURST = 48.0;     // how much bigger it gets on the way out
const SPREAD = 6.0;     // the most the lamp's reach grows with it

struct Orb {
  pos: vec3f,
  tint: vec3f,
  /** Lamp intensity, and the same number the core's brightness rides. */
  power: f32,
  /** World radius. 0 is absent — an orb with no size is not in the scene. */
  size: f32,
  /**
   * What the DRAWN body and glow ride, where `power` is what the lamp rides.
   *
   * They were one number, and that is what put a second explosion after the
   * first. The lamp should outlast the flash — a room stays lit for a moment
   * after the thing that lit it — but the PICTURE of a violet sphere has no
   * business still being there, slowly swelling, while it fades. Split, the
   * drawn blast is a flash and only the light lingers.
   */
  glow: f32,
  /**
   * 0 a solid body, 1 a hollow shell.
   *
   * What a detonation actually is. A ball that simply gets bigger reads as a
   * balloon however fast it grows — the middle stays as bright as the edge, so
   * nothing about it says the energy is LEAVING. A shell puts the light at the
   * limb and lets you see through the centre, and then the growth reads as a
   * front travelling outward.
   */
  shell: f32,
}

fn orbPhase(time: f32) -> f32 {
  return fract(time / max(params.PERIOD, 0.01));
}

/**
 * Where orb `i` is, and how hot, at `time`. 0 is blue, 1 is red, 2 is violet.
 *
 * TWO CHARGES FALLING TOGETHER. The pair do not fly a circle and then meet on
 * cue — they orbit a shrinking radius at a rising rate, which is what two things
 * pulling on each other actually do, and the meeting is where that ends rather
 * than an event scheduled on top of it. The turning accelerates (WHIP) while the
 * radius decays (FALL), so the last half-second is a whip-in of several turns
 * and the collision arrives as the conclusion of the motion.
 *
 * The violet never travels. It is made at the top, pulls itself smaller while
 * it gathers, and goes off where it was made — a thing that flew somewhere else
 * to explode would take the eye off her, and she is what the light is for.
 */
fn orbAt(i: u32, time: f32) -> Orb {
  var o: Orb;
  o.pos = vec3f(0.0);
  o.tint = vec3f(0.0);
  o.power = 0.0;
  o.glow = 0.0;
  o.size = 0.0;
  o.shell = 0.0;

  let s = rzSubject(0);
  if (!s.valid) { return o; }

  let p = orbPhase(time);
  // MEASURED FROM HER BODY, NOT FROM THE FLOOR SHE LEFT.
  //
  // `root` tracks her horizontally — it is the hip point with the placement's
  // floor height — so travel is not the problem. Height is. `root.y` is
  // deliberately NOT animated, because a jump lifts the character and not the
  // ground, which makes `center.y - root.y` mean "how high her hips are" rather
  // than "how tall she is". Scale an orbit by that and the whole arrangement
  // inflates the moment she leaves the floor, and the apex climbs two and a half
  // times as fast as she does.
  //
  // The bounding sphere's radius is a size rather than an altitude, so it says
  // the same thing in the air as on the ground. It is deliberately generous, so
  // the multipliers below are tuned against it rather than against a real
  // height: 0.95 puts the ring just outside her, 1.1 the apex a little over her
  // crown.
  let mid = s.center;
  let span = max(s.bounds.w, 0.3);
  // JUST CLEAR OF HER HEAD. High enough that the blast opens into air rather
  // than through her shoulders, low enough to stay in the same shot as her —
  // put it much further up and the camera has to choose between the figure and
  // the thing above her, which is a composition with no subject.
  let apex = mid + vec3f(0.0, span * 1.1, 0.0);
  let ring = span * 0.95;

  if (i == 2u) {
    // THE VIOLET. It does not exist until the other two have touched, and after
    // it goes there is nothing at all until the next pair launch.
    if (p < T_SPIRAL || p >= T_BOOM) { return o; }
    o.tint = params.PURPLE;
    o.pos = apex;

    if (p < T_COLLAPSE) {
      // GATHERING. It contracts rather than swells — the power is going inward,
      // and a thing that grows steadily before it bursts has already spent its
      // surprise. Brightness climbs while the body shrinks, which is the whole
      // tension of the moment.
      let k = (p - T_SPIRAL) / max(T_COLLAPSE - T_SPIRAL, 1e-4);
      o.size = params.RADIUS * mix(1.6, GATHER, smoothstep(0.0, 1.0, k));
      // The flash of them meeting, decaying into a hard rising glow. The climb
      // is quartic rather than square so the last instant before it goes is far
      // brighter than the rest of the gather — the wind-up has to arrive
      // somewhere.
      o.power = params.POWER * (2.0 + 6.0 * exp(-9.0 * k) + 7.0 * k * k * k * k);
      o.glow = o.power;
      return o;
    }
    // GOING OFF: a punch, then a long opening. The edge leaves fast — pow(k,
    // 0.55) is steepest at the very start — and then keeps travelling while the
    // light fades slowly behind it. That gap between the shell still growing
    // and the glow already dying is the whole tension of the thing; a blast
    // that expands and dims on the same curve is over before it is read, and one
    // that opens steadily is a balloon.
    let k = (p - T_COLLAPSE) / max(T_BOOM - T_COLLAPSE, 1e-4);
    // WHITE-HOT FIRST, VIOLET AFTER. Nothing that releases this much energy is
    // coloured at the moment it lets go — the colour is what is left once the
    // first instant is over, and starting violet is what made this read as a big
    // glow rather than as a detonation. Two frames of white is the whole
    // difference, and the lamp takes the same tint, so the room goes white and
    // then goes purple.
    o.tint = mix(vec3f(1.0), params.PURPLE, smoothstep(0.0, 0.16, k));
    o.size = params.RADIUS * (GATHER + BURST * pow(k, 0.55));
    // ARRIVING AT NOTHING, not merely approaching it. pow(1-k, 1.3) is small at
    // the end and never zero, and small is not the same as gone when it is being
    // multiplied by a limb term of three and painted around a sphere twelve units
    // across: what is left is a wide, faint, perfectly steady purple ring that
    // sits there until the phase wraps and then pops out of existence. The
    // smoothstep lands it on exactly zero before the cycle ends, so the last
    // thing the eye sees is a fade rather than a disappearance.
    o.power = params.POWER * 14.0 * pow(1.0 - k, 1.3) * smoothstep(1.0, 0.82, k);
    // THE PICTURE OPENS, THEN STOPS. Full through the first third, so the whole
    // sky-wide expansion is there to watch; then taken off deliberately rather
    // than left to trail away, because the tail is the part that reads as a
    // second, smaller explosion happening slowly after the real one. The lamp
    // above keeps its own longer curve, so what outlives the picture is LIGHT on
    // the walls.
    o.glow = params.POWER * 14.0 * pow(1.0 - k, 0.9) * smoothstep(0.62, 0.30, k);
    // Solid for the first instant and hollow after: the core is still there
    // while the front is leaving it, which is the moment the eye reads as the
    // thing coming apart.
    o.shell = smoothstep(0.0, 0.22, k);
    return o;
  }

  // THE PAIR. Gone the moment they meet: two lamps left burning inside the
  // violet would light her from three places and wash the colour out of it.
  if (p >= T_SPIRAL) { return o; }
  o.tint = select(params.RED, params.BLUE, i == 0u);

  let u = clamp(p / T_SPIRAL, 0.0, 1.0);
  // ACCELERATING. pow(u, WHIP) has a slope that climbs with u, so the turning
  // is slow and wide at the start and a blur by the end — the same few turns
  // spread over the first half and crowded into the last moment.
  let sweep = pow(u, WHIP);
  // Opposite directions, opposite starting sides, opposite lean. Everything
  // about them is mirrored, which is what makes the meeting read as a meeting
  // rather than as two things arriving.
  let way = select(-1.0, 1.0, i == 0u);
  let lead = select(0.0, TAU * 0.5, i == 0u);
  let ang = lead + way * sweep * params.TURNS * TAU;
  // DECAYING TO NOTHING at the top. The radius reaches zero exactly as the
  // climb reaches the apex, so they arrive at one point rather than crossing
  // near it.
  let r = ring * pow(1.0 - u, FALL);
  // Relative to her middle, so a jump carries the whole spiral with it.
  let climb = mix(-span * 0.11, span * 1.1, pow(u, 1.4));
  let lean = way * TILT * r * sin(ang);
  let onRing = mid + vec3f(cos(ang) * r, climb + lean, sin(ang) * r);

  var at = onRing;
  if (p < LAUNCH) {
    // OUT OF HER HAND. The anchor is checked rather than trusted: a rig without
    // 左手首 reports invalid, and an unchecked read would start the orb at the
    // world origin and fly it in from wherever that happens to be.
    let a = rzAnchor(0, i32(i));
    let hand = select(mid + vec3f(way * ring * 0.5, 0.0, 0.0), a.pos, a.valid);
    at = mix(hand, onRing, smoothstep(0.0, 1.0, p / LAUNCH));
  }
  o.pos = at;
  // Brighter and tighter as they close, so the pair are at their fiercest in the
  // instant before they touch.
  // FIERCE FROM THE FIRST FRAME. They leave her hands already burning — a pair
  // that fade up read as being switched on, and nothing about this is switched
  // on. The climb on top is the attraction winding them tighter.
  o.power = params.POWER * (1.4 + 1.5 * u * u + 0.06 * sin(time * 11.0 + f32(i)));
  o.glow = o.power;
  o.size = params.RADIUS * (0.9 + 0.35 * u);
  return o;
}

/**
 * Three lamps, and every one of them a real light in the scene.
 *
 * Slot 2 is silent for two thirds of the cycle and slots 0 and 1 for the last
 * third. A lamp that is not wanted gets radius 0, which is how the engine reads
 * "off" — dimming it to zero intensity instead would keep paying the shader's
 * per-fragment distance test for every pixel it covers, for a light nobody can
 * see.
 */
fn lightEmitTuned(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l: RzLight;
  let o = orbAt(i, time);
  l.pos = o.pos;
  l.color = o.tint;
  l.intensity = o.power;
  // THE REACH OPENS WITH THE BODY. A sphere that swells to thirty times its own
  // size while its light keeps a fixed radius is a lamp inside a balloon: the
  // detonation fills the frame and lights nothing further than the orbs did.
  // Capped, because reach is paid for per fragment by every pixel inside it.
  let spread = min(max(o.size / max(params.RADIUS, 0.01), 1.0), SPREAD);
  l.radius = select(0.0, params.REACH * spread, o.size > 0.0);
  return l;
}

/**
 * One orb, as an actual sphere in the world.
 *
 * INTERSECTED, not projected. A disc pinned to the screen is a sticker: it
 * keeps its shape as the camera turns, it has no silhouette against her
 * shoulder, and the moment it passes behind an arm the whole orbit reads as
 * paint on the lens. A sphere is fifteen instructions — a dot, a discriminant
 * and a square root — and it comes with the perspective, the occlusion and the
 * curvature for free.
 *
 * `depth` measures along the VIEW AXIS while `t` runs along the ray, so the hit
 * is converted before it is compared. Testing t against depth directly puts the
 * orb in front of her at the edges of frame and behind her at the centre.
 */
fn drawOrb(o: Orb, ro: vec3f, rd: vec3f, fwd: vec3f, depth: f32) -> vec3f {
  if (o.size <= 0.0 || o.glow <= 0.0) { return vec3f(0.0); }
  let oc = o.pos - ro;
  let along = dot(oc, rd);
  if (along <= 0.0) { return vec3f(0.0); }   // behind the lens

  // Perpendicular distance from the ray to the centre — the one number both
  // the surface and the halo are built from.
  let perp = length(oc - rd * along);
  // BOUNDED, and cut where it reaches zero. A 1/r halo has no end, so it is
  // given one; the alternative is a wash over the whole frame that costs a
  // sample per pixel and shows as a lifted black.
  //
  // TWO TIERS, AND THEY DO DIFFERENT JOBS. One halo has to choose between being
  // tight — so the core reads as the object and not as a will-o'-the-wisp with
  // the glow mistaken for its body — and being wide, so the thing looks like it
  // is throwing light into the air rather than sitting in front of it. It cannot
  // be both, and every single-tier value tried here was one or the other.
  //
  // So: a tight bright skirt at three radii that belongs to the object, and a
  // broad faint bloom out to nine that belongs to the air around it. The wide
  // tier is quiet enough never to be read as size, and on an additive layer it
  // is what makes a pinpoint glow instead of merely being bright.
  //
  // NINE RADII AT EVERY SIZE. On a pinpoint that is a couple of units of air
  // catching light; on the blast it is most of the sky, which is the point — a
  // detonation that lights only its own skin is a lamp, and this one is supposed
  // to be the brightest thing that has ever happened in the room.
  let glowR = o.size * 9.0;
  if (perp >= glowR) { return vec3f(0.0); }
  // Derived from the outer bound rather than from the body, so the tight tier
  // can never outrun the cull and leave a hard edge where the glow is chopped.
  let coreR = glowR * 0.34;

  let hot = o.glow / max(params.POWER, 0.01);
  var rgb = vec3f(0.0);

  // The halo, occluded on the CENTRE's depth. Per-pixel occlusion of a soft
  // glow would cut it along her silhouette with a hard edge, which is worse
  // than letting it bloom past her.
  let centreDepth = along * max(dot(rd, fwd), 1e-4);
  if (centreDepth < depth) {
    // Two tiers: a tight skirt that belongs to the object, a broad bloom that
    // belongs to the air around it.
    //
    // max() rather than a branch: pow() of a negative base is undefined in WGSL,
    // and select() evaluates both arms.
    let near = pow(max(1.0 - perp / coreR, 0.0), 4.0);
    let wide = pow(1.0 - perp / glowR, 2.5);
    // HOLLOWED WITH THE BODY. Both tiers peak at perp = 0, which is right while
    // there is something at the middle and wrong the moment the energy has left
    // it: a centre-peaked glow on a shell paints a second, smaller purple sphere
    // sitting inside the expanding one. Faded toward the axis in step with
    // `shell`, the glow follows the front out and leaves nothing behind.
    let hollow = mix(1.0, smoothstep(o.size * 0.3, o.size, perp), o.shell);
    rgb += o.tint * ((near * 1.15 + wide * 0.5) * hollow * hot);
  }

  // The body. h < 0 is a miss: the ray passed outside it and only the halo
  // applies.
  let h = o.size * o.size - perp * perp;
  if (h <= 0.0) { return rgb; }
  let t = along - sqrt(h);
  if (t <= 0.0) { return rgb; }
  if (t * max(dot(rd, fwd), 1e-4) > depth) { return rgb; }

  let n = (ro + rd * t - o.pos) / o.size;
  // White at the middle, its colour at the rim. That is what a light source
  // looks like — a small fierce thing is overexposed where it faces you and
  // keeps its hue where it turns away — and inverting it reads as a paint dot.
  let facing = clamp(dot(n, -rd), 0.0, 1.0);
  // COLOUR IN THE CORE. A source that goes fully white at the middle is a white
  // dot with a coloured fringe — the hue lives in the thin rim and the eye reads
  // the effect as white. Holding the whitening back and putting the energy into
  // BRIGHTNESS instead keeps the blue blue and the red red where they are most
  // intense, which is the whole point of there being two of them.
  let body = mix(o.tint, vec3f(1.0), pow(facing, 3.0) * 0.22);
  // A SOLID orb is brightest where it faces you; a SHELL is brightest at the
  // limb, where the ray passes along the skin and takes the long way through it.
  // The same number drives both, read from opposite ends.
  // DENSE. The body is small enough now that its brightness is what carries it:
  // a pinpoint at the same exposure as a big orb reads as a spark, and this is
  // not a spark.
  let solid = 1.5 + 2.8 * facing;
  // The shell THINS as it opens: the same light spread over a bigger sphere has
  // to come from somewhere, and a limb that stays as wide at twelve units as it
  // was at one reads as a painted outline rather than as a front.
  // Nearly nothing through the middle: a shell you can see the far side of is a
  // shell, and 0.12 across the whole disc was enough to read as a filled ball.
  let rim = 0.04 + 3.6 * pow(1.0 - facing, 2.2);
  return rgb + body * (hot * mix(solid, rim, o.shell));
}

/**
 * The room going violet, everywhere at once.
 *
 * The lamps light what they reach and nothing further; a detonation of this size
 * throws light past every wall in the scene, and no point lamp with a finite
 * radius can say that. A flat wash over the whole frame is what the eye reads as
 * the room flashing — the same thing Waveform does to put its curve's light on
 * everything rather than only where the curve is.
 *
 * Hard at the instant it goes and gone almost at once: a flash that lingers is a
 * tint, and a tinted scene reads as a colour grade rather than as an event.
 */
fn blastWash(time: f32) -> vec3f {
  let p = orbPhase(time);
  if (p < T_COLLAPSE || p >= T_BOOM) { return vec3f(0.0); }
  let k = (p - T_COLLAPSE) / max(T_BOOM - T_COLLAPSE, 1e-4);
  // TWO WASHES, AND THE SHORT ONE IS THE POINT. A blowout of white lasting a
  // few frames is what a camera does when something far brighter than the scene
  // happens in front of it, and it is the entire reason a blast reads as violent
  // rather than as bright. Under it, the violet fill that says what colour the
  // violence was, on a curve slow enough to see.
  //
  // Landed on exactly zero before the cycle ends, for the same reason the orb
  // itself is: a wash that is merely small at the wrap pops out of existence.
  let punch = exp(-k * 34.0);
  let fill = exp(-k * 9.0);
  let stop = smoothstep(1.0, 0.85, k);
  return (vec3f(1.0) * (punch * 1.35) + params.PURPLE * fill) * (params.FLASH * stop);
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let ro = rzCameraPos();
  let rd = normalize(ray);
  let fwd = rzCameraForward();
  var rgb = vec3f(0.0);
  rgb += drawOrb(orbAt(0u, time), ro, rd, fwd, depth);
  rgb += drawOrb(orbAt(1u, time), ro, rd, fwd, depth);
  rgb += drawOrb(orbAt(2u, time), ro, rd, fwd, depth);
  rgb += blastWash(time);
  let a = clamp(max(rgb.r, max(rgb.g, rgb.b)), 0.0, 1.0);
  if (a <= 0.0) { return vec4f(0.0); }
  return vec4f(rgb, a);
}

// Its lights as tuned on the engine's old curve — brightness at the light, zero
// at the radius — carried onto the inverse square every light now falls off by:
// the same brightness half-way out, brighter nearer, dimmer beyond, in
// Unity's units (no π); the bulb (near 2.5) is where that old curve flattened.
fn lightEmit(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l = lightEmitTuned(i, time);
  l.intensity = l.intensity * 0.64 * max(0.25 * l.radius * l.radius, 6.25);
  l.near = 2.5;
  return l;
}

#param float BREAK 1.5 0 20 — seconds before the cut lands
#param float SLIP 0.10 0 0.5 — how far the halves slide PAST each other
#param float PART 0.06 0 0.4 — how far they also open, in screen heights
#param float OPEN 1.6 0.2 8 — seconds the halves take to settle
#param float BOW 0.04 0 0.3 — how far the cut bows away from level
#param float TILT 0.012 0 0.2 — how much each half turns as it goes
#param float SPLIT 6 0 24 — colour separation along the cut, in pixels
#param float SHAKE 0.011 0 0.06 — how hard the frame kicks when it lands
#param float RISE 0.32 -1.5 2.5 — where it crosses her: 0 at the hips, 1 at the crown

// World Slash — the space is cut through at her waist, and the halves slide apart.
//
// IT CUTS THE PICTURE, NOT HER. A filter reads the finished frame through
// rzSceneFrame and decides where each pixel came FROM, which is the only way to
// move the image itself: a layer can draw over the scene but cannot displace it,
// and the whole effect is the displacement. Everything the other effects drew
// goes with its half — lights, ribbons, orbs — because by the time this runs
// they are part of the frame.
//
// THROUGH HER, WHEREVER SHE STANDS. rzSubject(0) gives the floor under her and
// her hips, which is a height in the WORLD — RISE picks a point up her body
// between them and beyond, and projecting THAT gives the line in the frame she
// is actually in. A cut fixed at the middle of the screen is a page fold; a cut
// through her is an event that happened to somebody, and it follows her as she
// moves so the shot can travel.
//
// The elevation is world-space rather than a nudge in screen pixels on purpose:
// a screen offset means one thing when the camera is level and something else
// the moment it tilts or pushes in, so the cut would slide up her body as the
// shot moved. Measured against her own height it crosses the same rib every
// time.
//
// THE SAMPLE RUNS BACKWARDS. For each output pixel this asks which source pixel
// belongs there, so a half is moved by SUBTRACTING its travel rather than
// adding it. Forwards would mean scattering pixels to wherever they land, which
// a fragment shader cannot do and which tears holes where the picture stretched.
//
// IT COVERS THE WHOLE FRAME, and has to. A filter writes the FOREGROUND layer,
// which is composited OVER the scene rather than in place of it, so every pixel
// it leaves transparent shows the UNDISPLACED picture underneath — her, standing
// uncut, beside the halves that were cut out of her. Covering only her silhouette
// and leaving the background open was tried and is worse than it sounds: the
// buried original becomes a black cut-out of a woman, because with a video
// backdrop there is nothing else to put there. A shader cannot read native video
// — it is a DOM element behind the canvas — so it cannot move it, and it cannot
// give it back.
//
// The consequence, stated plainly: a backdrop video is hidden while this runs.
// Drawing that video into the canvas instead of playing it natively is what
// would let the cut take it too, and that is a change to the backdrop, not to
// this file.
//
// It does not come back. The slip eases to its distance and stays — what ends
// this is the clip ending, not the space healing.

const GAP = 0.0012;      // half-width of the dark line between the halves
const FLASH = 0.09;      // seconds of white along the cut as it lands
const JOLT = 7.0;        // how fast the kick dies; larger is shorter

/**
 * The cut's y at horizontal position x.
 *
 * ONE LONG ARC, and a slight lean off level. A blade leaves a single curve and
 * the eye reads the whole of it at once; stacking sine terms until it looks
 * broken gives you glass shattered by a stone, which is a different picture
 * entirely — and at this hairline width the raggedness only reads as noise.
 *
 * The lean matters as much as the bow. A cut exactly level reads as a letterbox
 * bar or a split screen; a few degrees off is what makes it a stroke somebody
 * made.
 */
fn cutAt(x: f32, base: f32) -> f32 {
  let c = x - 0.5;
  let bow = sin(c * 2.4);   // a third of a cycle across the frame: one bend, no wobble
  let lean = c * 0.55;
  return base + params.BOW * (bow + lean);
}

fn foreground(ray: vec3f, uv: vec2f, time: f32, depth: f32) -> vec4f {
  let t = time - params.BREAK;
  // Before it lands the picture is its own, untouched. Returning nothing rather
  // than resampling matters: a filter that resamples an unmoved frame still
  // costs a fetch and a filter tap, and still softens it by one.
  if (t <= 0.0) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // HER WAIST, or the middle of frame when there is nobody to cut. Behind the
  // camera the projection is meaningless, so that falls back too.
  var base = 0.5;
  let s = rzSubject(0);
  if (s.valid) {
    // UP FROM HER HIPS, BY HER OWN SIZE. `root.y` is the placement's floor and
    // is deliberately not animated — a jump lifts the character, not the ground
    // — so `center.y - root.y` is how high her hips ARE, not how tall she is,
    // and a cut scaled by it climbs faster than she does the moment she leaves
    // the floor. The bounding radius is a size rather than an altitude and says
    // the same thing in the air. 0.71 of it is hips-to-crown on a human build,
    // so RISE keeps reading 0 at the hips and 1 at the top of her head.
    let span = max(s.bounds.w, 0.3);
    let through = s.center + vec3f(0.0, span * 0.71 * params.RISE, 0.0);
    let q = rzProject(through);
    // Behind the camera the projection is meaningless, so that falls back too.
    if (q.z > 0.0) { base = clamp(q.y, 0.05, 0.95); }
  }

  // Eased out and HELD. A curve that kept moving would carry the halves off
  // screen and leave black; this is fast at the cut and asymptotic after.
  let k = 1.0 - exp(-t / max(params.OPEN, 0.01) * 3.0);

  // THE KICK. Something that cuts through space should be felt, and a frame
  // that holds perfectly still through it reads as a transition rather than as
  // an impact.
  //
  // It shakes the LOOKUP, not the halves — everything downstream is computed
  // from `at`, so the cut, both halves and the gap all move together as one
  // picture. Shaking only the sampled content would leave the black gap nailed
  // to the screen while the image jittered behind it, which reads as a fault in
  // the display rather than a blow to the world.
  //
  // Two frequencies per axis, deliberately not related by a whole number: one
  // sine is a wobble, and anything an ear or an eye can count the period of
  // stops being an impact. Decayed hard, so it is gone in under half a second.
  let jolt = exp(-t * JOLT) * params.SHAKE;
  let at = uv + vec2f(
    (sin(t * 97.0) * 0.6 + sin(t * 53.3 + 1.7) * 0.4) * jolt,
    (sin(t * 71.7 + 2.3) * 0.6 + sin(t * 113.0) * 0.4) * jolt
  );

  let cut = cutAt(at.x, base);
  let side = select(-1.0, 1.0, at.y > cut);

  // TWO MOTIONS AT ONCE. They slide PAST each other along the blade, and they
  // also open AWAY from it — the shear is what says a blade went through, and
  // the opening is what says the space it cut is gone. Either alone is weaker:
  // pure shear reads as a printing misregistration, pure opening as a curtain.
  // Each half also turns a little about its own outer edge, which is what gives
  // it weight: two blocks sliding in parallel read as a wipe.
  let pivot = select(0.0, 1.0, side > 0.0);
  let turn = side * params.TILT * k;
  var src = at;
  src.y -= side * params.PART * k;
  src.x += side * params.SLIP * k;
  let dx = src.y - pivot;
  src.x += dx * turn;

  // THE CUT ITSELF. Everything between the two edges is nothing — the space is
  // gone there, and the black is what says the frame is in pieces rather than
  // merely sliding. Measured against the cut in the SOURCE, so the dark line
  // follows the blade's own curve rather than running straight across it.
  let fromCut = (src.y - cut) * side;
  // The cut itself, and anything slid off the edge of frame: the space is gone
  // there, and black is what says so.
  if (fromCut < GAP || src.x < 0.0 || src.x > 1.0 || src.y < 0.0 || src.y > 1.0) {
    return vec4f(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
  }

  // CHROMATIC SPLIT ALONG THE CUT ONLY. A whole-frame separation is a different
  // effect and a worse one; the tell here is the space lensing where it parted,
  // so it falls off within a few pixels of the blade and is gone elsewhere.
  let px = 1.0 / rzResolution().y;
  let edge = exp(-fromCut * 90.0);
  let sep = params.SPLIT * px * edge * side;
  var rgb = rzSceneFrame(src);
  if (sep != 0.0) {
    rgb = vec3f(
      rzSceneFrame(src + vec2f(0.0, sep)).r,
      rgb.g,
      rzSceneFrame(src - vec2f(0.0, sep)).b
    );
  }

  // The landing: one flash, gone in a tenth of a second, brightest along the
  // blade. It is what the eye reads as the strike — the slide alone reads as a
  // transition.
  let flash = exp(-t / FLASH) * (0.35 + 0.65 * edge);
  rgb = mix(rgb, vec3f(1.0), clamp(flash, 0.0, 1.0));

  return vec4f(rgb, 1.0);
}

#anchor 左手首 trail
#anchor 右手首 trail
#lights 2

#param color COLOR #1f9bff — the thread; everything else is derived from it
#param float STRANDS 6 1 12 — how many threads
#param float SPREAD 0.85 0 1 — how far they fray apart behind her hand
#param float WEAVE 5 0 20 — how often they cross, and how fast the pattern travels
#param float WIDTH 0.55 0.1 2 — the bundle's half-width, world units
#param float GLOW 1.1 0 4 — how much light each thread throws around itself
#param float LIFETIME 1.1 0.1 3 — seconds of path drawn behind each hand
#param float LIGHT_POWER 1.6 0 6 — how hard her hands light her

// Hand Threads — strings of light off both wrists, not a ribbon.
//
// SAME MOUNT AS A RIBBON, DIFFERENT PAINT. The engine builds one quad strip per
// trailed anchor and hands the shader where it is along the strip (`u`), where
// it is ACROSS it (`v`) and how old that stretch is. A ribbon puts one bright
// line down the middle of that quad. This puts several thin ones at wandering
// offsets, and the quad becomes a corridor they weave inside rather than a
// surface.
//
// Doing it any other way costs the frame. Threads drawn as their own geometry
// would be a ribbon each; threads found by a fullscreen shader would mean
// walking the trail's samples per pixel, which is a hundred-odd segment tests
// for every pixel on screen. Here the strands cost one gaussian each on the
// pixels the bundle already covers, and nothing anywhere else.
//
// THE WEAVE IS LOCKED TO THE MATERIAL, not to the screen. Each thread's offset
// is a function of `age` — how old that piece of the trail is — so a bend stays
// in the piece of thread it was born in and travels with it as the hand moves
// on. Keyed to `u` instead, the pattern would stand still in space and the
// threads would appear to slide through themselves whenever she moved her arm.

// ONE COLOUR, THREE TONES. A thread is not one colour at every brightness: it
// is deep and saturated in the light it sheds, its own hue through the body, and
// close to white only where strands stack. That is three slots asking a person
// to pick three harmonious colours, when the two they are not choosing follow
// from the one they are.
//
// `c*c/max(c)` deepens without shifting hue — squaring pulls the weaker channels
// down faster than the strong one and dividing by the peak puts the brightness
// back, which is a saturation increase in the only two lines it takes. Mixing
// toward white for the hot end is the same idea in reverse. Both hold for any
// colour dropped in the slot: a red thread deepens to blood and flares to pink,
// with nothing in here knowing it was blue.
fn threadDeep(c: vec3f) -> vec3f {
  let mx = max(c.r, max(c.g, c.b));
  return c * c / max(mx, 1e-4) * 0.6;
}
fn threadHot(c: vec3f) -> vec3f { return mix(c, vec3f(1.0), 0.6); }

const THREAD_FRAC = 0.07; // a thread's half-width, as a fraction of the bundle's
const DRIFT_FRAC = 0.12;  // how fast the weave travels, per unit of WEAVE
const LIGHT_R = 7.0;      // how far a wrist's lamp carries, world units
const TAIL_EASE = 0.12;   // how much of the tail is spent closing the quad
const INTENSITY = 1.9;
const GLOW_W = 3.0;       // the soft tier's width, in thread half-widths
const HEAD_FLARE = 1.6;   // extra brightness where a thread leaves the hand
const GOLDEN = 2.3999632; // radians between strand phases: no two ever in step

fn trailWidth(u: f32, age: f32) -> f32 {
  // The quad is the CORRIDOR, wide enough to hold every thread at full spread.
  // It does not narrow with speed: pinching where the hand slows breaks the
  // bundle at exactly the moments a hand turns, and a turn is where it slows.
  let a = clamp(1.0 - age / params.LIFETIME, 0.0, 1.0);
  return params.WIDTH * smoothstep(0.0, TAIL_EASE, a);
}

fn trailShade(u: f32, v: f32, age: f32, weight: f32, slot: i32) -> vec4f {
  // 1 at the hand, 0 at the end of the LONGEST thread. Individual strands stop
  // earlier; this only decides whether the quad carries anything at all.
  let a = clamp(1.0 - age / params.LIFETIME, 0.0, 1.0);
  if (a <= 0.0) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // THEY LEAVE HER HAND TOGETHER AND COME APART BEHIND IT. Spread is zero at
  // the live end and opens down the tail, so the bundle reads as one source
  // fraying rather than as several lines that happen to start near each other.
  // It opens QUICKLY — held back over half the length, the threads spend most of
  // the trail on top of each other and the whole thing reads as one ribbon with
  // a frayed end.
  let open = smoothstep(0.0, 0.18, 1.0 - a);
  let spread = params.SPREAD * open;
  let base = max(params.WIDTH * THREAD_FRAC, 1e-4);
  let drift = params.WEAVE * DRIFT_FRAC;

  var heat = 0.0;
  var glow = 0.0;
  // The dial is a count, so it is rounded once and the loop bound is uniform.
  let n = max(floor(params.STRANDS + 0.5), 1.0);
  for (var s = 0.0; s < n; s = s + 1.0) {
    let ph = s * GOLDEN;
    // EVERY THREAD ITS OWN. Identical strands read as a generated pattern
    // however well they weave — the eye finds the repeat in the widths long
    // before it finds it in the motion. One hash per strand gives it a
    // thickness, a brightness and a length, fixed for that strand and the same
    // every frame.
    let h = rzHash13(s * 1.37 + 4.1);
    // ITS OWN LENGTH, so the bundle ends ragged. All of them stopping at one
    // age cuts the trail off square, which is the single shape a handful of
    // loose threads never makes.
    let life = params.LIFETIME * mix(0.6, 1.0, h.z);
    let a1 = clamp(1.0 - age / life, 0.0, 1.0);
    if (a1 <= 0.0) { continue; }
    let ease1 = smoothstep(0.0, TAIL_EASE, a1);
    // A thread thins as it ages, which keeps the far end from reading as a
    // widening smear.
    let w = base * mix(0.7, 1.35, h.x) * mix(0.55, 1.0, a1);
    let gw = w * GLOW_W;
    // EACH THREAD HAS ITS OWN LANE. Sharing one wander with different phases was
    // the whole reason this read as a ribbon: two strands whose phases happened
    // to cross sat on the same line, and with six of them something was always
    // crossing, so the bundle spent most of its length collapsed into a band.
    // An even lane across the corridor means they can never all coincide — the
    // wander is then life on top of a separation that is already there, rather
    // than the only thing holding them apart.
    let lane = ((s + 0.5) / n) * 2.0 - 1.0;
    // Two terms, unrelated in frequency: one sine is a braid and reads as
    // machinery. The second is slower and half the height, which is enough to
    // stop the eye finding the period.
    let sway = sin(age * params.WEAVE + ph + rzTime() * drift)
             + 0.5 * sin(age * params.WEAVE * 0.43 + ph * 1.7 - rzTime() * drift * 0.6);
    let off = (lane + sway * 0.3) * spread;
    let d = abs(v - off);
    // Bounded on the WIDE tier, since the tight one is nothing long before it.
    if (d > gw * 2.5) { continue; }
    // THE FLARE AT HER HAND. A thread is brightest where it is being made, and
    // a bundle of even brightness along its whole length reads as drawn rather
    // than as emitted.
    let head = 1.0 + HEAD_FLARE * smoothstep(0.82, 1.0, a1);
    // `weight` is the engine's segment length over a full contribution — the
    // line integral — so a hand that pauses goes dim instead of piling every
    // sample onto one spot and blowing white.
    let lit = a1 * a1 * ease1 * mix(0.65, 1.35, h.y) * head * weight;
    heat += exp(-(d * d) / (w * w)) * lit;
    glow += exp(-(d * d) / (gw * gw)) * lit;
  }
  if (heat <= 0.0 && glow <= 0.0) { return vec4f(0.0); }

  // Colour FROM intensity, so white appears only where threads actually cross
  // and stack. That crossing is the whole reason to draw more than one: two
  // strands meeting is the brightest thing in the effect, and it happens on its
  // own out of the weave rather than being drawn anywhere on purpose.
  let deep = threadDeep(params.COLOR);
  var rgb = mix(deep, params.COLOR, smoothstep(0.05, 0.45, heat));
  rgb = mix(rgb, threadHot(params.COLOR), smoothstep(0.85, 1.9, heat));
  // THE GLOW IS ITS OWN LAYER, under the threads rather than part of them. Put
  // into `heat` it would only have widened them — a thicker thread, not a lit
  // one. Added as haze beneath, the line keeps its edge and the air around it
  // carries the light, which is what the eye reads as glowing.
  let soft = deep * (glow * params.GLOW);
  // COVERAGE BELONGS TO THE THREAD, not to its glow. Alpha decides how much of
  // the frame this claims, so feeding the soft tier in at full strength is what
  // turned six thin lines into one broad band: the light was reading as width.
  let lum = clamp(heat + glow * params.GLOW * 0.25, 0.0, 1.0);
  return vec4f(rgb * INTENSITY + soft, lum);
}

/**
 * A lamp at each wrist, so the threads light her rather than float over her.
 *
 * Two slots and subject 0 only. A light is a declared cost, and covering four
 * dancers would spend eight of them to put a glow on hands that are mostly out
 * of frame.
 */
fn lightEmitTuned(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l: RzLight;
  l.color = params.COLOR;
  l.intensity = 0.0;
  l.radius = 0.0;
  l.pos = vec3f(0.0);

  let a = rzAnchor(0, i32(i));
  if (!a.valid) { return l; }
  l.pos = a.pos;
  l.radius = LIGHT_R;
  // BRIGHTER WHEN SHE MOVES. The threads are drawn by motion — a still hand
  // trails nothing — so a lamp that stayed lit over a motionless hand would be
  // light with no visible source.
  let speed = clamp(length(a.vel) / 6.0, 0.0, 1.0);
  l.intensity = params.LIGHT_POWER * (0.25 + 0.75 * speed);
  return l;
}

// Its lights as tuned on the engine's old curve — brightness at the light, zero
// at the radius — carried onto the inverse square every light now falls off by:
// the same brightness half-way out, brighter nearer, dimmer beyond, in
// Unity's units (no π); the bulb (near 2.5) is where that old curve flattened.
fn lightEmit(i: u32, time: f32) -> RzLight {
  var l = lightEmitTuned(i, time);
  l.intensity = l.intensity * 0.64 * max(0.25 * l.radius * l.radius, 6.25);
  l.near = 2.5;
  return l;
}

#grid 384
#particles 2097152
// Opaque where drawn, with depth. A lawn stacks ten blades deep, and blended
// they are all shaded and all written; with depth the ones behind are
// rejected, and a blade in front of another is in front of it.
#blend cutout

// The joints that press the lawn — the whole body, not the feet. Standing,
// the ankles do nearly all of it; kneeling adds the knees; sitting the hips;
// lying down, everything at once. Each joint counts only while it is near
// the floor, so a raised hand presses nothing. Nine of the scene's sixteen
// anchor slots; the hips come free through rzSubject and cost none.
#anchor 左足首
#anchor 右足首
#anchor 左ひざ
#anchor 右ひざ
#anchor 左手首
#anchor 右手首
#anchor 左ひじ
#anchor 右ひじ
#anchor 頭

// ONE COLOUR SLOT. The dark cool sheath, the warm tip and the straw of a dead
// blade are all derived from it, so a blue-green fescue and a late-summer lawn
// are one swatch apart and neither can be set to a combination that stops
// reading as grass.
#param color GRASS #4f9a34
#param float SIZE 80 10 200 — the plane's radius. A larger plane is MORE grass at the same spacing, up to two million blades; past that the far field thins
#param float SPREAD 3 1 8 — the spacing between blades; 1 is turf, 3 a lawn, 8 a meadow going to seed
#param float WIND 0.45 0 1.5
#param color FLOWER #fb2c36 — the flower most of the field is; the rest are hue variants of it, and white
#param float FLOWERS 0.10 0 0.4 — the share of stems that carry a head

// Grass — blades, not a green field shader.
//
// Three things make a lawn, and this is those three inside the mounts this
// engine has.
//
//   THE BLADE IS GEOMETRY. A curved, tapered ribbon whose normal TURNS across
//   its width, because a blade is a shallow trough and not a flat strip. That
//   curl is why half a lawn is bright and half is dark under one sun, and it
//   is the whole difference between grass and a green carpet with noise on it.
//   Each blade is one particle, and the quad is pointed ALONG the blade by
//   `stretch` — the engine aims a stretched billboard down the velocity, so
//   this file keeps the blade's own axis there and gets a thin oriented
//   rectangle instead of a square with a blade painted in the middle. At 30:1
//   that is the difference between drawing the lawn twice and thirty times.
//
//   LIGHT COMES THROUGH IT. A blade is a tenth of a millimetre thick. Backlit
//   grass GLOWS yellow-green, and no amount of diffuse shading arrives there.
//   The sheen is Kajiya-Kay about the blade's tangent rather than Blinn-Phong
//   about its normal, because a blade is a fibre: its highlight is a streak
//   running the length of it, and a round specular dot is the single most
//   plastic thing that can be put on grass.
//
//   THE INTERACTION PERSISTS. That is the grid. A footfall presses the blades
//   down, HOLDS them while the foot is there, springs back over a second or so
//   once it lifts, and leaves a track behind a walk. None of that is a
//   function of position and time, which is what every other mount is. It is
//   memory, and the grid is the only memory an effect has. Water and Dry Ice
//   use it for the same reason.
//
// What this is not: a terrain system. Blades stand on y = 0 and the lawn is a
// disc about the origin, because a particle cannot ask how high the stage is
// underneath it. It is the patch a character stands in, which is the part
// worth having.

// ── The lawn ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** Blades per tuft. Grass grows in clumps, and a clump is also what HIDES THE
 *  GROUND: the same blades spread evenly show dirt between every one of them,
 *  where gathered into tufts they read as a lawn with gaps in it, which is
 *  what a lawn is. */
const TUFT: f32 = 7.0;
/** Blade height, world units. A model is about 20 tall. */
const BLADE_H: f32 = 2.6;
/** The share of blades gone to straw. */
const DRY: f32 = 0.14;
/** The key light: where it comes from and how high it sits. Not the scene's
 *  sun — a particle cannot read it — but the default sun's own default. */
const SUN_TURN: f32 = 40.0;
const SUN_HIGH: f32 = 0.5;
/** Blades per world unit squared at SPREAD 1. The default spacing of 3 is a
 *  lawn at a ninth of this, 36; the pool covers the dial's low end through the
 *  level of detail below. */
const DENSITY: f32 = 324.0;
/** The pool. Has to match #particles, and nothing checks that it does. An
 *  80-unit lawn at the default spacing takes a third of it; turf takes it
 *  all and then some, which is what the level of detail is for. */
const POOL: f32 = 2097152.0;
/** HDR headroom. These blades are drawn in the scene pass and go through AgX
 *  with everything else, and AgX's shoulder turns a 1.0 white into a grey and
 *  a saturated green into an olive. Anime colour is FLAT and VIVID: the
 *  lighting below is compressed toward its mean, and the result is lifted so
 *  that a white petal is white after the view transform, not #eee. */
const GAIN: f32 = 1.3;
/** Petals want more: white is white after AgX only from well above 1. */
const PETAL_GAIN: f32 = 2.1;
/** What a petal keeps in shadow: the sky's share of its light, about what a
 *  blade keeps once the sun's terms are gone. */
const PETAL_SHADE: f32 = 0.3;
/** The share of a colour's chroma the shade keeps. */
const SHADE_CHROMA: f32 = 0.6;
/** How far the shade leans toward the hue of the scene's ambient. */
const SHADE_TINT: f32 = 0.35;
/** How far the swatch is pushed from grey before shading — anime green is
 *  greener than a real lawn's. */
const VIVID: f32 = 0.45;
/** How far a blade sits from its tuft's centre, in blade heights. */
const TUFT_R: f32 = 0.40;
/** Blade half-width, in blade heights. Real grass is 3-5mm across and 150-250mm
 *  tall, which is where this comes from — measured, not tuned. */
const HALF_W: f32 = 0.036;
/** How far the normal swings from one edge of the blade to the other. */
const CURL: f32 = 1.05;
/** The share of blades folded along the spine rather than troughed. */
const FOLD: f32 = 0.2;
/** Seconds for a trampled blade to stand back up. */
const RECOVER: f32 = 1.45;
/** How wide a joint presses, and how far above the floor it still counts as
 *  touching. The hips are wider — they are the torso — and the head a little
 *  wider than a wrist. */
const PRESS_R: f32 = 3.4;
const PRESS_H: f32 = 2.2;
const JOINTS: i32 = 9;
fn pressRadius(slot: i32) -> f32 {
  if (slot == 8) { return 4.2; }   // head
  return PRESS_R;
}
/** Radians a blade goes over under a full press. */
const PRESS_LEAN: f32 = 1.15;
/** An edge-on blade keeps this fraction of its width rather than vanishing:
 *  quads that reach zero area flicker in and out as the camera turns. */
const EDGE_MIN: f32 = 0.17;
/** How much higher the control point sits per radian of lean — the bow. */
const WRAP: f32 = 0.42;
/** The most the blade's middle may leave its chord, in half-widths. The quad
 *  is as wide as the bow, and the prepass pays for every fragment of it. */
const BOW_MAX: f32 = 0.75;
/** A flower head's radius in stem heights, and how far a stem stands above the
 *  blades around it. */
const HEAD_R: f32 = 0.15;
const STEM_UP: f32 = 1.22;

fn gSpan() -> f32 { return params.SIZE * 2.0; }
fn gWorld(uv: vec2f) -> vec2f { return (uv - vec2f(0.5)) * gSpan(); }
fn gUV(xz: vec2f) -> vec2f { return xz / gSpan() + vec2f(0.5); }

// ── The interaction map ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   rg  which way the blades here have been pushed, and how hard. A vector, so
//       a walk combs the grass forward and a planted foot splays it outward,
//       and the two add instead of fighting over one scalar.
//   b   pressed flat regardless of direction — a heel straight down. A foot
//       standing still holds this at 1 by re-stamping every frame; recovery
//       only gets it back once the foot lifts.

fn gridStep(uv: vec2f, prev: vec4f, dt: f32) -> vec4f {
  // Standing and undisturbed. Unlike fog there IS a correct initial state for
  // a lawn, and it is nothing — every bend you see should be one something made.
  if (rzGridFrame() == 0) { return vec4f(0.0); }
  // One frame per step at most: a backgrounded tab comes back asking for a
  // tenth of a second at once, and a blur term stepped that far overshoots.
  let n = clamp(dt * 60.0, 0.0, 1.0);

  var push = prev.xy;
  var crush = prev.z;

  // Recovery, exponential so it is frame-rate independent.
  let k = 1.0 - exp(-dt / RECOVER);
  push = mix(push, vec2f(0.0), k);
  crush = mix(crush, 0.0, k);

  // One texel of bleed. Without it a footprint edge is a texel staircase; with
  // it the grass beside a track leans into the track rather than stopping dead
  // at its edge.
  let te = rzGridTexel();
  let nb = (rzGridPrev(uv + vec2f(te, 0.0)) + rzGridPrev(uv - vec2f(te, 0.0))
          + rzGridPrev(uv + vec2f(0.0, te)) + rzGridPrev(uv - vec2f(0.0, te))) * 0.25;
  push = mix(push, nb.xy, 0.22 * n);
  crush = mix(crush, nb.z, 0.22 * n);

  let here = gWorld(uv);
  for (var c = 0; c < rzSubjectCount(); c++) {
    let subj = rzSubject(c);
    if (!subj.valid) { continue; }
    // root.y is the model's own floor and is deliberately NOT animated, so a
    // jump lifts the ankles away from it rather than carrying the ground up.
    let floorY = subj.root.y;
    // Slot JOINTS is the hips: not an anchor but the subject's own centre,
    // which every effect gets for free.
    for (var slot = 0; slot <= JOINTS; slot++) {
      var pos = subj.center;
      var vel = vec3f(0.0);
      var pr = 6.0;
      if (slot < JOINTS) {
        let a = rzAnchor(c, slot);
        if (!a.valid) { continue; }
        pos = a.pos;
        vel = a.vel;
        pr = pressRadius(slot);
      }
      // The torso counts from higher up: hips sit a body's width above the
      // floor when she is lying down.
      let reach = select(PRESS_H, PRESS_H * 2.4, slot == JOINTS);
      let touch = clamp(1.0 - (pos.y - floorY) / reach, 0.0, 1.0);
      if (touch <= 0.0) { continue; }
      let d = distance(here, pos.xz);
      if (d > pr) { continue; }
      let amt = touch * (1.0 - smoothstep(pr * 0.3, pr, d));
      if (amt <= 0.001) { continue; }
      // Outward from the joint, plus where it is going. A planted foot splays
      // the grass around itself; a moving one lays it down ahead.
      let radial = select(vec2f(0.0, 1.0), normalize(here - pos.xz), d > 1e-3);
      let want = radial * 0.75 + vel.xz * 0.05;
      // Driven toward a target rather than integrated: a foot that stands
      // still has to HOLD the grass down, and an impulse decays out from
      // underneath it.
      push = mix(push, want, clamp(amt * 14.0 * dt, 0.0, 1.0));
      crush = max(crush, amt);
    }
  }
  return vec4f(clamp(push, vec2f(-2.0), vec2f(2.0)), clamp(crush, 0.0, 1.0), 0.0);
}

// ── One blade ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** Everything about a blade that both stages have to agree on, rebuilt from its
 *  slot index rather than stored.
 *
 *  Stored would be cheaper by a few hashes and WRONG: a blade lives for ever,
 *  so anything captured at birth pins BLADE_H and SIZE to whatever they were
 *  when the effect was installed, and both dials would then move nothing. The
 *  only thing kept in the particle is the index, in `seed`. */
struct Blade {
  root: vec3f,
  height: f32,
  /** A stem with a head instead of a blade. Decided by the index, so both
   *  stages agree without a word passing between them. */
  flower: bool,
  /** Which way the flat of the blade faces, as a world yaw. Independent of the
   *  lean, and the reason a lawn is variegated rather than uniform: under one
   *  sun, blades turned away from it are the dark ones. */
  yaw: f32,
  /** This blade's own randomness, 0..1 — colour, phase, everything per-blade. */
  rnd: f32,
  /** Width multiplier for the outer field, where blades are fewer. */
  broad: f32,
}

/** How many blades the field asks for: its area at the asked spacing. Blade
 *  positions are hashed from the index over the whole disc, so any prefix of
 *  indices is a uniform field — and growing SIZE adds blades rather than
 *  spreading the same ones thinner. */
fn wantedCount() -> f32 {
  let area = 3.14159265 * params.SIZE * params.SIZE;
  return area * DENSITY / max(params.SPREAD * params.SPREAD, 0.05);
}

/** How many of the pool the engine steps and draws this frame. The live
 *  blades are the first `wanted` slots, so a lawn at the default spacing
 *  costs a third of the pool and the rest costs nothing — not a read, not a
 *  write, not a degenerate quad. */
fn particleCount() -> u32 {
  return u32(min(wantedCount(), POOL));
}

// ── Level of detail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The pool is two million blades and a field of radius 80 at the default
// spacing wants 720k of them, so a much bigger field or a much tighter
// spacing asks for more than exist. Spread
// evenly over the whole disc, the shortfall thins the field EVERYWHERE, and
// both dials go dead at once. But the far field does not need its blades: at
// a hundred units a blade is one pixel wide, and four of them one pixel wide
// look exactly like one of them four pixels wide.
//
// So when the field wants K times the pool, every slot is given K candidate
// positions over the disc and draws the one NEAREST THE CAMERA, among those
// IN FRAME. A candidate behind the camera or off to the side never beats one
// the camera can see, so the pool is spent on the ground in the picture and
// nowhere else. Near the camera nearly every candidate is the nearest in its
// slot, and the field is at full density; far away most lose to a nearer one,
// and the field thins. The survivors there are widened by the thinning, so
// coverage holds. The total is exactly the pool with nothing counted.
//
// A slot keeps the candidate it drew last frame unless a challenger is
// clearly nearer or the old one has left the frame: without that, a pan
// swaps blades in the middle distance every frame, and that is sparkle.

/** The most candidates a slot considers. At SPREAD 1 and SIZE 200 the field
 *  wants twenty pools; past this many the far field just gets thinner. */
const LOD_MAX: i32 = 16;
/** How wide a far blade may grow to cover for its missing neighbours. Past
 *  this a blade is a leaf; the far field is allowed to thin instead. */
const LOD_BROAD: f32 = 1.5;
/** How much nearer a challenger has to be to take a slot from the blade it
 *  drew last frame. */
const LOD_HOLD: f32 = 0.8;
/** How far past the frame's edge a candidate still counts as in frame, in
 *  frame widths, so blades are standing when a pan brings them in. Every
 *  tenth here is pool spent on blades nobody sees: at 0.3 a side, more than
 *  half the pool stood outside the picture. */
const LOD_EDGE: f32 = 0.12;

fn candidateCount() -> i32 {
  return clamp(i32(ceil(wantedCount() / POOL)), 1, LOD_MAX);
}

/** Where a tuft stands: on an area-uniform disc, sqrt(u) putting as many tufts
 *  in an outer ring as the ring has room for, where a linear radius crowds them
 *  into the middle and leaves the rim bald. */
fn tuftCentre(tuft: f32) -> vec2f {
  let ta = rzHash21(vec2f(tuft, 17.31)) * 6.2831853;
  let tr = sqrt(rzHash21(vec2f(tuft, 91.77)));
  return vec2f(cos(ta), sin(ta)) * tr * params.SIZE;
}

/** The candidate this slot draws this frame, and how much wider it stands.
 *  `slot` is the index modulo the pool; the candidates are slot + k·POOL, and
 *  `current` is the one drawn last frame. */
struct Pick {
  index: u32,
  broad: f32,
  /** The tuft's centre, so the blade need not hash it again. */
  centre: vec2f,
  /** In frame. A blade that is not costs the step nothing past this. */
  seen: bool,
}

fn pickCandidate(slot: f32, current: f32) -> Pick {
  var o: Pick;
  let K = candidateCount();
  let cam = rzCameraPos().xz;
  let wanted = wantedCount();
  var best = slot;
  var bestD = 1e9;
  var bestC = vec2f(0.0);
  var curD = 1e9;
  var curC = vec2f(0.0);
  for (var k = 0; k < K; k++) {
    let j = slot + f32(k) * POOL;
    if (j >= wanted && k > 0) { break; }
    let c = tuftCentre(floor(j / TUFT));
    // Out of frame counts as a world away, so it loses to anything in frame.
    // The margin grows with the blade's own height on screen: a blade a few
    // units from the camera reaches a good way into the frame from a tuft
    // centre outside it, and one leaning in must not vanish at the edge.
    let p0 = rzProject(vec3f(c.x, 0.0, c.y));
    let p1 = rzProject(vec3f(c.x, BLADE_H * STEM_UP, c.y));
    let m = LOD_EDGE + 1.5 * distance(p0.xy, p1.xy);
    let seen = p0.z > 0.0 && all(p0.xy > vec2f(-m)) && all(p0.xy < vec2f(1.0 + m));
    let d = distance(c, cam) + select(1.0e6, 0.0, seen);
    if (j == current) { curD = d; curC = c; }
    if (d < bestD) { bestD = d; best = j; bestC = c; }
  }
  if (curD < 1.0e6 && bestD > curD * LOD_HOLD) { best = current; bestD = curD; bestC = curC; }
  o.index = u32(best);
  o.centre = bestC;
  o.seen = bestD < 1.0e6;
  o.broad = 1.0;
  if (K > 1) {
    // The odds this blade won: every other candidate of its slot stood further
    // out, within the frame. The frame's ground is a wedge from the camera,
    // so the share of it nearer than d goes as d squared over the wedge's
    // reach. The width makes up for the candidates that lost — count times
    // width is what the eye reads as coverage at that distance.
    let reach = length(cam) + params.SIZE;
    let f = min(bestD / reach, 1.0);
    let win = pow(1.0 - f * f, f32(K - 1));
    o.broad = clamp(inverseSqrt(max(win, 1e-4)), 1.0, LOD_BROAD);
  }
  return o;
}

/** Whether a stem carries a head, and what kind.
 *
 *  Flowers grow in COLONIES. Decided per tuft and then per stem, so heads come
 *  in twos and threes with grass between the groups rather than one every
 *  seventh blade, which is a sprinkle. The colour still varies stem by stem.
 *  Both stages call this from the index, so they cannot disagree. */
struct Head {
  flower: bool,
}

fn headOf(i: u32) -> Head {
  var h: Head;
  let bi = f32(i);
  let tuft = floor(bi / TUFT);
  // FLOWERS is still the share of stems: half the stems of a flowering tuft.
  // Most tufts are grass, and grass pays one hash here and leaves.
  h.flower = false;
  if (rzHash21(vec2f(tuft, 733.1)) >= min(params.FLOWERS * 2.0, 1.0)) { return h; }
  h.flower = rzHash21(vec2f(bi, 733.1)) < 0.5;
  return h;
}

fn bladeOf(i: u32) -> Blade {
  return bladeAt(i, tuftCentre(floor(f32(i) / TUFT)));
}

/** The blade, given its tuft's centre — the step has that in hand from the
 *  pick, and hashing it twice a frame for half a million blades is a cost. */
fn bladeAt(i: u32, centre: vec2f) -> Blade {
  var b: Blade;
  let bi = f32(i);
  b.rnd = rzHash11(bi * 0.61803 + 4.17);
  if (bi >= wantedCount()) {
    b.height = 0.0;
    b.root = vec3f(0.0, -1000.0, 0.0);
    b.broad = 1.0;
    b.flower = false;
    return b;
  }

  let tr = length(centre) / max(params.SIZE, 1e-3);
  // The outer third is broadened a little: it is seen from further away, and
  // a wider blade there holds its coverage at a glance for the same count.
  b.broad = 1.0 + 0.6 * smoothstep(0.65, 1.0, tr);

  let oa = rzHash21(vec2f(bi, 3.77)) * 6.2831853;
  let od = sqrt(rzHash21(vec2f(bi, 55.13)));
  // A tuft is a tuft at any spacing: SPREAD changes how many there are, never
  // how tight one is. Scaled by the spacing, a dense field knotted every
  // clump into one blade-wide bunch with the gaps between clumps untouched.
  let xz = centre + vec2f(cos(oa), sin(oa)) * od * TUFT_R * BLADE_H;

  b.root = vec3f(xz.x, 0.0, xz.y);
  // A tuft is not a hedge: the middle blades of a clump are the tall ones,
  // which is what gives a clump its dome.
  let dome = 1.0 - 0.42 * od;
  // The rim fades by getting SHORTER, not fainter. A blade that shrinks to
  // nothing leaves a degenerate quad the rasteriser drops, which is both the
  // softer edge and the cheaper one.
  let rim = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.78, 1.0, length(xz) / max(params.SIZE, 1e-3));
  b.height = BLADE_H * dome * rim * (0.62 + 0.76 * rzHash21(vec2f(bi, 129.4))) * (0.85 + 0.15 * b.broad);
  b.yaw = rzHash21(vec2f(bi, 7.91)) * 6.2831853;
  b.flower = headOf(i).flower;
  if (b.flower) { b.height *= STEM_UP; }
  return b;
}

/** The blade's lean as a world vector in the ground plane, whose LENGTH is the
 *  lean angle in radians. Wind and trample add here, so a gust crossing a
 *  footprint bends the flattened grass without either one winning outright. */
fn bladeLean(b: Blade, t: f32) -> vec2f {
  // A gust is a WAVE crossing the lawn, not noise per blade. Grass moves in
  // sheets; blades swaying independently read as a screensaver.
  let wd = vec2f(0.8763, 0.4818);
  let phase = dot(b.root.xz, wd) * 0.055 - t * 1.3;
  let sheet = (0.5 + 0.5 * sin(phase)) * 0.72 + (0.5 + 0.5 * sin(phase * 2.71 + 1.9)) * 0.28;
  // Flutter is per-blade and fast, and it is SCALED BY the gust: still air is
  // still, which is the detail that sells the gust when it arrives.
  let flutter = sin(t * 6.4 + b.rnd * 41.0) * 0.075 * (0.25 + sheet);
  let blow = params.WIND * (0.16 + 0.84 * sheet) + flutter;

  let g = rzGrid(gUV(b.root.xz));
  let v = wd * blow + g.xy * PRESS_LEAN;
  let mag = length(v);
  // Crushed flat sets a FLOOR under the lean rather than adding to it, so it
  // cannot stack with a gust into a blade bent past its own root. Capped just
  // under a right angle: at exactly 90 degrees the chord is horizontal and the
  // tangent frame loses its sign.
  let theta = min(max(mag, g.z * 1.3), 1.5);
  let axis = select(wd, v / max(mag, 1e-4), mag > 1e-4);
  return axis * theta;
}

fn particleInit(i: u32, seed: f32) -> Particle {
  var p: Particle;
  // THE INDEX, not a random number. Everything random about a blade is hashed
  // out of it, which is what makes an export frame-identical to the preview.
  // The step may move it among the slot's candidates (see pickCandidate);
  // the slot itself is always the index modulo the pool.
  p.seed = f32(i);
  // A blade does not die: every property is rebuilt from the index each step,
  // so there is nothing a respawn would refresh.
  p.life = 1.0e9;
  p.age = 0.0;
  p.pos = bladeOf(i).root;
  p.size = 0.0;
  p.rot = 0.0;
  p.stretch = 0.0;
  p.vel = vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
  return p;
}

/**
 * Stand the blade up, bend it, and fit the quad to it.
 *
 * The particle's fields are doing double duty here, and each for a stated
 * reason rather than to save space:
 *
 *   vel      the blade's chord direction. The engine only reads its DIRECTION
 *            when aiming a stretched billboard, so its LENGTH is free, and it
 *            carries the blade's height with the crush packed above it (see
 *            packVel). The height is what the shadow sample needs, and
 *            carrying it is eight hashes a pixel the shading stage no longer
 *            spends rebuilding the blade.
 *   rot      ignored entirely while stretched. It carries the bow: how far the
 *            blade's middle leaves its own chord, in quad widths and signed —
 *            plus 2 for a stem with a head, so |rot| > 1 is the flower flag.
 *   size     half the quad's width — the blade's drawn half-width PLUS the bow,
 *            so the curve never leaves the quad. That makes the blade's own
 *            half-width `1 - abs(rot)` in quad units, which is how the shading
 *            stage gets it back for nothing.
 *   stretch  the quad's half-LENGTH in units of size, measured on SCREEN: a
 *            blade seen end-on is short, and giving the quad its full world
 *            length would stand a foreshortened blade back up.
 */
fn particleStep(pIn: Particle, dt: f32) -> Particle {
  var p = pIn;
  let slot = p.seed - POOL * floor(p.seed / POOL);
  // A slot past what the field wants is the pool's spare, and costs one
  // compare. At the default two thirds of the pool leaves here.
  if (slot >= wantedCount()) { p.seed = slot; p.size = 0.0; return p; }
  let pick = pickCandidate(slot, p.seed);
  p.seed = f32(pick.index);
  // Off screen: nothing below is worth computing, and this is where most of
  // the field is from any camera that is looking at her.
  if (!pick.seen) { p.size = 0.0; return p; }
  var b = bladeAt(pick.index, pick.centre);
  b.broad *= pick.broad;
  if (b.height <= 1e-4) { p.size = 0.0; return p; }

  let lean = bladeLean(b, rzTime());
  let theta = length(lean);
  let over = select(vec2f(0.0, 0.0), lean / max(theta, 1e-5), theta > 1e-5);

  // The blade as a quadratic Bezier: root, a control point straight above it, and a
  // tip laid over by theta. Straight above is what makes it stiff at the base
  // and loose at the tip, which is how a blade actually bends — a circular arc
  // bends evenly and reads like wire.
  let h = b.height;
  let root = b.root;
  let tip = root + vec3f(over.x * sin(theta), cos(theta), over.y * sin(theta)) * h;
  let chord = tip - root;
  // Pulled a third of the way toward the chord's midpoint. Straight above the
  // root is the truest shape and also the widest bow, and the quad has to be
  // as wide as the bow: this keeps the tip looser than the base and takes a
  // third off every blade's fragment count.
  let above = root + vec3f(0.0, h * (0.5 + WRAP * theta * 0.22), 0.0);
  let ctrl = mix(above, root + chord * 0.5, 0.34);

  let clen = max(length(chord), 1e-5);
  let axis = chord / clen;
  // B(t) - lerp(root, tip, t) is exactly 2t(1-t) * D, so D is the entire bow
  // and its peak is D/2 at the midpoint. Only the part across the chord bows;
  // the part along it just slides the parametrisation.
  let bow3 = ctrl - (root + tip) * 0.5;
  let across = bow3 - axis * dot(bow3, axis);

  // Cull before the trigonometry: a blade behind the camera or well outside
  // the frame costs nothing but this projection.
  let mid = root + chord * 0.5 + across * 0.5;
  let pr = rzProject(mid);
  if (pr.z <= 0.0 || pr.x < -0.25 || pr.x > 1.25 || pr.y < -0.25 || pr.y > 1.25) {
    p.size = 0.0;
    return p;
  }

  // The camera basis the engine will billboard in, and the quad's own axes
  // inside it: the long one down the chord's screen direction, the short one
  // across it. Both have to be computed here, because the shading stage is
  // handed a quad and not the reasoning behind it.
  let cr = rzCameraRight();
  let cu = rzCameraUp();
  let scr = vec2f(dot(axis, cr), dot(axis, cu));
  let slen = length(scr);
  if (slen < 1e-4) { p.size = 0.0; return p; }
  let sdir = scr / slen;
  let wide = cr * sdir.y - cu * sdir.x;

  // The flat of the blade, made square to the chord, and the width direction
  // that follows from it. A blade turned edge-on to the camera is a sliver:
  // that is the foreshortening, and it is most of why a lawn does not look
  // like rows of identical cut-outs.
  let face0 = vec3f(sin(b.yaw), 0.0, cos(b.yaw));
  let face = normalize(face0 - axis * dot(face0, axis));
  let side = cross(axis, face);
  let fore = max(EDGE_MIN, abs(dot(side, wide)));
  // A stem's quad is as wide as its head, and `1 - abs(rot)` hands the shading
  // stage the head radius in quad units by the same arithmetic that hands it
  // a blade's half-width.
  let halfW = select(HALF_W * h * fore * b.broad, HEAD_R * h, b.flower);

  // The bow is capped at a fraction of the blade's width. The quad has to be
  // as wide as the bow, and an uncapped bow is twice the blade's width for an
  // ordinary lean: three quarters of the quad empty, rasterised ten blades
  // deep. Seen from above an upright blade's quad was a square with a sliver
  // in it. The curve this loses is a few degrees at the middle of the blade.
  let bow = clamp(dot(across, wide) * 0.5, -halfW * BOW_MAX, halfW * BOW_MAX);
  let size = halfW + abs(bow);

  p.pos = root + chord * 0.5;
  p.size = size;
  // The flower flag rides two units above the bow: the shading stages then
  // know a stem from a blade without a hash per fragment.
  p.rot = bow / size + select(0.0, 2.0, b.flower);
  p.stretch = max(1.05, (clen * slen * 0.5) / size);
  p.vel = axis * packVel(h, rzGrid(gUV(root.xz)).z);
  return p;
}

/** The length of `vel`: the height, under 8, with the crush in eighths stacked
 *  on top. The crush only dims the base of a trampled blade, and eight steps
 *  of that is more than the eye separates. */
const PACK: f32 = 8.0;
fn packVel(height: f32, crush: f32) -> f32 {
  return min(height, PACK - 0.01) + PACK * floor(crush * 7.0 + 0.5);
}
fn unpackHeight(len: f32) -> f32 { return len - PACK * floor(len / PACK); }
fn unpackCrush(len: f32) -> f32 { return floor(len / PACK) / 7.0; }

// ── Shading ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** A rotation about the grey axis: the hue moves, the brightness does not. */
fn hueTurn(c: vec3f, a: f32) -> vec3f {
  let k = vec3f(0.57735027);
  let cs = cos(a);
  return clamp(c * cs + cross(k, c) * sin(a) + k * dot(k, c) * (1.0 - cs), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));
}

/**
 * A meadow is not one flower, and it is not a random one either.
 *
 * FLOWER is the flower MOST of the field is — half the heads, each turned a
 * few degrees so they are not one stamp. The rest are its neighbours on the
 * wheel, at fixed offsets either side, and one in eight is white. Pink gives
 * rose, violet, coral and white; blue gives blue, lavender, teal and white.
 * One swatch chooses the field; the field is still a field.
 */
fn flowerColor(pick: f32, jit: f32) -> vec3f {
  let base = params.FLOWER;
  var turn = (jit - 0.5) * 0.28;
  if (pick > 0.86) { return vec3f(1.0); }
  if (pick > 0.74) { turn = turn + 1.05; }
  else if (pick > 0.62) { turn = turn - 0.85; }
  else if (pick > 0.50) { turn = turn + 0.45; }
  return hueTurn(base, turn) * (0.92 + 0.12 * jit);
}

/** Widest a little above the sheath, tapering to a point. The `s*s*s` keeps
 *  the blade near full width for its lower two thirds and then closes it
 *  quickly, which is the profile of a grass blade rather than of a triangle. */
fn bladeTaper(s: f32) -> f32 {
  return pow(max(0.0, 1.0 - s * s * s), 0.42) * (0.55 + 0.45 * smoothstep(0.0, 0.10, s));
}

/** What a fragment of the quad is: blade, stem, petal, eye, or nothing. The
 *  prepass wants only `cover`; the shading pass wants the rest. One function
 *  so the two passes cannot disagree about where the ink is — the shading
 *  pass draws at depth EQUAL to what the prepass wrote, and a shape that
 *  differed by a texel would leave holes. */
struct Shape {
  cover: f32,
  petal: f32,
  eye: f32,
  /** 0 root .. 1 tip, along the chord. */
  s: f32,
  /** Across the blade from its spine, in quad units, and the half-width there. */
  dx: f32,
  halfq: f32,
  flower: bool,
}

fn bladeShape(p: Particle, uv: vec2f) -> Shape {
  var o: Shape;
  // Taken first and unconditionally: a derivative inside a branch is undefined,
  // and this is the pixel footprint every edge below is softened by.
  let aa = max(fwidth(uv.x) * 1.4, 1e-5);

  let q = uv * 2.0 - 1.0;
  let s = clamp(q.y * 0.5 + 0.5, 0.0, 1.0);
  let idx = u32(p.seed);
  let flower = abs(p.rot) > 1.0;
  let bow = p.rot - select(0.0, 2.0 * sign(p.rot), flower);
  // 4s(1-s) peaks at exactly 1, so `bow` is the deviation at its widest and
  // the curve is guaranteed inside the quad it was sized against.
  let spine = 4.0 * s * (1.0 - s) * bow;
  // For a blade, its half-width; for a stem, its head's radius. The stem itself
  // is a fixed fraction of the head and does not taper — a stalk is a stalk.
  let wq = 1.0 - abs(bow);
  let halfq = max(select(wq * bladeTaper(s), wq * 0.13 * smoothstep(1.0, 0.94, s), flower), 1e-5);

  let dx = q.x - spine;
  var cover = 1.0 - smoothstep(halfq - aa, halfq + aa, abs(dx));

  // ── The head ──
  // A disc at the top of the stem. The quad's y unit is `stretch` times its x
  // unit, so a circle in the world is a circle here only after that is undone.
  var head = 0.0;
  var petal = 0.0;
  if (flower) {
    let ry = wq / max(p.stretch, 1.0);
    let cy = 1.0 - ry;
    let d2 = vec2f(dx, (q.y - cy) / max(ry, 1e-5) * wq);
    let ang = atan2(d2.y, d2.x);
    let rr = length(d2) / max(wq, 1e-5);
    // Five petals: the rosette r = a + b|cos(n/2 theta)|, closed at a small
    // yellow disc. The petal count is hashed per stem so a bed is not a stamp.
    let n = 5.0 + floor(rzHash21(vec2f(f32(idx), 88.3)) * 3.0);
    let lobe = 0.52 + 0.48 * pow(abs(cos(n * 0.5 * ang + rzHash21(vec2f(f32(idx), 9.1)) * 6.28)), 0.55);
    let aah = aa / max(wq, 1e-5);
    petal = 1.0 - smoothstep(lobe - aah, lobe + aah, rr);
    head = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.30 - aah, 0.30 + aah, rr);
    cover = max(cover, petal);
  }
  o.cover = cover;
  o.petal = petal;
  o.eye = head;
  o.s = s;
  o.dx = dx;
  o.halfq = halfq;
  o.flower = flower;
  return o;
}

/** The cutout's prepass: the shape alone, to depth. See #blend cutout. */
fn particleCover(p: Particle, uv: vec2f) -> f32 {
  return bladeShape(p, uv).cover;
}

fn particleShade(p: Particle, uv: vec2f) -> vec4f {
  let shape = bladeShape(p, uv);
  // No discard: this pass draws at depth EQUAL to the prepass, and a fragment
  // the prepass rejected fails that test on its own. A discard here would
  // cost the pass the hardware's early rejection, which is the whole point.
  let cover = shape.cover;
  let petal = shape.petal;
  let head = shape.eye;
  let s = shape.s;
  let dx = shape.dx;
  let halfq = shape.halfq;
  let flower = shape.flower;
  let bow = p.rot - select(0.0, 2.0 * sign(p.rot), flower);
  let idx = u32(p.seed);

  // ── The frame ──
  let yaw = rzHash21(vec2f(f32(idx), 7.91)) * 6.2831853;
  let rnd = rzHash11(f32(idx) * 0.61803 + 4.17);
  let vlen = length(p.vel);
  let crush = unpackCrush(vlen);
  let axis = p.vel / max(vlen, 1e-5);

  let face0 = vec3f(sin(yaw), 0.0, cos(yaw));
  let face = normalize(face0 - axis * dot(face0, axis));
  let side = cross(axis, face);
  // The tangent follows the bow: the blade's middle runs steeper than its
  // chord and its tip runs flatter, which is what a lengthwise highlight
  // needs in order to travel up the blade rather than sit on it.
  let tang = normalize(axis + side * (bow * (2.0 - 4.0 * s) * 0.55));
  // ACROSS the blade the normal turns, because a blade is a shallow trough.
  // This single term is what stops a lawn reading as a field of flat cards.
  let across = clamp(dx / halfq, -1.0, 1.0);
  // One blade in five is FOLDED along its spine instead of troughed: the two
  // halves face different ways and meet at a crease, one side lit, one not.
  let bend = select(CURL * across, CURL * 0.85 * sign(across), rnd < FOLD);
  let nrm = normalize(face * cos(bend) + side * sin(bend));

  // ── The light ──
  let sa = radians(SUN_TURN);
  let sy = clamp(SUN_HIGH, 0.05, 1.0);
  let sr = sqrt(max(0.0, 1.0 - sy * sy));
  let sun = normalize(vec3f(cos(sa) * sr, sy, sin(sa) * sr));
  let eye = normalize(p.pos - rzCameraPos());
  // The scene's shadow — the cast's and a stage's — where this fragment sits
  // on the blade. The chord is exactly the blade's height long (the tip is
  // laid over, never stretched), so a tip can stand in sun above a shaded
  // root. Shadow takes the SUN away: its diffuse, its sheen and the light
  // through the blade. The sky and the bounce stay.
  let sunlit = rzShadow(p.pos + axis * (unpackHeight(vlen) * (s - 0.5)));

  let ndl = dot(nrm, sun);
  // Wrapped, because a blade is thin enough that its unlit side is never
  // black — and then compressed toward its mean: a cel lawn has a light side
  // and a dark side, not a gradient between them.
  let dif = 0.55 + 0.45 * clamp((ndl + 0.42) / 1.42, 0.0, 1.0);

  // Kajiya-Kay about the TANGENT. A blade is a fibre: its highlight is a
  // streak down its length, and a round Blinn lobe is the most plastic thing
  // that can be put on grass.
  let hv = normalize(sun - eye);
  let tdh = dot(tang, hv);
  let sheen = pow(max(0.0, 1.0 - tdh * tdh), 22.0) * (0.18 + 0.82 * s) * smoothstep(-0.25, 0.15, ndl);

  // Light THROUGH the blade — strongest looking into the sun, and stronger at
  // the thin tip than at the sheath. This is the term that reads as real.
  let back = pow(clamp(dot(eye, -sun), 0.0, 1.0), 3.0);
  let thru = back * (0.28 + 0.72 * s) * clamp(0.5 - ndl * 0.5, 0.0, 1.0);

  // Sky above, and the blade's own neighbours in the way of it. The floor of
  // the occlusion is HIGH on purpose: a lawn's base is dim green, and driving
  // it toward black reads as a hole rather than as depth.
  let sky = mix(0.55, 1.0, nrm.y * 0.5 + 0.5);
  var ao = mix(0.60, 1.0, smoothstep(0.0, 0.42, s));
  ao *= mix(1.0, 0.82, crush);

  // ── The colour, all of it out of one swatch ──
  let lum0 = dot(params.GRASS, vec3f(0.3, 0.6, 0.1));
  let base = clamp(params.GRASS + (params.GRASS - vec3f(lum0)) * VIVID, vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.5));
  let lum = dot(base, vec3f(0.3, 0.6, 0.1));
  let sheath = base * 0.48 + vec3f(0.0, 0.01, 0.02);
  let tipc = mix(base, vec3f(lum * 1.45 + 0.20, lum * 1.30 + 0.16, lum * 0.42), 0.42);
  var col = mix(sheath, tipc, s * s * 0.8 + s * 0.2);
  // Tone varies by TUFT, with a little left per blade. A lawn is clumps that
  // differ — one bluer, one yellower, one darker — and blades varying on
  // their own inside a clump is salt and pepper.
  let tt = rzHash21(vec2f(floor(f32(idx) / TUFT), 271.9));
  col *= (0.82 + 0.36 * tt) * (0.94 + 0.12 * rnd);
  col *= mix(vec3f(0.94, 1.0, 1.06), vec3f(1.06, 1.0, 0.92), fract(tt * 7.31));
  let straw = vec3f(lum * 1.62 + 0.16, lum * 1.34 + 0.11, lum * 0.50 + 0.03);
  col = mix(col, straw, step(rzHash21(vec2f(f32(idx), 401.7)), DRY) * (0.4 + 0.5 * s));

  let thruc = vec3f(lum * 1.75 + 0.10, lum * 1.95 + 0.20, lum * 0.55);
  // Bounce is green: what lights the base of a blade is the lawn around it.
  let bounce = base * 0.30 * (1.0 - s);
  // The sky term is neutral rather than blue: a blue ambient greys a green.
  var lit = col * (dif * sunlit * vec3f(1.0, 0.98, 0.92) + sky * vec3f(0.30, 0.32, 0.30) + bounce);
  lit += thruc * thru * sunlit * 1.35;
  lit += vec3f(1.0, 0.98, 0.90) * sheen * sunlit * 0.55;
  lit *= ao * GAIN;

  if (flower) {
    // Petals are matte and mostly lit by the sky; the eye is a warm yellow
    // derived from the petal colour's brightness so a blue flower still has a
    // convincing centre.
    let fl = flowerColor(rzHash21(vec2f(f32(idx), 51.7)), rzHash21(vec2f(f32(idx), 63.9)));
    let flum = dot(fl, vec3f(0.3, 0.6, 0.1));
    let eye_c = vec3f(flum * 0.95 + 0.10, flum * 0.72 + 0.05, flum * 0.12);
    let head_c = mix(fl, eye_c, head);
    // Petals are cel-flat: a touch of the sun for the dish, and the gain.
    let flit = head_c * (0.90 + 0.14 * clamp(dot(nrm, sun) * 0.5 + 0.5, 0.0, 1.0)) * PETAL_GAIN * mix(PETAL_SHADE, 1.0, sunlit);
    lit = mix(lit, flit, petal);
  }

  // ── The shade's colour ──
  // The shade keeps part of a colour's chroma and leans toward the hue of the
  // scene's ambient, the colour her own shaded side takes. AgX keeps a dark
  // colour saturated and washes a bright one toward white, so a pastel petal
  // darkened at full chroma turns coral and a lemon one mustard. The hue is
  // scaled by its brightest channel, so the lean only ever takes light away.
  let amb = rzWorldAmbient(vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 0.0));
  let ambTop = max(max(amb.x, amb.y), amb.z);
  let hue = select(vec3f(1.0), amb / max(ambTop, 1e-4), ambTop > 1e-4);
  let shadeCol = mix(vec3f(dot(lit, vec3f(0.3, 0.6, 0.1))), lit, SHADE_CHROMA) * mix(vec3f(1.0), hue, SHADE_TINT);
  lit = mix(shadeCol, lit, sunlit);

  // Depth by shading as well: the cutout sorts the blades, and the far half
  // of the lawn still has to LOOK further away.
  let far = smoothstep(0.0, params.SIZE * 2.4, distance(p.pos, rzCameraPos()));
  lit = mix(lit, lit * vec3f(0.80, 0.88, 1.0) * 0.86, far * 0.35);

  return vec4f(lit, cover);
}

#anchor 左手首
#anchor 右手首
#particles 768

#param color FLOWER #e8559a — the flower most of them are; the rest are its neighbours, and white, cream and yellow
#param float SIZE 0.2 0.05 0.6 — a head's radius, world units
#param float SPEED 1.6 0.2 6 — world units a second, leaving the hand
#param float LIFE 1.5 0.3 3 — seconds in the air

// Hand Blossoms — flowers streaming from her hands.
//
// Hand Sparks' emitter with flowers for sparks: a STEADY stream off each
// wrist, thrown gently in a cone the way the bone points, carrying a little
// of the hand's own motion, then braked by the air and falling to a slow
// settle — swaying, turning over, gone in a second or two. Steady is the
// point: the pool refills at its own rate whether the hand moves or not, so
// a held-out hand pours a soft, even stream, and a sweep lays the same
// stream along an arc.
//
// The heads are the Field of Flowers' own rosettes, so a scene wearing both
// is one flower in two places. Drawn as matter, alpha and no bloom — a petal
// that glows is a firefly.

const CONE: f32 = 0.9;         // half-angle of the spray, radians — a wide, soft fan
const SPEED_VAR: f32 = 0.6;    // how much the leaving speed varies, head to head
const INHERIT: f32 = 0.25;     // how much of the hand's own motion they carry
const GRAVITY: f32 = 2.0;
const DRAG: f32 = 2.4;         // gravity over drag is the settle: ~0.8 units a second
const SWAY: f32 = 0.3;
const TUMBLE: f32 = 1.4;
const LIFE_VAR: f32 = 0.5;
const GAIN: f32 = 2.1;         // HDR headroom — white is white after AgX only above 1

/** A rotation about the grey axis: the hue moves, the brightness does not. */
fn hueTurn(c: vec3f, a: f32) -> vec3f {
  let k = vec3f(0.57735027);
  let cs = cos(a);
  return clamp(c * cs + cross(k, c) * sin(a) + k * dot(k, c) * (1.0 - cs), vec3f(0.0), vec3f(1.0));
}

/** The Field of Flowers' palette: FLOWER for most, its deeper and warmer
 *  neighbours beside it, and the meadow's own white, cream and yellow. */
fn flowerColor(pick: f32, jit: f32) -> vec3f {
  let base = params.FLOWER;
  let turn = (jit - 0.5) * 0.16;
  if (pick > 0.88) { return vec3f(1.0, 0.86, 0.30) * (0.94 + 0.06 * jit); }
  if (pick > 0.76) { return vec3f(1.0, 0.96, 0.78); }
  if (pick > 0.58) { return vec3f(1.0); }
  if (pick > 0.48) { return hueTurn(base, turn - 0.30) * 0.86; }
  if (pick > 0.38) { return mix(hueTurn(base, turn + 0.35), vec3f(1.0, 0.6, 0.45), 0.35); }
  return hueTurn(base, turn) * (0.94 + 0.10 * jit);
}

fn particleInit(id: u32, seed: f32) -> Particle {
  var p: Particle;
  let r = rzHash13(seed + f32(id) * 0.0211);
  let r2 = rzHash13(seed * 2.17 + f32(id) * 0.0473);

  // Either hand, either character, chosen per head, so both wrists draw from
  // one pool and neither is given half of it up front.
  let slot = select(0, 1, r.z > 0.5);
  let nSubjects = max(1, rzSubjectCount());
  var subject = i32(r2.x * f32(nSubjects)) % nSubjects;
  if (!rzAnchor(subject, slot).valid) { subject = 0; }
  let a = rzAnchor(subject, slot);
  // No such bone on this rig: park it dead rather than pouring from the
  // world origin.
  if (!a.valid) {
    p.life = 0.0;
    p.pos = vec3f(0.0, -1000.0, 0.0);
    return p;
  }
  p.pos = a.pos;

  // A cone along the bone, uniform in cos(theta) so the rim of the fan is as
  // full as its axis. The bone's own forward turns the fan with her wrist.
  var axis = a.fwd;
  if (dot(axis, axis) < 1e-6) { axis = vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 0.0); }
  axis = normalize(axis);
  let away = select(vec3f(0.0, 1.0, 0.0), vec3f(1.0, 0.0, 0.0), abs(axis.y) > 0.9);
  let t = normalize(cross(away, axis));
  let b = cross(axis, t);
  let phi = r.x * 6.2831853;
  let cz = mix(1.0, cos(CONE), r.y);
  let sz = sqrt(max(0.0, 1.0 - cz * cz));
  let dir = normalize(axis * cz + (t * cos(phi) + b * sin(phi)) * sz);

  let speed = params.SPEED * mix(1.0 - SPEED_VAR, 1.0 + SPEED_VAR, r2.x * r2.x);
  p.vel = dir * speed + a.vel * INHERIT;

  p.size = params.SIZE * mix(0.7, 1.3, r2.y);
  p.life = params.LIFE * (1.0 - LIFE_VAR * 0.5 + LIFE_VAR * r2.z);
  p.age = 0.0;
  p.stretch = 0.0;
  p.rot = r.x * 6.2831853;
  p.seed = seed + f32(id) * 0.0211;
  return p;
}

fn particleStep(p: Particle, dt: f32) -> Particle {
  var q = p;
  // Drag first, then gravity: the air takes the throw away before the ground
  // takes the height, and what is left is a slow settle. Swaying as it goes —
  // a petal is a sail.
  q.vel = q.vel * exp(-DRAG * dt);
  q.vel.y = q.vel.y - GRAVITY * dt;
  let t = rzTime() * 2.2 + q.seed * 40.0;
  q.pos = q.pos + (q.vel + vec3f(sin(t), 0.0, cos(t * 0.8)) * SWAY) * dt;
  q.rot = q.rot + (fract(q.seed * 91.7) - 0.5) * TUMBLE * dt;
  return q;
}

fn particleShade(p: Particle, uv: vec2f) -> vec4f {
  let aa = max(fwidth(uv.x) * 1.5, 1e-4);
  let q = uv * 2.0 - 1.0;
  let t = clamp(p.age / max(p.life, 1e-3), 0.0, 1.0);
  // Gently in, and gently out: opens over the first fifth, and over the last
  // third both shrinks a little and fades, so it settles out of sight rather
  // than blinking off.
  let scale = smoothstep(0.0, 0.2, t) * (1.0 - smoothstep(0.66, 1.0, t) * 0.4);
  let fade = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.66, 1.0, t);
  let r2 = q / max(scale, 1e-3);

  let idx = p.seed;
  let rr = length(r2);
  let ang = atan2(r2.y, r2.x);
  let n = 5.0 + floor(rzHash11(idx * 88.3) * 3.0);
  let lobe = 0.52 + 0.48 * pow(abs(cos(n * 0.5 * ang)), 0.55);
  let cover = 1.0 - smoothstep(lobe - aa, lobe + aa, rr);
  if (cover <= 0.003) { discard; }
  let eye = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.30 - aa, 0.30 + aa, rr);

  let fl = flowerColor(rzHash11(idx * 51.7), rzHash11(idx * 63.9));
  let flum = dot(fl, vec3f(0.3, 0.6, 0.1));
  let eye_c = vec3f(flum * 0.95 + 0.10, flum * 0.72 + 0.05, flum * 0.12);
  var col = mix(fl, eye_c, eye);
  // A cel edge: the rim a shade deeper than the middle, so a white head on a
  // white shirt still has an outline.
  let rim = smoothstep(0.6, 1.0, rr);
  col *= (1.0 - 0.18 * rim) * GAIN;
  return vec4f(col, cover * fade);
}

